const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Rossillon. A room in the Countess's palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram, the Countess of Rossillon, Helena, and Lafew, all in black.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["In delivering my son from me, I bury a second husband."],"id":2,"m":"In sending my son away from me, I bury a second husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["And I in going, madam, weep o'er my father's death anew; but I must","attend his majesty's command, to whom I am now in ward, evermore in","subjection."],"id":3,"m":"And in going, madam, I weep for my father's death all over again. But I must obey the King's command. I'm his ward now, and always his subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["You shall find of the king a husband, madam; you, sir, a father. He","that so generally is at all times good, must of necessity hold his","virtue to you, whose worthiness would stir it up where it wanted,","rather than lack it where there is such abundance."],"id":4,"m":"You'll find the King a husband to you, madam, and a father to you, sir. He is good to everyone at all times, so he's bound to be good to you. Your own worth would bring out his goodness even if he lacked it, and there's no lack of it in him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["What hope is there of his majesty's amendment?"],"id":5,"m":"What hope is there of the King getting better?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["He hath abandon'd his physicians, madam; under whose practices he hath","persecuted time with hope, and finds no other advantage in the process","but only the losing of hope by time."],"id":6,"m":"He has given up his doctors, madam. Under their treatment he has spent his time hoping, and all he has gained from it is losing hope as time goes on."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["This young gentlewoman had a father—O that \"had!\", how sad a passage","'tis!—whose skill was almost as great as his honesty; had it stretch'd","so far, would have made nature immortal, and death should have play for","lack of work. Would for the king's sake he were living! I think it","would be the death of the king's disease."],"id":7,"m":"This young lady had a father (oh, that \"had\"! What a sad word it is!) whose skill was almost as great as his honesty. If it had stretched that far, it would have made people immortal, and Death would have had nothing to do. I wish for the King's sake he were alive! I think he would have been the death of the King's disease."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["How called you the man you speak of, madam?"],"id":8,"m":"What was the man's name, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["He was famous, sir, in his profession, and it was his great right to be","so: Gerard de Narbon."],"id":9,"m":"He was famous in his profession, sir, and he deserved to be: Gerard de Narbon."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["He was excellent indeed, madam; the king very lately spoke of him","admiringly, and mourningly; he was skilful enough to have liv'd still,","if knowledge could be set up against mortality."],"id":10,"m":"He was excellent indeed, madam. The King spoke of him only recently, with admiration and with grief. He was skilful enough to be living still, if knowledge could hold out against death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["What is it, my good lord, the king languishes of?"],"id":11,"m":"What illness is the King wasting away from, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["A fistula, my lord."],"id":12,"m":"A fistula, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I heard not of it before."],"id":13,"m":"I hadn't heard of it before."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I would it were not notorious. Was this gentlewoman the daughter of","Gerard de Narbon?"],"id":14,"m":"I wish it weren't common knowledge. Was this young lady Gerard de Narbon's daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["His sole child, my lord, and bequeathed to my overlooking. I have those","hopes of her good that her education promises her dispositions she","inherits, which makes fair gifts fairer; for where an unclean mind","carries virtuous qualities, there commendations go with pity, they are","virtues and traitors too. In her they are the better for their","simpleness; she derives her honesty, and achieves her goodness."],"id":15,"m":"His only child, my lord, and left in my care. I expect great good of her, because her upbringing adds to the nature she was born with, and that makes fine gifts finer. When someone with a corrupt mind has good abilities, praise comes mixed with regret: those abilities are virtues and traitors at once. In her they are all the better for being pure. Her honesty she inherits; her goodness she earns."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Your commendations, madam, get from her tears."],"id":16,"m":"Your praise brings tears to her eyes, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["'Tis the best brine a maiden can season her praise in. The remembrance","of her father never approaches her heart but the tyranny of her sorrows","takes all livelihood from her cheek. No more of this, Helena; go to, no","more, lest it be rather thought you affect a sorrow than to have."],"id":17,"m":"Tears are the best salt a young woman can preserve her praise in. Whenever she remembers her father, her grief is so cruel that it drains all the life from her face. No more of this, Helena. Come on, no more, or people will think you are putting on a sorrow rather than really feeling one."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I do affect a sorrow indeed, but I have it too."],"id":18,"m":"I do put on a sorrow, but I really feel one too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Moderate lamentation is the right of the dead; excessive grief the","enemy to the living."],"id":19,"m":"Moderate grief is what the dead deserve. Too much grief is the enemy of the living."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["If the living be enemy to the grief, the excess makes it soon mortal."],"id":20,"m":"If the living fight against grief, too much of it soon kills itself off."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Madam, I desire your holy wishes."],"id":21,"m":"Madam, I ask for your blessing."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["How understand we that?"],"id":22,"m":"What does he mean by that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Be thou blest, Bertram, and succeed thy father","In manners, as in shape! Thy blood and virtue","Contend for empire in thee, and thy goodness","Share with thy birthright! Love all, trust a few,","Do wrong to none. Be able for thine enemy","Rather in power than use; and keep thy friend","Under thy own life's key. Be check'd for silence,","But never tax'd for speech. What heaven more will,","That thee may furnish and my prayers pluck down,","Fall on thy head! Farewell. My lord,","'Tis an unseason'd courtier; good my lord,","Advise him."],"id":23,"m":"Bless you, Bertram, and may you be like your father in behaviour as you are in looks! May your noble blood and your goodness compete to rule you, and may your virtue match your birth! Love everyone, trust only a few, and do wrong to no one. Be strong enough to beat your enemy, but don't use that strength. Guard your friend as closely as your own life. Be told off for keeping quiet, but never blamed for what you say. Whatever else heaven wants to give you, and my prayers can bring down, may it fall on your head! Goodbye. My lord, he's an inexperienced courtier. Please, my lord, advise him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["He cannot want the best","That shall attend his love."],"id":24,"m":"He won't lack the best advice my love can give him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Heaven bless him! Farewell, Bertram."],"id":25,"m":"Heaven bless him! Goodbye, Bertram."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Countess.","id":26},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["The best wishes that can be forg'd in your thoughts be servants to you!","[To Helena] Be comfortable to my mother, your mistress, and make","much of her."],"id":27,"m":"May the best wishes you can think of serve you well! [To Helena] Look after my mother, your mistress, and take good care of her."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Farewell, pretty lady, you must hold the credit of your father."],"id":28,"m":"Goodbye, pretty lady. You must live up to your father's good name."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bertram and Lafew.","id":29},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O, were that all! I think not on my father,","And these great tears grace his remembrance more","Than those I shed for him. What was he like?","I have forgot him; my imagination","Carries no favour in't but Bertram's.","I am undone: there is no living, none,","If Bertram be away. 'Twere all one","That I should love a bright particular star,","And think to wed it, he is so above me.","In his bright radiance and collateral light","Must I be comforted, not in his sphere.","Th'ambition in my love thus plagues itself:","The hind that would be mated by the lion","Must die for love. 'Twas pretty, though a plague,","To see him every hour; to sit and draw","His arched brows, his hawking eye, his curls,","In our heart's table,—heart too capable","Of every line and trick of his sweet favour.","But now he's gone, and my idolatrous fancy","Must sanctify his relics. Who comes here?"],"id":30,"m":"If only that were all! I'm not thinking about my father, and these big tears honour his memory more than the ones I actually shed for him. What did he look like? I've forgotten him. My imagination holds no face but Bertram's. I'm ruined: there's no living, none at all, if Bertram is away. I might as well love one bright star in the sky and think of marrying it, he is so far above me. I must take comfort in his light shining on me from a distance, not in sharing his sphere. My ambitious love torments itself: the deer that wants to mate with the lion must die of love. It was sweet, though it was torture, to see him every hour, to sit and draw his arched eyebrows, his keen hawk-like eyes and his curls on the drawing board of my heart, a heart that took in every line and feature of his lovely face. But now he's gone, and my love, which worships him like an idol, must treat the things he left behind as holy relics. Who's this coming?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Parolles.","id":31},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","cont":true,"lines":["One that goes with him: I love him for his sake,","And yet I know him a notorious liar,","Think him a great way fool, solely a coward;","Yet these fix'd evils sit so fit in him","That they take place when virtue's steely bones","Looks bleak i' th' cold wind: withal, full oft we see","Cold wisdom waiting on superfluous folly."],"id":32,"m":"One of the men going with him. I love him for Bertram's sake, and yet I know he's a notorious liar, I think he's mostly a fool, and he's nothing but a coward. Yet these rooted faults suit him so well that they find a welcome, while virtue, with its hard bones, is left shivering out in the cold wind. Very often we see cold wisdom serving showy foolishness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Save you, fair queen!"],"id":33,"m":"God save you, beautiful queen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["And you, monarch!"],"id":34,"m":"And you, king!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["No."],"id":35,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["And no."],"id":36,"m":"And no."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Are you meditating on virginity?"],"id":37,"m":"Are you thinking about virginity?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Ay. You have some stain of soldier in you; let me ask you a question.","Man is enemy to virginity; how may we barricado it against him?"],"id":38,"m":"Yes. You have a touch of the soldier about you, so let me ask you a question. Man is the enemy of virginity. How can we barricade it against him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Keep him out."],"id":39,"m":"Keep him out."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["But he assails; and our virginity, though valiant, in the defence, yet","is weak. Unfold to us some warlike resistance."],"id":40,"m":"But he attacks, and our virginity, though it is brave, is weak in defending itself. Show us some military way to resist."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["There is none. Man setting down before you will undermine you and blow","you up."],"id":41,"m":"There isn't one. A man who lays siege to you will dig under your walls and blow you up."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Bless our poor virginity from underminers and blowers-up! Is there no","military policy how virgins might blow up men?"],"id":42,"m":"God protect our poor virginity from diggers and blowers-up! Is there no military plan by which virgins might blow men up?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Virginity being blown down, man will quicklier be blown up; marry, in","blowing him down again, with the breach yourselves made, you lose your","city. It is not politic in the commonwealth of nature to preserve","virginity. Loss of virginity is rational increase, and there was never","virgin got till virginity was first lost. That you were made of is","metal to make virgins. Virginity, by being once lost, may be ten times","found; by being ever kept, it is ever lost. 'Tis too cold a companion.","Away with it!"],"id":43,"m":"Once virginity is blown down, a man will quickly be blown up (made proud and swollen). But when you blow him down again, through the breach you've made yourselves, you lose your city. It isn't good policy in nature's state to keep virginity. Losing virginity leads to a sensible increase, and no virgin was ever conceived until virginity was first lost. What you were made of is the stuff for making virgins. Virginity, once lost, can be found ten times over; kept for ever, it is lost for ever. It's too cold a companion. Get rid of it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I will stand for't a little, though therefore I die a virgin."],"id":44,"m":"I'll defend it a little longer, even if that means I die a virgin."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["There's little can be said in't; 'tis against the rule of nature. To","speak on the part of virginity is to accuse your mothers; which is most","infallible disobedience. He that hangs himself is a virgin: virginity","murders itself, and should be buried in highways out of all sanctified","limit, as a desperate offendress against nature. Virginity breeds","mites, much like a cheese; consumes itself to the very paring, and so","dies with feeding his own stomach. Besides, virginity is peevish,","proud, idle, made of self-love, which is the most inhibited sin in the","canon. Keep it not; you cannot choose but lose by't. Out with't! Within","the year it will make itself two, which is a goodly increase, and the","principal itself not much the worse. Away with it!"],"id":45,"m":"There's little to be said for it; it's against the law of nature. To speak up for virginity is to accuse your own mothers, which is outright disobedience. A virgin is like a man who hangs himself: virginity murders itself, and should be buried at a crossroads outside holy ground, as a desperate offender against nature. Virginity breeds mites, just like a cheese; it eats itself away to the rind, and so it dies from feeding on itself. Besides, virginity is bad-tempered, proud, idle and made of self-love, which is the most forbidden sin in the Church's law. Don't keep it; you can only lose by it. Out with it! Within a year it will turn into two, which is a fine profit, and the original investment won't be much the worse. Get rid of it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["How might one do, sir, to lose it to her own liking?"],"id":46,"m":"How might a woman lose it, sir, in a way she would like?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Let me see. Marry, ill, to like him that ne'er it likes. 'Tis a","commodity will lose the gloss with lying; the longer kept, the less","worth. Off with't while 'tis vendible; answer the time of request.","Virginity, like an old courtier, wears her cap out of fashion, richly","suited, but unsuitable, just like the brooch and the toothpick, which","wear not now. Your date is better in your pie and your porridge than in","your cheek. And your virginity, your old virginity, is like one of our","French wither'd pears; it looks ill, it eats drily; marry, 'tis a","wither'd pear; it was formerly better; marry, yet 'tis a wither'd pear.","Will you anything with it?"],"id":47,"m":"Let me see. Well, by doing wrong and liking a man who doesn't like virginity. It's a product that loses its shine the longer it sits on the shelf; the longer it's kept, the less it's worth. Sell it while it's still saleable; meet the demand while it lasts. Virginity is like an old courtier who wears an old-fashioned hat: richly dressed but out of style, like the brooch and the toothpick, which nobody wears any more. A date is better in your pie and your porridge than in your cheek. And your virginity, your old virginity, is like one of our French withered pears: it looks bad and tastes dry. Yes, it's a withered pear. It used to be better, but it's still a withered pear. Do you want to do anything with it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Not my virginity yet.","There shall your master have a thousand loves,","A mother, and a mistress, and a friend,","A phoenix, captain, and an enemy,","A guide, a goddess, and a sovereign,","A counsellor, a traitress, and a dear:","His humble ambition, proud humility,","His jarring concord, and his discord dulcet,","His faith, his sweet disaster; with a world","Of pretty, fond, adoptious christendoms","That blinking Cupid gossips. Now shall he—","I know not what he shall. God send him well!","The court's a learning-place; and he is one."],"id":48,"m":"Not with my virginity yet. At court your master will find a thousand loves: a mother, a mistress and a friend, a phoenix, a captain and an enemy, a guide, a goddess and a ruler, an adviser, a traitor and a darling; his humble ambition and proud humility, his clashing harmony and sweet discord, his faith and his sweet disaster, and a whole world of pretty, foolish pet names that blind Cupid gives out. Now he will... I don't know what he will. God keep him well! The court is a place for learning, and he is one..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["What one, i' faith?"],"id":49,"m":"One what, really?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["That I wish well. 'Tis pity—"],"id":50,"m":"One I wish well. It's a pity..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["What's pity?"],"id":51,"m":"What's a pity?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["That wishing well had not a body in't","Which might be felt, that we, the poorer born,","Whose baser stars do shut us up in wishes,","Might with effects of them follow our friends,","And show what we alone must think, which never","Returns us thanks."],"id":52,"m":"That wishing well has no body that can be felt. Then we who are born poor, whose lowly stars shut us up in mere wishes, might follow our friends with real actions, and show them what we can only think, which never earns us any thanks."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Page.","id":53},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Monsieur Parolles, my lord calls for you."],"id":54,"m":"Monsieur Parolles, my lord is calling for you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Page.","id":55},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Little Helen, farewell. If I can remember thee, I will think of thee at","court."],"id":56,"m":"Goodbye, little Helen. If I can remember you, I'll think of you at court."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Monsieur Parolles, you were born under a charitable star."],"id":57,"m":"Monsieur Parolles, you were born under a kindly star."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Under Mars, I."],"id":58,"m":"Under Mars, I was."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I especially think, under Mars."],"id":59,"m":"I really do think so: under Mars."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Why under Mars?"],"id":60,"m":"Why under Mars?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["The wars hath so kept you under, that you must needs be born under","Mars."],"id":61,"m":"The wars have kept you so far under that you must have been born under Mars."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["When he was predominant."],"id":62,"m":"When Mars was at his strongest."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["When he was retrograde, I think rather."],"id":63,"m":"When he was going backwards, I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Why think you so?"],"id":64,"m":"Why do you think that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["You go so much backward when you fight."],"id":65,"m":"You go backwards so much when you fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["That's for advantage."],"id":66,"m":"That's for tactical advantage."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["So is running away, when fear proposes the safety: but the composition","that your valour and fear makes in you is a virtue of a good wing, and","I like the wear well."],"id":67,"m":"So is running away, when fear says it's safer. But the mixture of courage and fear in you is a quality that gives you good wings, and I like the way it suits you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I am so full of business I cannot answer thee acutely. I will return","perfect courtier; in the which my instruction shall serve to naturalize","thee, so thou wilt be capable of a courtier's counsel, and understand","what advice shall thrust upon thee; else thou diest in thine","unthankfulness, and thine ignorance makes thee away. Farewell. When","thou hast leisure, say thy prayers; when thou hast none, remember thy","friends. Get thee a good husband, and use him as he uses thee. So,","farewell."],"id":68,"m":"I'm so busy I can't give you a sharp answer. I'll come back a perfect courtier, and then my teaching will make you a native of the court, if you're able to take a courtier's advice and understand what's urged on you. Otherwise you'll die ungrateful, and your ignorance will finish you off. Goodbye. When you have time, say your prayers; when you don't, remember your friends. Get yourself a good husband, and treat him as he treats you. So, goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":69},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie,","Which we ascribe to heaven: the fated sky","Gives us free scope; only doth backward pull","Our slow designs when we ourselves are dull.","What power is it which mounts my love so high,","That makes me see, and cannot feed mine eye?","The mightiest space in fortune nature brings","To join like likes, and kiss like native things.","Impossible be strange attempts to those","That weigh their pains in sense, and do suppose","What hath been cannot be. Who ever strove","To show her merit that did miss her love?","The king's disease,—my project may deceive me,","But my intents are fix'd, and will not leave me."],"id":70,"m":"The cures we look to heaven for are often inside ourselves. The stars give us freedom to act; they only hold back our plans when we ourselves are slow. What power is it that lifts my love so high, that lets me see what I want but never have it? Nature can bring together things separated by the greatest distance of fortune, and make them join and kiss as if they were born equals. Strange attempts seem impossible only to those who measure the effort by common sense and assume that what has never happened can't happen. What woman who tried to show her worth ever failed to win her love? The King's disease... my plan may let me down, but my mind is made up, and it won't leave me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":71},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"Paris. A room in the King's palace.","id":72},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish of cornets. Enter the King of France, with letters; Lords and others attending.","id":73},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The Florentines and Senoys are by th' ears;","Have fought with equal fortune, and continue","A braving war."],"id":74,"m":"The Florentines and the Sienese are at each other's throats. They've fought with equal luck, and the war of defiance goes on."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["So 'tis reported, sir."],"id":75,"m":"So it's reported, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Nay, 'tis most credible, we here receive it,","A certainty, vouch'd from our cousin Austria,","With caution, that the Florentine will move us","For speedy aid; wherein our dearest friend","Prejudicates the business, and would seem","To have us make denial."],"id":76,"m":"No, it's entirely believable. We've received it here as a certainty, confirmed by our cousin the Duke of Austria, along with a warning that the Duke of Florence will ask us for quick help. In this our dearest friend has judged the matter in advance and seems to want us to refuse."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["His love and wisdom,","Approv'd so to your majesty, may plead","For amplest credence."],"id":77,"m":"His love and wisdom, so well proven to your majesty, deserve your fullest trust."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["He hath arm'd our answer,","And Florence is denied before he comes:","Yet, for our gentlemen that mean to see","The Tuscan service, freely have they leave","To stand on either part."],"id":78,"m":"He has armed our answer for us: Florence is refused before he even asks. Still, any of our gentlemen who want to see service in Tuscany are free to fight on either side."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["It well may serve","A nursery to our gentry, who are sick","For breathing and exploit."],"id":79,"m":"It may well serve as a training ground for our young noblemen, who are longing for exercise and action."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What's he comes here?"],"id":80,"m":"Who's this coming?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram, Lafew and Parolles.","id":81},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["It is the Count Rossillon, my good lord,","Young Bertram."],"id":82,"m":"It is the Count of Rossillon, my good lord, young Bertram."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Youth, thou bear'st thy father's face;","Frank nature, rather curious than in haste,","Hath well compos'd thee. Thy father's moral parts","Mayst thou inherit too! Welcome to Paris."],"id":83,"m":"Young man, you have your father's face. Generous nature took care rather than hurry when she made you, and made you well. May you inherit your father's good character too! Welcome to Paris."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["My thanks and duty are your majesty's."],"id":84,"m":"My thanks and my duty belong to your majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I would I had that corporal soundness now,","As when thy father and myself in friendship","First tried our soldiership. He did look far","Into the service of the time, and was","Discipled of the bravest. He lasted long,","But on us both did haggish age steal on,","And wore us out of act. It much repairs me","To talk of your good father; in his youth","He had the wit which I can well observe","Today in our young lords; but they may jest","Till their own scorn return to them unnoted","Ere they can hide their levity in honour","So like a courtier, contempt nor bitterness","Were in his pride or sharpness; if they were,","His equal had awak'd them, and his honour,","Clock to itself, knew the true minute when","Exception bid him speak, and at this time","His tongue obey'd his hand. Who were below him","He us'd as creatures of another place,","And bow'd his eminent top to their low ranks,","Making them proud of his humility,","In their poor praise he humbled. Such a man","Might be a copy to these younger times;","Which, followed well, would demonstrate them now","But goers backward."],"id":85,"m":"I wish I were as healthy in body now as I was when your father and I, as friends, first tried our hand at soldiering. He understood the warfare of the time deeply, and he learned from the bravest. He lasted a long time, but ugly old age crept up on us both and wore us out of action. It does me a lot of good to talk about your fine father. In his youth he had the wit I can see today in our young lords, but they can go on joking until their mockery comes back on them unnoticed, before they can cover their lightness with honour. He was such a true courtier that there was no contempt or bitterness in his pride or his sharpness. If there was, it was only because an equal had provoked it. His sense of honour, like a clock, knew the exact minute when an objection called on him to speak, and then his tongue obeyed his hand. People below him he treated as if they came from a higher rank, and bowed his great head to their low position, so that they were proud of his humility while he humbled himself by accepting their poor praise. Such a man could be a model for these younger times. If they followed it well, it would show them that they are now only going backwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["His good remembrance, sir,","Lies richer in your thoughts than on his tomb;","So in approof lives not his epitaph","As in your royal speech."],"id":86,"m":"Your memory of him, sir, is richer than anything on his tomb. His epitaph is not as well proved there as it is in your royal words."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Would I were with him! He would always say,—","Methinks I hear him now; his plausive words","He scatter'd not in ears, but grafted them","To grow there and to bear,—\"Let me not live,\"","This his good melancholy oft began","On the catastrophe and heel of pastime,","When it was out,—\"Let me not live\" quoth he,","\"After my flame lacks oil, to be the snuff","Of younger spirits, whose apprehensive senses","All but new things disdain; whose judgments are","Mere fathers of their garments; whose constancies","Expire before their fashions.\" This he wish'd.","I, after him, do after him wish too,","Since I nor wax nor honey can bring home,","I quickly were dissolved from my hive","To give some labourers room."],"id":87,"m":"I wish I were with him! He always used to say... I think I can hear him now. He didn't scatter his fine words into people's ears; he planted them there to grow and bear fruit. \"Let me not live,\" his gentle sadness often began, at the end of some entertainment, when it was over. \"Let me not live,\" he said, \"after my lamp runs out of oil, to be the snuff-end of younger spirits, whose quick minds scorn anything that isn't new, whose judgements give birth to nothing but new clothes, and whose loyalty dies before their fashions do.\" That's what he wished. And I, after him, wish the same. Since I can bring home neither wax nor honey, I wish I were quickly removed from my hive to make room for some workers."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["You're lov'd, sir;","They that least lend it you shall lack you first."],"id":88,"m":"You're loved, sir. Those who give you least love will be the first to miss you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I fill a place, I know't. How long is't, Count,","Since the physician at your father's died?","He was much fam'd."],"id":89,"m":"I take up space, I know it. How long is it, Count, since your father's doctor died? He was very famous."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Some six months since, my lord."],"id":90,"m":"About six months ago, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["If he were living, I would try him yet;—","Lend me an arm;—the rest have worn me out","With several applications; nature and sickness","Debate it at their leisure. Welcome, Count;","My son's no dearer."],"id":91,"m":"If he were alive, I would still try him. Give me your arm. The others have worn me out with their different treatments; nature and sickness are left to argue it out at their leisure. Welcome, Count. My own son is no dearer to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Thank your majesty."],"id":92,"m":"Thank you, your majesty."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt. Flourish.","id":93},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"Rossillon. A Room in the Palace.","id":94},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Countess, Steward and Clown.","id":95},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["I will now hear. What say you of this gentlewoman?"],"id":96,"m":"Now I'll listen. What do you have to say about this young lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STEWARD","lines":["Madam, the care I have had to even your content, I wish might be found","in the calendar of my past endeavours; for then we wound our modesty,","and make foul the clearness of our deservings, when of ourselves we","publish them."],"id":97,"m":"Madam, I hope you'll find the care I've taken to please you written down in the record of my past service. We hurt our modesty and spoil the honesty of our good deeds when we advertise them ourselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["What does this knave here? Get you gone, sirrah. The complaints I have","heard of you I do not all believe; 'tis my slowness that I do not; for","I know you lack not folly to commit them, and have ability enough to","make such knaveries yours."],"id":98,"m":"What's this rogue doing here? Get out, fellow. I don't believe all the complaints I've heard about you. That's my slowness, because I know you're foolish enough to do those things, and clever enough to carry off such tricks."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["'Tis not unknown to you, madam, I am a poor fellow."],"id":99,"m":"You know very well, madam, that I'm a poor man."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Well, sir."],"id":100,"m":"Well, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["No, madam, 'tis not so well that I am poor, though many of the rich are","damned; but if I may have your ladyship's good will to go to the world,","Isbel the woman and I will do as we may."],"id":101,"m":"No, madam, it's not so well that I'm poor, though plenty of rich people are damned. But if I may have your ladyship's permission to get married, Isbel the maid and I will do the best we can."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Wilt thou needs be a beggar?"],"id":102,"m":"Are you determined to be a beggar?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I do beg your good will in this case."],"id":103,"m":"I do beg your permission in this case."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["In what case?"],"id":104,"m":"In what case?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["In Isbel's case and mine own. Service is no heritage, and I think I","shall never have the blessing of God till I have issue of my body; for","they say barnes are blessings."],"id":105,"m":"In Isbel's case and my own. Being a servant is no inheritance, and I don't think I'll have God's blessing until I have children of my own body, because they say children are blessings."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Tell me thy reason why thou wilt marry."],"id":106,"m":"Tell me why you want to marry."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["My poor body, madam, requires it; I am driven on by the flesh, and he","must needs go that the devil drives."],"id":107,"m":"My poor body needs it, madam. I'm driven on by the flesh, and a man has to go where the devil drives him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Is this all your worship's reason?"],"id":108,"m":"Is that all the reason your worship has?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Faith, madam, I have other holy reasons, such as they are."],"id":109,"m":"Honestly, madam, I have other holy reasons, such as they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["May the world know them?"],"id":110,"m":"May the world hear them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I have been, madam, a wicked creature, as you and all flesh and blood","are; and indeed I do marry that I may repent."],"id":111,"m":"I've been a wicked creature, madam, as you and all flesh and blood are, and in fact I'm marrying so that I can repent."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Thy marriage, sooner than thy wickedness."],"id":112,"m":"You'll repent your marriage sooner than your wickedness."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I am out of friends, madam, and I hope to have friends for my wife's","sake."],"id":113,"m":"I've no friends, madam, and I hope to make friends for my wife's sake."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Such friends are thine enemies, knave."],"id":114,"m":"Friends like that are your enemies, you rogue."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Y'are shallow, madam, in great friends; for the knaves come to do that","for me which I am a-weary of. He that ears my land spares my team, and","gives me leave to in the crop: if I be his cuckold, he's my drudge. He","that comforts my wife is the cherisher of my flesh and blood; he that","cherishes my flesh and blood loves my flesh and blood; he that loves my","flesh and blood is my friend; ergo, he that kisses my wife is my","friend. If men could be contented to be what they are, there were no","fear in marriage; for young Charbon the puritan and old Poysam the","papist, howsome'er their hearts are sever'd in religion, their heads","are both one; they may jowl horns together like any deer i' the herd."],"id":115,"m":"You don't understand much about great friends, madam, because those rogues come to do for me the work I'm tired of. The man who ploughs my land saves my horses and lets me bring in the harvest. If he makes me a cuckold, he's my labourer. The man who comforts my wife looks after my flesh and blood; the man who looks after my flesh and blood loves my flesh and blood; the man who loves my flesh and blood is my friend; therefore the man who kisses my wife is my friend. If men could be happy to be what they are, there'd be nothing to fear in marriage. Young Charbon the Puritan and old Poysam the Catholic, however much their hearts are split by religion, have heads that are just the same: they can knock horns together like any deer in the herd."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Wilt thou ever be a foul-mouth'd and calumnious knave?"],"id":116,"m":"Will you always be a foul-mouthed, slandering rogue?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["A prophet I, madam; and I speak the truth the next way:","~For I the ballad will repeat,","~Which men full true shall find;","~Your marriage comes by destiny,","~Your cuckoo sings by kind."],"id":117,"m":"I'm a prophet, madam, and I speak the truth the quickest way: \"For I the ballad will repeat, which men will find is true: your marriage comes by destiny, the cuckoo sings by nature.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Get you gone, sir; I'll talk with you more anon."],"id":118,"m":"Go away, sir. I'll talk to you more later."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEWARD","lines":["May it please you, madam, that he bid Helen come to you; of her I am to","speak."],"id":119,"m":"If you please, madam, tell him to ask Helen to come to you. It's her I have to speak about."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Sirrah, tell my gentlewoman I would speak with her; Helen I mean."],"id":120,"m":"Fellow, tell my young lady I want to speak to her. Helen, I mean."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["[Sings]","~Was this fair face the cause, quoth she,","~Why the Grecians sacked Troy?","~Fond done, done fond,","~Was this King Priam's joy?","~With that she sighed as she stood,","~With that she sighed as she stood,","~And gave this sentence then:","~Among nine bad if one be good,","~Among nine bad if one be good,","~There's yet one good in ten."],"id":121,"m":"[Sings] \"Was this lovely face the reason,\" she said, \"why the Greeks destroyed Troy? Foolishly done, done foolishly; was this King Priam's joy?\" With that she sighed as she stood, and then gave this verdict: \"Among nine bad, if one is good, there's still one good in ten.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["What, one good in ten? You corrupt the song, sirrah."],"id":122,"m":"What, one good in ten? You're spoiling the song, fellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["One good woman in ten, madam, which is a purifying o' the song. Would","God would serve the world so all the year! We'd find no fault with the","tithe-woman, if I were the parson. One in ten, quoth 'a! And we might","have a good woman born but or every blazing star, or at an earthquake,","'twould mend the lottery well; a man may draw his heart out ere he","pluck one."],"id":123,"m":"One good woman in ten, madam: that's improving the song. I wish God would treat the world like that all year! We'd find no fault with the tenth woman if I were the parson. One in ten, he says! If only a good woman were born at every comet or every earthquake, it would improve the lottery a lot. As it is, a man could pull his heart out before he picked one."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["You'll be gone, sir knave, and do as I command you!"],"id":124,"m":"Will you go, sir rogue, and do as I tell you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["That man should be at woman's command, and yet no hurt done! Though","honesty be no puritan, yet it will do no hurt; it will wear the","surplice of humility over the black gown of a big heart. I am going,","forsooth; the business is for Helen to come hither."],"id":125,"m":"To think that a man should take orders from a woman and no harm be done! Though honesty is no Puritan, it will do no harm: it will wear the white robe of humility over the black gown of a proud heart. I'm going, indeed. The business is for Helen to come here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":126},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Well, now."],"id":127,"m":"Well, now."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEWARD","lines":["I know, madam, you love your gentlewoman entirely."],"id":128,"m":"I know, madam, that you love your young lady completely."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Faith I do. Her father bequeath'd her to me, and she herself, without","other advantage, may lawfully make title to as much love as she finds;","there is more owing her than is paid, and more shall be paid her than","she'll demand."],"id":129,"m":"I certainly do. Her father left her to me, and she herself, with no other advantage, deserves all the love she gets. She is owed more than she's paid, and she'll be paid more than she asks for."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEWARD","lines":["Madam, I was very late more near her than I think she wish'd me; alone","she was, and did communicate to herself her own words to her own ears;","she thought, I dare vow for her, they touch'd not any stranger sense.","Her matter was, she loved your son. Fortune, she said, was no goddess,","that had put such difference betwixt their two estates; Love no god,","that would not extend his might only where qualities were level; Diana","no queen of virgins, that would suffer her poor knight surpris'd,","without rescue in the first assault or ransom afterward. This she","deliver'd in the most bitter touch of sorrow that e'er I heard virgin","exclaim in, which I held my duty speedily to acquaint you withal;","sithence, in the loss that may happen, it concerns you something to","know it."],"id":130,"m":"Madam, I was much nearer her just now than I think she wanted. She was alone, and speaking her own words to her own ears. She thought, I'd swear, that no one else could hear. What she said was that she loved your son. Fortune, she said, was no goddess, to have put such a difference between their two ranks; Love was no god, if he would only use his power where people were equal; Diana was no queen of virgins, if she let her poor follower be captured in the first attack with no rescue, or ransom afterwards. She said this in the most bitter sorrow I ever heard a young woman speak in. I thought it my duty to tell you quickly, since, in case anything bad comes of it, it is your concern to know."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["You have discharg'd this honestly; keep it to yourself; many","likelihoods inform'd me of this before, which hung so tottering in the","balance that I could neither believe nor misdoubt. Pray you leave me;","stall this in your bosom; and I thank you for your honest care. I will","speak with you further anon."],"id":131,"m":"You've done this honestly. Keep it to yourself. Many signs told me this before, but they hung so unsteadily in the balance that I could neither believe nor doubt it. Please leave me. Lock this away in your heart, and thank you for your honest care. I'll talk to you more later."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Steward.","id":132},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Helena.","id":133},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Even so it was with me when I was young;","If ever we are nature's, these are ours; this thorn","Doth to our rose of youth rightly belong;","Our blood to us, this to our blood is born;","It is the show and seal of nature's truth,","Where love's strong passion is impress'd in youth.","By our remembrances of days foregone,","Such were our faults, or then we thought them none.","Her eye is sick on't; I observe her now."],"id":134,"m":"It was just like this with me when I was young. If we are nature's creatures at all, these feelings belong to us. This thorn rightly belongs to the rose of youth; our passions are born with us, and this is born with our passions. It is the mark and seal of nature's truth when love's strong passion is stamped on someone young. When we remember days gone by, we see these were our faults, or else we thought them none. Her eyes are sick with it; I can see it in her now."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["What is your pleasure, madam?"],"id":135,"m":"What do you want, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["You know, Helen,","I am a mother to you."],"id":136,"m":"You know, Helen, I am a mother to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Mine honourable mistress."],"id":137,"m":"My honoured mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Nay, a mother.","Why not a mother? When I said a mother,","Methought you saw a serpent. What's in mother,","That you start at it? I say I am your mother,","And put you in the catalogue of those","That were enwombed mine. 'Tis often seen","Adoption strives with nature, and choice breeds","A native slip to us from foreign seeds.","You ne'er oppress'd me with a mother's groan,","Yet I express to you a mother's care.","God's mercy, maiden! does it curd thy blood","To say I am thy mother? What's the matter,","That this distempered messenger of wet,","The many-colour'd Iris, rounds thine eye?","—Why, that you are my daughter?"],"id":138,"m":"No, a mother. Why not a mother? When I said \"mother\", I thought you looked as if you'd seen a snake. What is there in \"mother\" that makes you start? I say I am your mother, and I put you on the list of those born from my womb. We often see adoption compete with nature, and choice grow a branch of our own from someone else's seed. You never made me groan in childbirth, yet I show you a mother's care. God's mercy, girl! Does it curdle your blood to hear me say I'm your mother? What's the matter, that this upset messenger of rain, the many-coloured rainbow, rings your eye? Why, because you're my daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["That I am not."],"id":139,"m":"That's what I'm not."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["I say, I am your mother."],"id":140,"m":"I say, I am your mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Pardon, madam;","The Count Rossillon cannot be my brother.","I am from humble, he from honoured name;","No note upon my parents, his all noble,","My master, my dear lord he is; and I","His servant live, and will his vassal die.","He must not be my brother."],"id":141,"m":"Forgive me, madam. The Count of Rossillon can't be my brother. I come from a humble family, he from an honoured one. My parents are unknown; his are all noble. He's my master, my dear lord, and I live as his servant and will die as his subject. He must not be my brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Nor I your mother?"],"id":142,"m":"And I can't be your mother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["You are my mother, madam; would you were—","So that my lord your son were not my brother,—","Indeed my mother! or were you both our mothers,","I care no more for than I do for heaven,","So I were not his sister. Can't no other,","But, I your daughter, he must be my brother?"],"id":143,"m":"You are my mother, madam. I wish you really were my mother, as long as my lord your son were not my brother! Or if you were mother to us both, I'd want that as much as I want heaven, as long as I weren't his sister. Is there no other way? If I'm your daughter, must he be my brother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Yes, Helen, you might be my daughter-in-law.","God shield you mean it not! daughter and mother","So strive upon your pulse. What! pale again?","My fear hath catch'd your fondness; now I see","The mystery of your loneliness, and find","Your salt tears' head. Now to all sense 'tis gross","You love my son; invention is asham'd,","Against the proclamation of thy passion","To say thou dost not. Therefore tell me true;","But tell me then, 'tis so; for, look, thy cheeks","Confess it, t'one to th'other; and thine eyes","See it so grossly shown in thy behaviours,","That in their kind they speak it; only sin","And hellish obstinacy tie thy tongue,","That truth should be suspected. Speak, is't so?","If it be so, you have wound a goodly clew;","If it be not, forswear't: howe'er, I charge thee,","As heaven shall work in me for thine avail,","To tell me truly."],"id":144,"m":"Yes, Helen, you could be my daughter-in-law. God forbid that's what you mean! \"Daughter\" and \"mother\" make your pulse race so. What, pale again? My fear has caught your love. Now I see the secret of your loneliness, and find the source of your salty tears. Now it's plain to anyone that you love my son. Excuses are ashamed to deny it against what your passion proclaims. So tell me the truth, and tell me it is so, because look, your cheeks confess it to each other, and your eyes see it so plainly in your behaviour that they speak it in their own way. Only sin and wicked stubbornness tie your tongue, so that the truth is doubted. Speak: is it so? If it is, you've wound yourself a fine tangle. If it isn't, swear it isn't. Either way, I order you, as heaven will help me to help you, to tell me truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Good madam, pardon me."],"id":145,"m":"Good madam, forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Do you love my son?"],"id":146,"m":"Do you love my son?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Your pardon, noble mistress."],"id":147,"m":"Your pardon, noble mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Love you my son?"],"id":148,"m":"Do you love my son?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Do not you love him, madam?"],"id":149,"m":"Don't you love him, madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Go not about; my love hath in't a bond","Whereof the world takes note. Come, come, disclose","The state of your affection, for your passions","Have to the full appeach'd."],"id":150,"m":"Don't go round the point. My love for him has a bond that the whole world knows about. Come, come, reveal what you feel, because your passions have fully given you away."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Then I confess,","Here on my knee, before high heaven and you,","That before you, and next unto high heaven,","I love your son.","My friends were poor, but honest; so's my love.","Be not offended; for it hurts not him","That he is lov'd of me; I follow him not","By any token of presumptuous suit,","Nor would I have him till I do deserve him;","Yet never know how that desert should be.","I know I love in vain, strive against hope;","Yet in this captious and inteemable sieve","I still pour in the waters of my love","And lack not to lose still. Thus, Indian-like,","Religious in mine error, I adore","The sun that looks upon his worshipper,","But knows of him no more. My dearest madam,","Let not your hate encounter with my love,","For loving where you do; but if yourself,","Whose aged honour cites a virtuous youth,","Did ever, in so true a flame of liking,","Wish chastely, and love dearly, that your Dian","Was both herself and love; O then, give pity","To her whose state is such that cannot choose","But lend and give where she is sure to lose;","That seeks not to find that her search implies,","But riddle-like, lives sweetly where she dies!"],"id":151,"m":"Then I confess, here on my knees, before high heaven and you, that more than you, and next only to high heaven, I love your son. My family were poor but honest, and so is my love. Don't be offended, because it does him no harm that I love him. I don't pursue him with any sign of presumptuous wooing, nor would I have him until I deserve him, and yet I don't know how I ever could. I know I love in vain and struggle against hope. Yet into this deceiving sieve that holds nothing I keep pouring the waters of my love, and never run short of more to lose. So, like a sun-worshipper in India, devoted in my mistake, I adore the sun, which looks down on its worshipper but knows nothing more of him. My dearest madam, don't let your hate meet my love because I love the one you love. If you yourself, whose honourable old age points to a virtuous youth, ever in so true a flame of love wished chastely and loved dearly, so that your Diana was both chaste and loving, then pity a woman whose situation means she can only lend and give where she's sure to lose; who doesn't try to find what she's searching for, but, like a riddle, lives sweetly where she dies!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Had you not lately an intent,—speak truly,—","To go to Paris?"],"id":152,"m":"Didn't you mean, not long ago (tell the truth), to go to Paris?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Madam, I had."],"id":153,"m":"Madam, I did."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Wherefore? tell true."],"id":154,"m":"Why? Tell the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I will tell truth; by grace itself I swear.","You know my father left me some prescriptions","Of rare and prov'd effects, such as his reading","And manifest experience had collected","For general sovereignty; and that he will'd me","In heedfull'st reservation to bestow them,","As notes whose faculties inclusive were","More than they were in note. Amongst the rest","There is a remedy, approv'd, set down,","To cure the desperate languishings whereof","The king is render'd lost."],"id":155,"m":"I'll tell the truth; I swear by grace itself. You know my father left me some prescriptions with rare and proven effects, which his reading and plain experience had collected as cures for everything, and that he told me to keep them with the greatest care, as notes whose powers were greater than people knew. Among them is a remedy, tested and written down, to cure the hopeless wasting illness that the King is said to be lost to."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["This was your motive","For Paris, was it? Speak."],"id":156,"m":"Was this your reason for going to Paris? Speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["My lord your son made me to think of this;","Else Paris, and the medicine, and the king,","Had from the conversation of my thoughts","Haply been absent then."],"id":157,"m":"My lord your son made me think of it. Otherwise Paris and the medicine and the King might never have crossed my mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["But think you, Helen,","If you should tender your supposed aid,","He would receive it? He and his physicians","Are of a mind; he, that they cannot help him;","They, that they cannot help. How shall they credit","A poor unlearned virgin, when the schools,","Embowell'd of their doctrine, have let off","The danger to itself?"],"id":158,"m":"But do you think, Helen, if you offered your supposed help, he would accept it? He and his doctors agree: he, that they can't help him; they, that they can't help. How will they believe a poor, uneducated young woman, when the medical schools have emptied out all their learning and left the illness to run its course?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["There's something in't","More than my father's skill, which was the great'st","Of his profession, that his good receipt","Shall for my legacy be sanctified","By th' luckiest stars in heaven; and would your honour","But give me leave to try success, I'd venture","The well-lost life of mine on his grace's cure.","By such a day, an hour."],"id":159,"m":"There's something in it beyond my father's skill, though that was the greatest in his profession: I feel his good prescription will be blessed by the luckiest stars in heaven as my inheritance. If your honour would only let me try, I'd risk my life, which would be well lost, on curing his majesty by such and such a day and hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Dost thou believe't?"],"id":160,"m":"Do you believe it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Ay, madam, knowingly."],"id":161,"m":"Yes, madam, I know it."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Why, Helen, thou shalt have my leave and love,","Means and attendants, and my loving greetings","To those of mine in court. I'll stay at home,","And pray God's blessing into thy attempt.","Be gone tomorrow; and be sure of this,","What I can help thee to, thou shalt not miss."],"id":162,"m":"Why, Helen, you shall have my permission and my love, money and servants, and my loving greetings to my friends at court. I'll stay at home and pray for God's blessing on your attempt. Leave tomorrow, and be sure of this: whatever I can help you with, you won't go without."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":163},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Paris. A room in the King's palace.","id":164},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter the King with young Lords taking leave for the Florentine war; Bertram, Parolles and Attendants.","id":165},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Farewell, young lords; these warlike principles","Do not throw from you; and you, my lords, farewell;","Share the advice betwixt you; if both gain all,","The gift doth stretch itself as 'tis receiv'd,","And is enough for both."],"id":166,"m":"Goodbye, young lords. Don't throw away these principles of war. And you, my lords, goodbye too. Share the advice between you. If each of you takes all of it, the gift stretches as it's received, and there's enough for both."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["'Tis our hope, sir,","After well-ent'red soldiers, to return","And find your grace in health."],"id":167,"m":"We hope, sir, after we've seasoned ourselves as soldiers, to come back and find your grace in good health."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["No, no, it cannot be; and yet my heart","Will not confess he owes the malady","That doth my life besiege. Farewell, young lords.","Whether I live or die, be you the sons","Of worthy Frenchmen; let higher Italy,—","Those bated that inherit but the fall","Of the last monarchy—see that you come","Not to woo honour, but to wed it, when","The bravest questant shrinks: find what you seek,","That fame may cry you loud. I say farewell."],"id":168,"m":"No, no, that can't be. And yet my heart won't admit it suffers from the illness that is besieging my life. Goodbye, young lords. Whether I live or die, show yourselves the sons of worthy Frenchmen. Let northern Italy (except those who have inherited only the fall of the last empire) see that you come not to court honour but to marry it. Where the bravest seeker shrinks back, find what you are looking for, so that fame shouts your name aloud. I say goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Health, at your bidding serve your majesty!"],"id":169,"m":"May health obey your command and serve your majesty!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Those girls of Italy, take heed of them;","They say our French lack language to deny","If they demand; beware of being captives","Before you serve."],"id":170,"m":"Those Italian girls: watch out for them. They say we French have no words to say no if they ask. Be careful not to be taken prisoner before you've even fought."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH","lines":["Our hearts receive your warnings."],"id":171,"m":"Our hearts take in your warnings."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Farewell.—Come hither to me."],"id":172,"m":"Goodbye. Come here to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"The King retires to a couch.","id":173},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["O my sweet lord, that you will stay behind us!"],"id":174,"m":"Oh, my dear lord, to think you're staying behind!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["'Tis not his fault; the spark."],"id":175,"m":"It's not his fault, the young spark."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["O, 'tis brave wars!"],"id":176,"m":"Oh, it's a glorious war!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Most admirable! I have seen those wars."],"id":177,"m":"Wonderful! I have seen those wars."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I am commanded here, and kept a coil with,","\"Too young\", and \"the next year\" and \"'tis too early\"."],"id":178,"m":"I'm ordered to stay here, and pestered with \"too young\" and \"next year\" and \"it's too early\"."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["An thy mind stand to't, boy, steal away bravely."],"id":179,"m":"If your heart is set on it, boy, slip away bravely."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I shall stay here the forehorse to a smock,","Creaking my shoes on the plain masonry,","Till honour be bought up, and no sword worn","But one to dance with. By heaven, I'll steal away."],"id":180,"m":"I'll stay here leading a woman's skirt like the front horse of a team, squeaking my shoes on the smooth floors, until all the honour is used up and nobody wears a sword except to dance with. By heaven, I'll slip away."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["There's honour in the theft."],"id":181,"m":"There's honour in that theft."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Commit it, count."],"id":182,"m":"Do it, Count."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["I am your accessary; and so farewell."],"id":183,"m":"I'm your accomplice. And so, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I grow to you, and our parting is a tortur'd body."],"id":184,"m":"I've grown into you, and parting from you is like tearing a body apart."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Farewell, captain."],"id":185,"m":"Goodbye, captain."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Sweet Monsieur Parolles!"],"id":186,"m":"Sweet Monsieur Parolles!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Noble heroes, my sword and yours are kin. Good sparks and lustrous, a","word, good metals. You shall find in the regiment of the Spinii one","Captain Spurio, with his cicatrice, an emblem of war, here on his","sinister cheek; it was this very sword entrench'd it. Say to him I","live; and observe his reports for me."],"id":187,"m":"Noble heroes, my sword and yours are related. Good, shining sparks, a word, my fine metals. In the Spinii regiment you'll find a Captain Spurio, with a scar, a badge of war, here on his left cheek. It was this very sword that carved it. Tell him I'm alive, and note what he says about me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["We shall, noble captain."],"id":188,"m":"We will, noble captain."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Mars dote on you for his novices!"],"id":189,"m":"May Mars love you as his apprentices!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lords.","id":190},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","cont":true,"lines":["What will ye do?"],"id":191,"m":"What will you do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Stay the king."],"id":192,"m":"Wait on the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Use a more spacious ceremony to the noble lords; you have restrain'd","yourself within the list of too cold an adieu. Be more expressive to","them; for they wear themselves in the cap of the time; there do muster","true gait; eat, speak, and move, under the influence of the most","receiv'd star; and though the devil lead the measure, such are to be","followed. After them, and take a more dilated farewell."],"id":193,"m":"Give the noble lords a more generous goodbye. You kept yourself within the limits of too cold a farewell. Be more open with them, because they're the jewel in the cap of fashion. They show off the right way to walk, they eat, speak and move under the most fashionable star, and even if the devil leads the dance, men like that should be followed. Go after them, and say a fuller goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["And I will do so."],"id":194,"m":"And so I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Worthy fellows, and like to prove most sinewy sword-men."],"id":195,"m":"Fine fellows, and likely to turn out very strong swordsmen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bertram and Parolles.","id":196},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lafew.","id":197},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Pardon, my lord [Kneeling], for me and for my tidings."],"id":198,"m":"Forgive me, my lord, [Kneeling] for myself and for my news."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I'll fee thee to stand up."],"id":199,"m":"I'll pay you to stand up."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Then here's a man stands that has brought his pardon.","I would you had kneel'd, my lord, to ask me mercy,","And that at my bidding you could so stand up."],"id":200,"m":"Then here's a man who has bought his pardon standing. I wish, my lord, that you had knelt to ask me for mercy, and that you could stand up when I told you to."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I would I had; so I had broke thy pate,","And ask'd thee mercy for't."],"id":201,"m":"I wish I had: then I'd have broken your head and asked you to forgive me for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Good faith, across;","But, my good lord, 'tis thus: will you be cur'd","Of your infirmity?"],"id":202,"m":"Well, that's a miss. But, my good lord, it's like this: do you want to be cured of your illness?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["No."],"id":203,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["O, will you eat","No grapes, my royal fox? Yes, but you will","My noble grapes, and if my royal fox","Could reach them. I have seen a medicine","That's able to breathe life into a stone,","Quicken a rock, and make you dance canary","With sprightly fire and motion; whose simple touch","Is powerful to araise King Pippen, nay,","To give great Charlemain a pen in's hand","And write to her a love-line."],"id":204,"m":"Oh, won't you eat any grapes, my royal fox? Yes, you would eat my noble grapes, if my royal fox could reach them. I have seen a medicine that can breathe life into a stone, bring a rock to life, and make you dance a lively canary dance with fiery energy. Its mere touch has the power to raise King Pepin from the dead, or even to put a pen in great Charlemagne's hand and have him write her a love poem."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What 'her' is this?"],"id":205,"m":"What \"her\" is this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Why, doctor 'she'! My lord, there's one arriv'd,","If you will see her. Now, by my faith and honour,","If seriously I may convey my thoughts","In this my light deliverance, I have spoke","With one that in her sex, her years, profession,","Wisdom, and constancy, hath amaz'd me more","Than I dare blame my weakness. Will you see her,","For that is her demand, and know her business?","That done, laugh well at me."],"id":206,"m":"Why, a lady doctor! My lord, someone has arrived, if you'll see her. Now, on my faith and honour, if I can say something serious in this light way of talking, I have spoken with a woman who, for her sex, her age, her profession, her wisdom and her steadiness, has amazed me more than I can blame on my own weakness. Will you see her (that's what she asks) and hear her business? Once that's done, laugh at me all you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Now, good Lafew,","Bring in the admiration; that we with thee","May spend our wonder too, or take off thine","By wond'ring how thou took'st it."],"id":207,"m":"Now, good Lafew, bring in this wonder, so that we can share your amazement, or cure you of yours by wondering how you caught it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Nay, I'll fit you,","And not be all day neither."],"id":208,"m":"No, I'll prove it to you, and not take all day about it either."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lafew.","id":209},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thus he his special nothing ever prologues."],"id":210,"m":"He always makes a grand introduction to his special nothings."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lafew with Helena.","id":211},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Nay, come your ways."],"id":212,"m":"Come along, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["This haste hath wings indeed."],"id":213,"m":"This haste really does have wings."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Nay, come your ways.","This is his majesty, say your mind to him.","A traitor you do look like, but such traitors","His majesty seldom fears; I am Cressid's uncle,","That dare leave two together. Fare you well."],"id":214,"m":"Come along. This is his majesty: tell him what you have in mind. You look like a traitor, but his majesty rarely fears traitors like you. I'm like Cressida's uncle, who dared to leave two people alone together. Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":215},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Now, fair one, does your business follow us?"],"id":216,"m":"Now, beautiful lady, does your business concern us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Ay, my good lord.","Gerard de Narbon was my father,","In what he did profess, well found."],"id":217,"m":"Yes, my good lord. Gerard de Narbon was my father, and he was highly skilled in his profession."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I knew him."],"id":218,"m":"I knew him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["The rather will I spare my praises towards him.","Knowing him is enough. On his bed of death","Many receipts he gave me; chiefly one,","Which, as the dearest issue of his practice,","And of his old experience the only darling,","He bade me store up as a triple eye,","Safer than mine own two; more dear I have so,","And hearing your high majesty is touch'd","With that malignant cause, wherein the honour","Of my dear father's gift stands chief in power,","I come to tender it, and my appliance,","With all bound humbleness."],"id":219,"m":"Then I'll spare my praise of him; knowing him is enough. On his deathbed he gave me many prescriptions, and one above all, the dearest result of his practice and the only darling of his long experience. He told me to treasure it like a third eye, more safely than my own two. I have done so. And hearing that your majesty is afflicted with the very illness in which my dear father's gift is most powerful, I've come to offer it, and my treatment, in all due humility."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["We thank you, maiden,","But may not be so credulous of cure,","When our most learned doctors leave us, and","The congregated college have concluded","That labouring art can never ransom nature","From her inaidable estate. I say we must not","So stain our judgment, or corrupt our hope,","To prostitute our past-cure malady","To empirics, or to dissever so","Our great self and our credit, to esteem","A senseless help, when help past sense we deem."],"id":220,"m":"We thank you, young lady, but we can't be so ready to believe in a cure, when our most learned doctors have given up on us, and the whole college of physicians has concluded that their hard-working skill can never rescue nature from her hopeless state. I say we must not so stain our judgement, or spoil our hope, as to hand over our incurable illness to quacks, or so split our royal self from our reputation as to value a senseless remedy when we think we're past all sensible help."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["My duty then shall pay me for my pains.","I will no more enforce mine office on you,","Humbly entreating from your royal thoughts","A modest one to bear me back again."],"id":221,"m":"Then my sense of duty will be my reward for my trouble. I won't force my services on you any more. I humbly ask your royal mind for one modest thought to take back home with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I cannot give thee less, to be call'd grateful.","Thou thought'st to help me; and such thanks I give","As one near death to those that wish him live.","But what at full I know, thou know'st no part;","I knowing all my peril, thou no art."],"id":222,"m":"I can't give you less if I want to be called grateful. You meant to help me, and I give you the kind of thanks a dying man gives to those who wish him to live. But what I fully know, you don't know at all: I know all my danger, and you know no cure for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["What I can do can do no hurt to try,","Since you set up your rest 'gainst remedy.","He that of greatest works is finisher","Oft does them by the weakest minister.","So holy writ in babes hath judgment shown,","When judges have been babes. Great floods have flown","From simple sources, and great seas have dried","When miracles have by the great'st been denied.","Oft expectation fails, and most oft there","Where most it promises; and oft it hits","Where hope is coldest, and despair most fits."],"id":223,"m":"There's no harm in trying what I can do, since you've staked everything against any remedy. God, who finishes the greatest works, often does them through the weakest servant. So the Bible shows wisdom in babies when judges have behaved like babies. Great floods have flowed from small springs, and great seas have dried up when the greatest men refused to believe in miracles. Expectation often fails, and most often where it promises most; and it often succeeds where hope is coldest and despair seems most fitting."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I must not hear thee. Fare thee well, kind maid.","Thy pains, not us'd, must by thyself be paid;","Proffers, not took, reap thanks for their reward."],"id":224,"m":"I mustn't listen to you. Goodbye, kind girl. Since your efforts aren't used, you must pay yourself for them. Offers that are not accepted earn thanks as their reward."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Inspired merit so by breath is barr'd.","It is not so with Him that all things knows","As 'tis with us that square our guess by shows;","But most it is presumption in us when","The help of heaven we count the act of men.","Dear sir, to my endeavours give consent;","Of heaven, not me, make an experiment.","I am not an impostor, that proclaim","Myself against the level of mine aim,","But know I think, and think I know most sure,","My art is not past power nor you past cure."],"id":225,"m":"So inspired worth is shut out by a word. It isn't like this with God, who knows everything, as it is with us, who judge by appearances. But it is the greatest presumption in us when we count heaven's help as the work of human beings. Dear sir, agree to my attempt. Make a trial of heaven, not of me. I'm not a fraud who claims more than I can hit. Know that I think, and think I know for certain, that my skill is not powerless and you are not past cure."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Art thou so confident? Within what space","Hop'st thou my cure?"],"id":226,"m":"Are you so confident? How soon do you hope to cure me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["The greatest grace lending grace.","Ere twice the horses of the sun shall bring","Their fiery torcher his diurnal ring,","Ere twice in murk and occidental damp","Moist Hesperus hath quench'd her sleepy lamp;","Or four and twenty times the pilot's glass","Hath told the thievish minutes how they pass;","What is infirm from your sound parts shall fly,","Health shall live free, and sickness freely die."],"id":227,"m":"With God's grace helping, before the horses of the sun have twice carried their fiery torch round its daily circle; before the evening star has twice put out her sleepy lamp in the dark western mist; or before the sailor's hourglass has counted the stealing minutes twenty-four times, whatever is sick will leave your healthy parts. Health will live free, and sickness will die."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Upon thy certainty and confidence","What dar'st thou venture?"],"id":228,"m":"If you're so sure and confident, what do you dare to risk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Tax of impudence,","A strumpet's boldness, a divulged shame,","Traduc'd by odious ballads; my maiden's name","Sear'd otherwise; nay worse of worst extended","With vilest torture, let my life be ended."],"id":229,"m":"Being charged with shamelessness, with a whore's boldness; a public disgrace, mocked in vile ballads; my good name as a virgin branded; or worse than the worst, let my life be ended with the cruellest torture."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Methinks in thee some blessed spirit doth speak","His powerful sound within an organ weak;","And what impossibility would slay","In common sense, sense saves another way.","Thy life is dear, for all that life can rate","Worth name of life in thee hath estimate:","Youth, beauty, wisdom, courage, all","That happiness and prime can happy call.","Thou this to hazard needs must intimate","Skill infinite, or monstrous desperate.","Sweet practiser, thy physic I will try,","That ministers thine own death if I die."],"id":230,"m":"I think some blessed spirit speaks its powerful voice through you, a weak instrument. What common sense would rule impossible, a higher sense saves another way. Your life is precious, because everything that makes life worth the name is valued in you: youth, beauty, wisdom, courage, everything that happiness and the prime of life can call happy. To risk all this, you must have either infinite skill or monstrous desperation. Sweet doctor, I will try your medicine, which will bring about your own death if I die."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["If I break time, or flinch in property","Of what I spoke, unpitied let me die,","And well deserv'd. Not helping, death's my fee;","But if I help, what do you promise me?"],"id":231,"m":"If I miss the time, or fail in any detail of what I've said, let me die unpitied, and deservedly. If I don't help, death is my payment. But if I do help, what do you promise me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Make thy demand."],"id":232,"m":"Make your request."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["But will you make it even?"],"id":233,"m":"But will you grant it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Ay, by my sceptre and my hopes of heaven."],"id":234,"m":"Yes, by my sceptre and my hopes of heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Then shalt thou give me, with thy kingly hand","What husband in thy power I will command:","Exempted be from me the arrogance","To choose from forth the royal blood of France,","My low and humble name to propagate","With any branch or image of thy state;","But such a one, thy vassal, whom I know","Is free for me to ask, thee to bestow."],"id":235,"m":"Then you will give me, with your royal hand, whatever husband in your power I ask for. I won't be so arrogant as to choose from the royal blood of France, to join my low and humble name with any branch or image of your royal line; but one of your subjects whom I know I'm free to ask for and you're free to give."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Here is my hand; the premises observ'd,","Thy will by my performance shall be serv'd;","So make the choice of thy own time, for I,","Thy resolv'd patient, on thee still rely.","More should I question thee, and more I must,","Though more to know could not be more to trust:","From whence thou cam'st, how tended on; but rest","Unquestion'd welcome, and undoubted bless'd.","Give me some help here, ho! If thou proceed","As high as word, my deed shall match thy deed."],"id":236,"m":"Here is my hand. If you keep to your terms, I'll keep my promise and do your will. So choose your own time, because I, your determined patient, rely on you completely. I ought to question you more, and I must, though knowing more could not make me trust you more: where you come from, who came with you. But stay unquestioned and welcome, and blessed beyond doubt. Help me here, someone! If you do as much as you've said, my actions will match yours."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt.","id":237},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Rossillon. A room in the Countess's palace.","id":238},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Countess and Clown.","id":239},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Come on, sir; I shall now put you to the height of your breeding."],"id":240,"m":"Come on, sir. Now I'll put your upbringing to the highest test."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I will show myself highly fed and lowly taught. I know my business is","but to the court."],"id":241,"m":"I'll show myself well fed and badly taught. I know my business is only to go to court."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["To the court! Why, what place make you special, when you put off that","with such contempt? But to the court!"],"id":242,"m":"Only to court! Why, what place do you think so special, when you dismiss the court with such contempt? \"Only to court\"!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Truly, madam, if God have lent a man any manners, he may easily put it","off at court: he that cannot make a leg, put off's cap, kiss his hand,","and say nothing, has neither leg, hands, lip, nor cap; and indeed such","a fellow, to say precisely, were not for the court; but for me, I have","an answer will serve all men."],"id":243,"m":"Honestly, madam, if God has lent a man any manners, he can easily show them off at court. Anyone who can't bow, take off his cap, kiss his hand and say nothing has no leg, no hands, no lips and no cap, and to be precise, such a fellow isn't fit for court. But as for me, I have an answer that will do for everyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Marry, that's a bountiful answer that fits all questions."],"id":244,"m":"Well, that's a generous answer if it fits every question."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["It is like a barber's chair, that fits all buttocks—the pin-buttock,","the quatch-buttock, the brawn-buttock, or any buttock."],"id":245,"m":"It's like a barber's chair, which fits every backside: the thin backside, the squat backside, the fat backside, any backside."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Will your answer serve fit to all questions?"],"id":246,"m":"Will your answer really fit every question?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["As fit as ten groats is for the hand of an attorney, as your French","crown for your taffety punk, as Tib's rush for Tom's forefinger, as a","pancake for Shrove-Tuesday, a morris for May-day, as the nail to his","hole, the cuckold to his horn, as a scolding quean to a wrangling","knave, as the nun's lip to the friar's mouth; nay, as the pudding to","his skin."],"id":247,"m":"As well as ten groats fit a lawyer's hand, a French crown fits a dressed-up whore, Tib's rush ring fits Tom's finger, a pancake fits Shrove Tuesday, a morris dance fits May Day, a nail fits its hole, a cuckold fits his horns, a nagging woman fits a quarrelling rogue, the nun's lip fits the friar's mouth; yes, as the sausage fits its skin."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Have you, I say, an answer of such fitness for all questions?"],"id":248,"m":"I ask you again, do you have an answer that fits all questions so well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["From below your duke to beneath your constable, it will fit any","question."],"id":249,"m":"From your duke down to below your constable, it'll fit any question."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["It must be an answer of most monstrous size that must fit all demands."],"id":250,"m":"It must be an answer of enormous size if it fits every question."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["But a trifle neither, in good faith, if the learned should speak truth","of it. Here it is, and all that belongs to't. Ask me if I am a","courtier; it shall do you no harm to learn."],"id":251,"m":"It's only a trifle, honestly, if the learned told the truth about it. Here it is, and everything that goes with it. Ask me if I'm a courtier. It won't do you any harm to learn."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["To be young again, if we could: I will be a fool in question, hoping to","be the wiser by your answer. I pray you, sir, are you a courtier?"],"id":252,"m":"To be young again, if only we could! I'll play the fool by asking, hoping to be wiser for your answer. Tell me, sir, are you a courtier?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["O Lord, sir! There's a simple putting off. More, more, a hundred of","them."],"id":253,"m":"\"O Lord, sir!\" There's an easy way to dodge a question. More, more, a hundred of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Sir, I am a poor friend of yours, that loves you."],"id":254,"m":"Sir, I'm a poor friend of yours who loves you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["O Lord, sir! Thick, thick; spare not me."],"id":255,"m":"\"O Lord, sir!\" Quick, quick, don't spare me."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["I think, sir, you can eat none of this homely meat."],"id":256,"m":"I don't suppose, sir, you can eat any of this plain food."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["O Lord, sir! Nay, put me to't, I warrant you."],"id":257,"m":"\"O Lord, sir!\" Go on, test me, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["You were lately whipp'd, sir, as I think."],"id":258,"m":"I think you were whipped recently, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["O Lord, sir! Spare not me."],"id":259,"m":"\"O Lord, sir!\" Don't spare me."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Do you cry 'O Lord, sir!' at your whipping, and 'spare not me'? Indeed","your 'O Lord, sir!' is very sequent to your whipping. You would answer","very well to a whipping, if you were but bound to't."],"id":260,"m":"Do you cry \"O Lord, sir!\" and \"Don't spare me\" when you're whipped? Indeed your \"O Lord, sir!\" follows very naturally from a whipping. You'd make a very good answer to a whipping, if only you were tied up for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I ne'er had worse luck in my life in my 'O Lord, sir!' I see things may","serve long, but not serve ever."],"id":261,"m":"I've never had worse luck in my life with my \"O Lord, sir!\" I see things can be useful for a long time, but not for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["I play the noble housewife with the time, to entertain it so merrily","with a fool."],"id":262,"m":"I'm being a fine housewife with my time, spending it so merrily with a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["O Lord, sir! Why, there't serves well again."],"id":263,"m":"\"O Lord, sir!\" Why, there it works again."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["An end, sir! To your business. Give Helen this,","And urge her to a present answer back.","Commend me to my kinsmen and my son.","This is not much."],"id":264,"m":"Enough, sir! Get on with your business. Give Helen this, and urge her to send an answer back at once. Give my regards to my relatives and my son. This isn't much."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Not much commendation to them?"],"id":265,"m":"Not much of a greeting to them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Not much employment for you. You understand me?"],"id":266,"m":"Not much work for you. Do you understand me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Most fruitfully. I am there before my legs."],"id":267,"m":"Very well. I'll be there before my legs."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Haste you again."],"id":268,"m":"Hurry back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt severally.","id":269},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"Paris. The King's palace.","id":270},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram, Lafew and Parolles.","id":271},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["They say miracles are past; and we have our philosophical persons to","make modern and familiar things supernatural and causeless. Hence is it","that we make trifles of terrors, ensconcing ourselves into seeming","knowledge when we should submit ourselves to an unknown fear."],"id":272,"m":"They say the age of miracles is over, and we have our philosophers who make supernatural things seem ordinary and explainable. That's why we make light of terrible things, hiding behind a show of knowledge when we should give in to a fear of the unknown."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Why, 'tis the rarest argument of wonder that hath shot out in our","latter times."],"id":273,"m":"Why, it's the most extraordinary wonder to appear in recent times."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["And so 'tis."],"id":274,"m":"And so it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["To be relinquish'd of the artists,—"],"id":275,"m":"To be given up by the experts..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["So I say; both of Galen and Paracelsus."],"id":276,"m":"That's what I say: by followers of both Galen and Paracelsus."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Of all the learned and authentic fellows,—"],"id":277,"m":"By all the learned and qualified men..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Right; so I say."],"id":278,"m":"Right, that's what I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["That gave him out incurable,—"],"id":279,"m":"Who declared him incurable..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Why, there 'tis; so say I too."],"id":280,"m":"Why, there it is. That's what I say too."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Not to be helped."],"id":281,"m":"Beyond help."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Right; as 'twere a man assur'd of a—"],"id":282,"m":"Right, like a man certain of a..."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Uncertain life and sure death."],"id":283,"m":"An uncertain life and a sure death."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Just; you say well. So would I have said."],"id":284,"m":"Exactly, well said. That's what I would have said."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I may truly say, it is a novelty to the world."],"id":285,"m":"I can truly say it's something new to the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["It is indeed; if you will have it in showing, you shall read it in what","do you call there?"],"id":286,"m":"It is indeed. If you want it set out, you can read it in that, what do you call it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["A showing of a heavenly effect in an earthly actor."],"id":287,"m":"\"A demonstration of heavenly power in an earthly agent.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["That's it; I would have said the very same."],"id":288,"m":"That's it. I would have said exactly the same."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Why, your dolphin is not lustier; fore me, I speak in respect—"],"id":289,"m":"Why, a young dolphin isn't livelier than the King. Upon my soul, I say it with respect..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Nay, 'tis strange, 'tis very strange; that is the brief and the tedious","of it; and he's of a most facinerious spirit that will not acknowledge","it to be the—"],"id":290,"m":"No, it's strange, very strange, that's the long and short of it; and anyone who won't admit it is the... must have a very wicked spirit..."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Very hand of heaven."],"id":291,"m":"Very hand of heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Ay, so I say."],"id":292,"m":"Yes, that's what I say."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["In a most weak—"],"id":293,"m":"In a very weak..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["And debile minister, great power, great transcendence, which should","indeed give us a further use to be made than alone the recov'ry of the","king, as to be—"],"id":294,"m":"And feeble agent, great power, great transcendence, which should indeed be put to further use than just the King's recovery, so as to be..."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Generally thankful."],"id":295,"m":"Thankful to everyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I would have said it; you say well. Here comes the king."],"id":296,"m":"I would have said that; you put it well. Here comes the King."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter King, Helena and Attendants.","id":297},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Lustique, as the Dutchman says. I'll like a maid the better, whilst I","have a tooth in my head. Why, he's able to lead her a coranto."],"id":298,"m":"Lusty, as the Dutchman says! I'll like girls all the better while I have a tooth left in my head. Why, he's fit to lead her in a lively dance."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Mor du vinager! is not this Helen?"],"id":299,"m":"God's death and vinegar! Isn't that Helen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Fore God, I think so."],"id":300,"m":"By God, I think so."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Go, call before me all the lords in court."],"id":301,"m":"Go and call all the lords at court to come before me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit an Attendant.","id":302},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":true,"lines":["Sit, my preserver, by thy patient's side,","And with this healthful hand, whose banish'd sense","Thou has repeal'd, a second time receive","The confirmation of my promis'd gift,","Which but attends thy naming."],"id":303,"m":"Sit beside your patient, my saviour. With this healthy hand, whose lost feeling you have brought back, receive a second time the confirmation of the gift I promised you, which is only waiting for you to name it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter several Lords.","id":304},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":true,"lines":["Fair maid, send forth thine eye. This youthful parcel","Of noble bachelors stand at my bestowing,","O'er whom both sovereign power and father's voice","I have to use. Thy frank election make;","Thou hast power to choose, and they none to forsake."],"id":305,"m":"Beautiful girl, look around. This group of young noble bachelors is mine to give away, and over them I have both a king's power and a father's say. Make your choice freely: you have the power to choose, and they have none to refuse."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["To each of you one fair and virtuous mistress","Fall, when love please! Marry, to each but one!"],"id":306,"m":"May each of you find a beautiful and virtuous wife, whenever Love pleases! Well, one each, at least!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I'd give bay curtal and his furniture","My mouth no more were broken than these boys',","And writ as little beard."],"id":307,"m":"I'd give my bay horse with the docked tail, and all his harness, if my teeth were as unbroken as these boys' and I had as little beard."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Peruse them well.","Not one of those but had a noble father."],"id":308,"m":"Look them over well. Every one of them had a noble father."},{"t":"sd","text":"She addresses her to a Lord.","id":309},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Gentlemen,","Heaven hath through me restor'd the king to health."],"id":310,"m":"Gentlemen, heaven has restored the King to health through me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["We understand it, and thank heaven for you."],"id":311,"m":"We know it, and thank heaven for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I am a simple maid, and therein wealthiest","That I protest I simply am a maid.","Please it, your majesty, I have done already.","The blushes in my cheeks thus whisper me:","\"We blush that thou shouldst choose; but, be refused,","Let the white death sit on thy cheek for ever,","We'll ne'er come there again.\""],"id":312,"m":"I'm a simple girl, and my greatest wealth is that I can say I'm simply a virgin. If it please your majesty, I've finished already. The blushes in my cheeks whisper to me: \"We blush that you should choose; but if you're refused, let pale death sit on your cheek for ever. We'll never come there again.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Make choice; and, see,","Who shuns thy love shuns all his love in me."],"id":313,"m":"Make your choice. And mark this: anyone who rejects your love rejects all my love for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Now, Dian, from thy altar do I fly,","And to imperial Love, that god most high,","Do my sighs stream. [To first Lord] Sir, will you hear my suit?"],"id":314,"m":"Now, Diana, I run from your altar, and to imperial Love, that highest god, my sighs flow. [To first Lord] Sir, will you hear my request?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["And grant it."],"id":315,"m":"And grant it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Thanks, sir; all the rest is mute."],"id":316,"m":"Thank you, sir. All the rest is silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I had rather be in this choice than throw ames-ace for my life."],"id":317,"m":"I'd rather be in this lottery than throw a double one at dice with my life at stake."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["[To second Lord] The honour, sir, that flames in your fair eyes,","Before I speak, too threat'ningly replies.","Love make your fortunes twenty times above","Her that so wishes, and her humble love!"],"id":318,"m":"[To second Lord] The pride, sir, that burns in your fine eyes answers me too threateningly before I even speak. May Love make your fortune twenty times higher than she who wishes it, and her humble love!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["No better, if you please."],"id":319,"m":"No better than that, if you please."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["My wish receive,","Which great Love grant; and so I take my leave."],"id":320,"m":"Take my wish, which may great Love grant; and so I take my leave."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Do all they deny her? An they were sons of mine I'd have them whipp'd;","or I would send them to th' Turk to make eunuchs of."],"id":321,"m":"Are they all refusing her? If they were my sons, I'd have them whipped, or send them to the Turks to be made eunuchs."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["[To third Lord] Be not afraid that I your hand should take;","I'll never do you wrong for your own sake.","Blessing upon your vows, and in your bed","Find fairer fortune, if you ever wed!"],"id":322,"m":"[To third Lord] Don't be afraid I'll take your hand. I'd never wrong you, for your own sake. Bless your vows, and may you find better fortune in your bed, if you ever marry!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["These boys are boys of ice, they'll none have her. Sure, they are","bastards to the English; the French ne'er got 'em."],"id":323,"m":"These boys are boys of ice; none of them will have her. They must be English bastards; no Frenchman ever fathered them."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["[To fourth Lord] You are too young, too happy, and too good,","To make yourself a son out of my blood."],"id":324,"m":"[To fourth Lord] You're too young, too lucky and too good to make yourself a son out of my blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH LORD","lines":["Fair one, I think not so."],"id":325,"m":"Beautiful lady, I don't think so."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["There's one grape yet. I am sure thy father drank wine. But if thou","beest not an ass, I am a youth of fourteen; I have known thee already."],"id":326,"m":"There's one good grape left. I'm sure your father drank wine. But if you're not an ass, I'm a fourteen-year-old boy. I've seen through you already."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["[To Bertram] I dare not say I take you, but I give","Me and my service, ever whilst I live,","Into your guiding power. This is the man."],"id":327,"m":"[To Bertram] I don't dare say I take you, but I give myself and my service, for as long as I live, into your guiding power. This is the man."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Why, then, young Bertram, take her; she's thy wife."],"id":328,"m":"Why, then, young Bertram, take her. She's your wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["My wife, my liege! I shall beseech your highness,","In such a business give me leave to use","The help of mine own eyes."],"id":329,"m":"My wife, my lord! I beg your highness to let me use my own eyes in a matter like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Know'st thou not, Bertram,","What she has done for me?"],"id":330,"m":"Don't you know, Bertram, what she has done for me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Yes, my good lord,","But never hope to know why I should marry her."],"id":331,"m":"Yes, my good lord, but I never expect to know why I should marry her."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thou know'st she has rais'd me from my sickly bed."],"id":332,"m":"You know she has raised me from my sickbed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["But follows it, my lord, to bring me down","Must answer for your raising? I know her well;","She had her breeding at my father's charge:","A poor physician's daughter my wife! Disdain","Rather corrupt me ever!"],"id":333,"m":"But does it follow, my lord, that I must be brought down to pay for your raising? I know her well: she was brought up at my father's expense. A poor doctor's daughter my wife! I'd rather contempt ruined me for ever!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["'Tis only title thou disdain'st in her, the which","I can build up. Strange is it that our bloods,","Of colour, weight, and heat, pour'd all together,","Would quite confound distinction, yet stands off","In differences so mighty. If she be","All that is virtuous, save what thou dislik'st,","A poor physician's daughter,—thou dislik'st—","Of virtue for the name. But do not so.","From lowest place when virtuous things proceed,","The place is dignified by the doer's deed.","Where great additions swell's, and virtue none,","It is a dropsied honour. Good alone","Is good without a name; vileness is so:","The property by what it is should go,","Not by the title. She is young, wise, fair;","In these to nature she's immediate heir;","And these breed honour: that is honour's scorn","Which challenges itself as honour's born,","And is not like the sire. Honours thrive","When rather from our acts we them derive","Than our fore-goers. The mere word's a slave,","Debauch'd on every tomb, on every grave","A lying trophy, and as oft is dumb","Where dust and damn'd oblivion is the tomb","Of honour'd bones indeed. What should be said?","If thou canst like this creature as a maid,","I can create the rest. Virtue and she","Is her own dower; honour and wealth from me."],"id":334,"m":"It's only her rank you despise in her, and that I can build up. It's strange that our bloods, which if you poured them all together would be impossible to tell apart in colour, weight and heat, should stand apart with such great differences. If she is everything virtuous, except for what you dislike (a poor doctor's daughter), you dislike virtue because of a name. But don't do that. When virtuous deeds come from the lowest place, the place is honoured by the doer's deed. Where great titles puff someone up and there's no virtue, it is a swollen, diseased honour. Good is good on its own, without a name; so is wickedness. A thing should be judged by what it is, not by what it is called. She is young, wise and beautiful; she inherits these directly from nature, and these produce honour. The honour that claims to be honour's child but is not like its father deserves scorn. Honours flourish when we earn them by our own deeds rather than from our ancestors. The mere word is a slave, abused on every tomb, a lying trophy on every grave, and is just as often silent where dust and cursed forgetting are the only tomb of truly honourable bones. What more is there to say? If you can like this creature as a young woman, I can create the rest. Her virtue and herself are her own dowry; honour and wealth will come from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I cannot love her, nor will strive to do 't."],"id":335,"m":"I can't love her, and I won't try to."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thou wrong'st thyself, if thou shouldst strive to choose."],"id":336,"m":"You wrong yourself if you try to choose for yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["That you are well restor'd, my lord, I am glad.","Let the rest go."],"id":337,"m":"I'm glad, my lord, that you are well again. Let the rest go."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["My honour's at the stake, which to defeat,","I must produce my power. Here, take her hand,","Proud scornful boy, unworthy this good gift,","That dost in vile misprision shackle up","My love and her desert; that canst not dream","We, poising us in her defective scale,","Shall weigh thee to the beam; that wilt not know","It is in us to plant thine honour where","We please to have it grow. Check thy contempt;","Obey our will, which travails in thy good;","Believe not thy disdain, but presently","Do thine own fortunes that obedient right","Which both thy duty owes and our power claims;","Or I will throw thee from my care for ever","Into the staggers and the careless lapse","Of youth and ignorance; both my revenge and hate","Loosing upon thee in the name of justice,","Without all terms of pity. Speak! Thine answer!"],"id":338,"m":"My honour is at stake, and to defend it I must use my power. Here, take her hand, you proud, scornful boy, unworthy of this good gift, who with your vile contempt chain up my love and her worth; who can't imagine that I, adding my weight to her lighter side of the scales, can outweigh you completely; who won't recognise that I have the power to plant your honour wherever I please. Hold back your contempt. Obey my will, which works for your good. Don't trust your disdain, but at once give your own fortunes the obedience that your duty owes and my power demands, or I'll throw you out of my care for ever into the staggering and reckless fall of youth and ignorance, letting loose both my revenge and my hatred on you in the name of justice, without any pity. Speak! Your answer!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Pardon, my gracious lord; for I submit","My fancy to your eyes. When I consider","What great creation, and what dole of honour","Flies where you bid it, I find that she, which late","Was in my nobler thoughts most base, is now","The praised of the king; who, so ennobled,","Is as 'twere born so."],"id":339,"m":"Forgive me, my gracious lord, for I submit my likings to your eyes. When I consider what great promotions and what shares of honour go wherever you command, I find that she, who until now was the lowest in my noble thoughts, is now the one the King praises; and, so ennobled, she is as good as born noble."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Take her by the hand,","And tell her she is thine; to whom I promise","A counterpoise; if not to thy estate,","A balance more replete."],"id":340,"m":"Take her by the hand and tell her she is yours. And I promise her a fortune to match yours, or, if not to match it, one that outweighs it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I take her hand."],"id":341,"m":"I take her hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Good fortune and the favour of the king","Smile upon this contract; whose ceremony","Shall seem expedient on the now-born brief,","And be perform'd tonight. The solemn feast","Shall more attend upon the coming space,","Expecting absent friends. As thou lov'st her,","Thy love's to me religious; else, does err."],"id":342,"m":"May good fortune and the King's favour smile on this contract. Its ceremony shall follow quickly on this new agreement and be performed tonight. The formal wedding feast will wait a little longer, for absent friends to arrive. If you love her, your love to me is sacred; otherwise it is wrong."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt King, Bertram, Helena, Lords, and Attendants.","id":343},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Do you hear, monsieur? A word with you."],"id":344,"m":"Listen, monsieur. A word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Your pleasure, sir."],"id":345,"m":"As you wish, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Your lord and master did well to make his recantation."],"id":346,"m":"Your lord and master did well to take back what he said."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Recantation! My lord! My master!"],"id":347,"m":"Take it back! My lord! My master!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Ay. Is it not a language I speak?"],"id":348,"m":"Yes. Am I not speaking a language?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["A most harsh one, and not to be understood without bloody succeeding.","My master!"],"id":349,"m":"A very harsh one, and not to be understood without bloodshed to follow. My master!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Are you companion to the Count Rossillon?"],"id":350,"m":"Are you the Count of Rossillon's companion?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["To any count; to all counts; to what is man."],"id":351,"m":"To any count; to all counts; to any man."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["To what is count's man: count's master is of another style."],"id":352,"m":"To any count's man: a count's master is called something else."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["You are too old, sir; let it satisfy you, you are too old."],"id":353,"m":"You're too old, sir. Let that be enough for you: you're too old."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I must tell thee, sirrah, I write man; to which title age cannot bring","thee."],"id":354,"m":"I must tell you, fellow, I count myself a man, a title that age can't bring you to."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["What I dare too well do, I dare not do."],"id":355,"m":"What I dare do only too well, I don't dare do."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I did think thee, for two ordinaries, to be a pretty wise fellow; thou","didst make tolerable vent of thy travel; it might pass. Yet the scarfs","and the bannerets about thee did manifoldly dissuade me from believing","thee a vessel of too great a burden. I have now found thee; when I lose","thee again I care not. Yet art thou good for nothing but taking up, and","that thou art scarce worth."],"id":356,"m":"For two dinners, I thought you were a reasonably wise fellow. You gave a tolerable account of your travels; it might pass. Yet the scarves and the ribbons all over you strongly put me off believing you were a ship with much cargo. Now I've found you out, and when I lose you again I won't care. You're good for nothing except being picked up, and you're hardly worth that."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Hadst thou not the privilege of antiquity upon thee—"],"id":357,"m":"If you didn't have the privilege of old age..."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Do not plunge thyself too far in anger, lest thou hasten thy trial;","which if—Lord have mercy on thee for a hen! So, my good window of","lattice, fare thee well; thy casement I need not open, for I look","through thee. Give me thy hand."],"id":358,"m":"Don't throw yourself too far into anger, or you'll bring on your own trial, and if... Lord have mercy on you for a chicken! So, my good lattice window, goodbye. I don't need to open your shutters; I can see straight through you. Give me your hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["My lord, you give me most egregious indignity."],"id":359,"m":"My lord, you insult me most outrageously."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Ay, with all my heart; and thou art worthy of it."],"id":360,"m":"Yes, with all my heart, and you deserve it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I have not, my lord, deserv'd it."],"id":361,"m":"I haven't deserved it, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Yes, good faith, every dram of it; and I will not bate thee a scruple."],"id":362,"m":"Yes, I swear, every drop of it, and I won't let you off a grain."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Well, I shall be wiser."],"id":363,"m":"Well, I'll be wiser."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Ev'n as soon as thou canst, for thou hast to pull at a smack o' th'","contrary. If ever thou beest bound in thy scarf and beaten, thou shalt","find what it is to be proud of thy bondage. I have a desire to hold my","acquaintance with thee, or rather my knowledge, that I may say in the","default, \"He is a man I know.\""],"id":364,"m":"The sooner the better, because you've a lot of the opposite to drink up first. If you're ever tied up in your scarf and beaten, you'll find out what it means to be proud of your chains. I want to keep up my acquaintance with you, or rather my knowledge of you, so that when you fail I can say, \"He is a man I know.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["My lord, you do me most insupportable vexation."],"id":365,"m":"My lord, you annoy me unbearably."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I would it were hell-pains for thy sake, and my poor doing eternal; for","doing I am past, as I will by thee, in what motion age will give me","leave."],"id":366,"m":"I wish it were the pains of hell for your sake, and my poor efforts went on for ever. But I'm past doing, as I'll be past you, as fast as old age will let me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":367},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Well, thou hast a son shall take this disgrace off me; scurvy, old,","filthy, scurvy lord! Well, I must be patient; there is no fettering of","authority. I'll beat him, by my life, if I can meet him with any","convenience, an he were double and double a lord. I'll have no more","pity of his age than I would have of—I'll beat him, and if I could but","meet him again."],"id":368,"m":"Well, you have a son who will wipe this disgrace off me, you scabby, old, filthy, scabby lord! Well, I must be patient. There's no chaining up authority. I'll beat him, I swear on my life, if I can meet him at a good moment, even if he were twice as much a lord as he is. I'll have no more pity for his age than I would for... I'll beat him, if I could only meet him again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lafew.","id":369},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Sirrah, your lord and master's married; there's news for you; you have","a new mistress."],"id":370,"m":"Fellow, your lord and master is married. There's news for you: you have a new mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I most unfeignedly beseech your lordship to make some reservation of","your wrongs. He is my good lord; whom I serve above is my master."],"id":371,"m":"I most sincerely beg your lordship to hold back your insults. He is my good lord; the one I serve above is my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Who? God?"],"id":372,"m":"Who? God?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Ay, sir."],"id":373,"m":"Yes, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["The devil it is that's thy master. Why dost thou garter up thy arms o'","this fashion? Dost make hose of thy sleeves? Do other servants so? Thou","wert best set thy lower part where thy nose stands. By mine honour, if","I were but two hours younger, I'd beat thee. Methink'st thou art a","general offence, and every man should beat thee. I think thou wast","created for men to breathe themselves upon thee."],"id":374,"m":"The devil is your master. Why do you tie up your arms in this fashion? Are you making stockings of your sleeves? Do other servants do that? You'd better turn yourself upside down and put your backside where your nose is. On my honour, if I were just two hours younger, I'd beat you. You seem to me an offence to everyone, and every man should beat you. I think you were created for men to get their exercise on."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["This is hard and undeserved measure, my lord."],"id":375,"m":"This is harsh and undeserved treatment, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Go to, sir; you were beaten in Italy for picking a kernel out of a","pomegranate; you are a vagabond, and no true traveller. You are more","saucy with lords and honourable personages than the commission of your","birth and virtue gives you heraldry. You are not worth another word,","else I'd call you knave. I leave you."],"id":376,"m":"Come off it, sir. You were beaten in Italy for stealing a seed out of a pomegranate. You're a tramp and no real traveller. You're cheekier with lords and honourable people than your birth and worth give you any right to be. You're not worth another word, or I'd call you a rogue. I'm leaving you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":377},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram.","id":378},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Good, very good, it is so then. Good, very good; let it be conceal'd","awhile."],"id":379,"m":"Good, very good, so that's how it is. Good, very good. Let it be kept quiet for a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Undone, and forfeited to cares for ever!"],"id":380,"m":"Ruined, and handed over to misery for ever!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["What's the matter, sweetheart?"],"id":381,"m":"What's the matter, sweetheart?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Although before the solemn priest I have sworn,","I will not bed her."],"id":382,"m":"Although I've sworn it in front of the solemn priest, I will not sleep with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["What, what, sweetheart?"],"id":383,"m":"What, what, sweetheart?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["O my Parolles, they have married me!","I'll to the Tuscan wars, and never bed her."],"id":384,"m":"Oh, my Parolles, they've married me! I'll go to the Tuscan wars and never sleep with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["France is a dog-hole, and it no more merits","The tread of a man's foot: to the wars!"],"id":385,"m":"France is a kennel, and it doesn't deserve a man's footstep any more. To the wars!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["There's letters from my mother; what th' import is","I know not yet."],"id":386,"m":"There are letters from my mother. I don't know yet what they say."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Ay, that would be known. To th' wars, my boy, to th' wars!","He wears his honour in a box unseen","That hugs his kicky-wicky here at home,","Spending his manly marrow in her arms,","Which should sustain the bound and high curvet","Of Mars's fiery steed. To other regions!","France is a stable; we that dwell in't, jades,","Therefore, to th' war!"],"id":387,"m":"Yes, that should be found out. To the wars, my boy, to the wars! A man who cuddles his little darling here at home keeps his honour hidden in a box, spending his manly strength in her arms, strength that should control the leaps and high bounds of Mars's fiery horse. To other countries! France is a stable, and we who live in it are worn-out nags. So, to the war!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["It shall be so; I'll send her to my house,","Acquaint my mother with my hate to her,","And wherefore I am fled; write to the king","That which I durst not speak. His present gift","Shall furnish me to those Italian fields","Where noble fellows strike. War is no strife","To the dark house and the detested wife."],"id":388,"m":"So be it. I'll send her to my house, tell my mother that I hate her and why I've run away, and write to the King what I didn't dare say to his face. His gift to me just now will equip me for those Italian battlefields where noble fellows fight. War is no trouble compared with a gloomy home and a hated wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Will this caprichio hold in thee, art sure?"],"id":389,"m":"Will this whim of yours last? Are you sure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Go with me to my chamber and advise me.","I'll send her straight away. Tomorrow","I'll to the wars, she to her single sorrow."],"id":390,"m":"Come with me to my room and advise me. I'll send her away at once. Tomorrow I go to the wars, and she to her lonely sorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Why, these balls bound; there's noise in it. 'Tis hard:","A young man married is a man that's marr'd.","Therefore away, and leave her bravely; go.","The king has done you wrong; but hush 'tis so."],"id":391,"m":"Why, these balls are bouncing; there's some life in this. It's hard: a young man married is a man ruined. So, away, and leave her bravely. Go. The King has done you wrong, but hush, that's how it is."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":392},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"Paris. The King's palace.","id":393},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Helena and Clown.","id":394},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["My mother greets me kindly: is she well?"],"id":395,"m":"My mother sends me kind greetings. Is she well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["She is not well, but yet she has her health; she's very merry, but yet","she is not well. But thanks be given, she's very well, and wants","nothing i' the world; but yet she is not well."],"id":396,"m":"She's not well, and yet she has her health. She's very cheerful, and yet she's not well. But thank God, she's very well and wants for nothing in the world. And yet she's not well."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["If she be very well, what does she ail that she's not very well?"],"id":397,"m":"If she's very well, what's wrong with her that she's not very well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Truly, she's very well indeed, but for two things."],"id":398,"m":"Honestly, she's very well indeed, except for two things."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["What two things?"],"id":399,"m":"What two things?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["One, that she's not in heaven, whither God send her quickly! The other,","that she's in earth, from whence God send her quickly!"],"id":400,"m":"One, that she's not in heaven, where God send her quickly! The other, that she's on earth, from where God send her quickly!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Parolles.","id":401},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Bless you, my fortunate lady!"],"id":402,"m":"Bless you, my lucky lady!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I hope, sir, I have your good will to have mine own good fortune."],"id":403,"m":"I hope, sir, I have your good wishes to keep my own good luck."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["You had my prayers to lead them on; and to keep them on, have them","still. O, my knave how does my old lady?"],"id":404,"m":"You had my prayers to bring it on, and you have them still to keep it going. Oh, my rogue, how is my old lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["So that you had her wrinkles and I her money, I would she did as you","say."],"id":405,"m":"If you had her wrinkles and I had her money, I'd wish she were as you say."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Why, I say nothing."],"id":406,"m":"Why, I say nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Marry, you are the wiser man; for many a man's tongue shakes out his","master's undoing. To say nothing, to do nothing, to know nothing, and","to have nothing, is to be a great part of your title; which is within a","very little of nothing."],"id":407,"m":"Well, you're the wiser man, because many a man's tongue shakes out his master's ruin. To say nothing, do nothing, know nothing and have nothing makes up a great part of your title, which comes very close to nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Away! Thou art a knave."],"id":408,"m":"Get away! You're a rogue."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["You should have said, sir, before a knave thou art a knave; that is","before me thou art a knave. This had been truth, sir."],"id":409,"m":"You should have said, sir, \"Before a rogue you are a rogue\", that is, \"Before me you are a rogue.\" That would have been the truth, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Go to, thou art a witty fool; I have found thee."],"id":410,"m":"Come on, you're a witty fool. I've found you out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Did you find me in yourself, sir? or were you taught to find me? The","search, sir, was profitable; and much fool may you find in you, even to","the world's pleasure and the increase of laughter."],"id":411,"m":"Did you find me in yourself, sir, or were you taught where to find me? The search was worth it, sir, and may you find plenty of fool in yourself, to the world's delight and the increase of laughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["A good knave, i' faith, and well fed.","Madam, my lord will go away tonight;","A very serious business calls on him.","The great prerogative and right of love,","Which, as your due, time claims, he does acknowledge;","But puts it off to a compell'd restraint;","Whose want, and whose delay, is strew'd with sweets;","Which they distil now in the curbed time,","To make the coming hour o'erflow with joy","And pleasure drown the brim."],"id":412,"m":"A good rogue, honestly, and well fed. Madam, my lord is going away tonight. Very serious business calls him away. He acknowledges the great privilege and right of love, which time now claims as your due, but he is forced to postpone it. Its absence and its delay are sweetened with pleasures, which are being distilled during this time of restraint, to make the hour to come overflow with joy and fill pleasure to the brim."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["What's his will else?"],"id":413,"m":"What else does he want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["That you will take your instant leave o' the king,","And make this haste as your own good proceeding,","Strengthen'd with what apology you think","May make it probable need."],"id":414,"m":"That you take leave of the King at once, and present this hurry as your own idea, backed with whatever excuse you think will make it seem necessary."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["What more commands he?"],"id":415,"m":"What else does he order?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["That, having this obtain'd, you presently","Attend his further pleasure."],"id":416,"m":"That, once you've done this, you come to him at once for his further instructions."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["In everything I wait upon his will."],"id":417,"m":"I obey his will in everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I shall report it so."],"id":418,"m":"I'll report that."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I pray you. Come, sirrah."],"id":419,"m":"Please do. Come, fellow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":420},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"V","loc":"Another room in the same.","id":421},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lafew and Bertram.","id":422},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["But I hope your lordship thinks not him a soldier."],"id":423,"m":"But I hope your lordship doesn't think he's a soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Yes, my lord, and of very valiant approof."],"id":424,"m":"Yes, my lord, and of proven courage."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["You have it from his own deliverance."],"id":425,"m":"You have that from his own account."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["And by other warranted testimony."],"id":426,"m":"And from other reliable witnesses."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Then my dial goes not true; I took this lark for a bunting."],"id":427,"m":"Then my clock is wrong. I took this lark for a common bunting."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I do assure you, my lord, he is very great in knowledge, and","accordingly valiant."],"id":428,"m":"I assure you, my lord, he knows a great deal and is brave to match."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I have, then, sinned against his experience and transgressed against","his valour; and my state that way is dangerous, since I cannot yet find","in my heart to repent. Here he comes; I pray you make us friends; I","will pursue the amity."],"id":429,"m":"Then I've sinned against his experience and offended against his courage, and my soul is in danger there, since I can't yet find it in my heart to repent. Here he comes. Please make us friends; I'll work at the friendship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Parolles.","id":430},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["[To Bertram] These things shall be done, sir."],"id":431,"m":"[To Bertram] These things will be done, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Pray you, sir, who's his tailor?"],"id":432,"m":"Tell me, sir, who's his tailor?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Sir!"],"id":433,"m":"Sir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["O, I know him well, I, sir; he, sir, is a good workman, a very good","tailor."],"id":434,"m":"Oh, I know him well, I do, sir. He's a good workman, sir, a very good tailor."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["[Aside to Parolles] Is she gone to the king?"],"id":435,"m":"[Aside to Parolles] Has she gone to the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["She is."],"id":436,"m":"She has."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Will she away tonight?"],"id":437,"m":"Will she leave tonight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["As you'll have her."],"id":438,"m":"As you want her to."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I have writ my letters, casketed my treasure,","Given order for our horses; and tonight,","When I should take possession of the bride,","End ere I do begin."],"id":439,"m":"I've written my letters, locked up my valuables and ordered our horses. And tonight, when I should take possession of my bride, I'll end before I begin."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["A good traveller is something at the latter end of a dinner; but one","that lies three-thirds and uses a known truth to pass a thousand","nothings with, should be once heard and thrice beaten.— God save you,","Captain."],"id":440,"m":"A good traveller is worth something at the end of a dinner. But one who lies two-thirds of the time, and uses one known truth to pass off a thousand pieces of nonsense, should be heard once and beaten three times. God save you, Captain."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Is there any unkindness between my lord and you, monsieur?"],"id":441,"m":"Is there some bad feeling between my lord and you, monsieur?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I know not how I have deserved to run into my lord's displeasure."],"id":442,"m":"I don't know how I've deserved to fall into my lord's displeasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["You have made shift to run into 't, boots and spurs and all, like him","that leapt into the custard; and out of it you'll run again, rather","than suffer question for your residence."],"id":443,"m":"You've managed to run into it, boots, spurs and all, like the clown who jumps into the custard at the feast; and you'll run out of it again rather than be asked why you're there."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["It may be you have mistaken him, my lord."],"id":444,"m":"Perhaps you've misjudged him, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["And shall do so ever, though I took him at his prayers. Fare you well,","my lord; and believe this of me, there can be no kernal in this light","nut; the soul of this man is his clothes; trust him not in matter of","heavy consequence; I have kept of them tame, and know their natures.","Farewell, monsieur; I have spoken better of you than you have or will","to deserve at my hand; but we must do good against evil."],"id":445,"m":"And I always will, even if I caught him at his prayers. Goodbye, my lord, and believe me: there can be no kernel in this light nut. This man's soul is his clothes. Don't trust him in any serious matter. I've kept creatures like him as pets and know their nature. Goodbye, monsieur. I've spoken better of you than you have deserved or ever will deserve from me, but we must return good for evil."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":446},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["An idle lord, I swear."],"id":447,"m":"A foolish lord, I swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I think so."],"id":448,"m":"I think so."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Why, do you not know him?"],"id":449,"m":"Why, don't you know him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Yes, I do know him well; and common speech","Gives him a worthy pass. Here comes my clog."],"id":450,"m":"Yes, I know him well, and people speak well of him. Here comes my ball and chain."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Helena.","id":451},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I have, sir, as I was commanded from you,","Spoke with the king, and have procur'd his leave","For present parting; only he desires","Some private speech with you."],"id":452,"m":"Sir, as you ordered, I have spoken with the King and got his permission to leave at once. Only he wants to speak with you in private."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I shall obey his will.","You must not marvel, Helen, at my course,","Which holds not colour with the time, nor does","The ministration and required office","On my particular. Prepared I was not","For such a business; therefore am I found","So much unsettled: this drives me to entreat you;","That presently you take your way for home,","And rather muse than ask why I entreat you:","For my respects are better than they seem;","And my appointments have in them a need","Greater than shows itself at the first view","To you that know them not. This to my mother."],"id":453,"m":"I'll obey his wishes. You mustn't be surprised, Helen, at what I'm doing, which doesn't suit the occasion, nor do I carry out the duties expected of me. I wasn't prepared for such a business, and so I'm very unsettled. This makes me ask you to set off for home at once, and to wonder rather than ask why I ask you, because my reasons are better than they look, and my business has a need in it greater than appears at first sight to you, who don't know about it. This is for my mother."},{"t":"sd","text":"Giving a letter.","id":454},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","cont":true,"lines":["'Twill be two days ere I shall see you; so","I leave you to your wisdom."],"id":455,"m":"It will be two days before I see you. So I leave you to your good sense."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Sir, I can nothing say","But that I am your most obedient servant."],"id":456,"m":"Sir, I can say nothing except that I am your most obedient servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Come, come, no more of that."],"id":457,"m":"Come, come, no more of that."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["And ever shall","With true observance seek to eke out that","Wherein toward me my homely stars have fail'd","To equal my great fortune."],"id":458,"m":"And I will always try, with loyal service, to make up for what my humble birth has failed to give me to match my great good fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Let that go.","My haste is very great. Farewell; hie home."],"id":459,"m":"Never mind that. I'm in a great hurry. Goodbye. Hurry home."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Pray, sir, your pardon."],"id":460,"m":"Please, sir, forgive me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Well, what would you say?"],"id":461,"m":"Well, what do you want to say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I am not worthy of the wealth I owe;","Nor dare I say 'tis mine, and yet it is;","But, like a timorous thief, most fain would steal","What law does vouch mine own."],"id":462,"m":"I'm not worthy of the wealth I own, and I don't dare say it's mine, and yet it is. But like a nervous thief, I'd very much like to steal what the law says is my own."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["What would you have?"],"id":463,"m":"What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Something; and scarce so much; nothing indeed.","I would not tell you what I would, my lord. Faith, yes,","Strangers and foes do sunder and not kiss."],"id":464,"m":"Something, and hardly even that: nothing, really. I'd rather not tell you what I want, my lord. Well, yes: strangers and enemies part without a kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I pray you, stay not, but in haste to horse."],"id":465,"m":"Please, don't wait, but hurry to your horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I shall not break your bidding, good my lord.","Where are my other men, monsieur?","Farewell,"],"id":466,"m":"I won't disobey you, my good lord. Where are my other servants, monsieur? Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Helena.","id":467},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Go thou toward home, where I will never come","Whilst I can shake my sword or hear the drum.","Away, and for our flight."],"id":468,"m":"You go home, where I will never come as long as I can wave a sword or hear a drum. Away, and let's make our escape."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Bravely, coragio!"],"id":469,"m":"Bravely! Courage!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":470},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Florence. A room in the Duke's palace.","id":471},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter the Duke of Florence attended; two French Lords, and Soldiers.","id":472},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["So that, from point to point, now have you heard","The fundamental reasons of this war,","Whose great decision hath much blood let forth,","And more thirsts after."],"id":473,"m":"So now you have heard, point by point, the basic reasons for this war, whose great outcome has already cost much blood and thirsts for more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Holy seems the quarrel","Upon your Grace's part; black and fearful","On the opposer."],"id":474,"m":"The quarrel seems holy on your Grace's side, and black and frightening on your enemy's."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Therefore we marvel much our cousin France","Would, in so just a business, shut his bosom","Against our borrowing prayers."],"id":475,"m":"So we're very surprised that our cousin the King of France should, in so just a cause, close his heart against our requests for help."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Good my lord,","The reasons of our state I cannot yield,","But like a common and an outward man","That the great figure of a council frames","By self-unable motion; therefore dare not","Say what I think of it, since I have found","Myself in my incertain grounds to fail","As often as I guess'd."],"id":476,"m":"My good lord, I can't explain the reasons of our state, except as an ordinary outsider who imagines the great workings of a council with no power of his own to understand them. So I don't dare say what I think about it, since I've found myself wrong every time I've guessed on such uncertain ground."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Be it his pleasure."],"id":477,"m":"Let it be as he pleases."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["But I am sure the younger of our nature,","That surfeit on their ease, will day by day","Come here for physic."],"id":478,"m":"But I'm sure the younger men of our kind, who are sick of their easy life, will come here day by day for a cure."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Welcome shall they be;","And all the honours that can fly from us","Shall on them settle. You know your places well;","When better fall, for your avails they fell.","Tomorrow to the field."],"id":479,"m":"They'll be welcome, and every honour I can give will settle on them. You know your positions well. When better ones fall vacant, they fall for your benefit. Tomorrow, to the battlefield."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt.","id":480},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Rossillon. A room in the Countess's palace.","id":481},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Countess and Clown.","id":482},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["It hath happen'd all as I would have had it, save that he comes not","along with her."],"id":483,"m":"Everything has happened as I wanted, except that he isn't coming with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["By my troth, I take my young lord to be a very melancholy man."],"id":484,"m":"Honestly, I think my young lord is a very melancholy man."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["By what observance, I pray you?"],"id":485,"m":"What makes you say so, may I ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Why, he will look upon his boot and sing; mend the ruff and sing; ask","questions and sing; pick his teeth and sing. I know a man that had this","trick of melancholy sold a goodly manor for a song."],"id":486,"m":"Why, he looks at his boot and sings, straightens his collar and sings, asks questions and sings, picks his teeth and sings. I knew a man with this melancholy habit who sold a fine estate for a song."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Let me see what he writes, and when he means to come."],"id":487,"m":"Let me see what he writes, and when he plans to come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Opening a letter.","id":488},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I have no mind to Isbel since I was at court. Our old lings and our","Isbels o' th' country are nothing like your old ling and your Isbels o'","th' court. The brains of my Cupid's knock'd out, and I begin to love,","as an old man loves money, with no stomach."],"id":489,"m":"I've lost my taste for Isbel since I was at court. Our old salt fish and our Isbels in the country are nothing like your old salt fish and your Isbels at court. My Cupid's brains have been knocked out, and I'm starting to love the way an old man loves money: with no appetite."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["What have we here?"],"id":490,"m":"What have we here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["E'en that you have there."],"id":491,"m":"Just what you have there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":492},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["[Reads] I have sent you a daughter-in-law; she hath recovered the","king and undone me. I have wedded her, not bedded her, and sworn to","make the \"not\" eternal. You shall hear I am run away; know it before","the report come. If there be breadth enough in the world, I will hold a","long distance. My duty to you.","Your unfortunate son,","BERTRAM."],"id":493,"m":"[Reads] \"I have sent you a daughter-in-law. She has cured the King and ruined me. I have married her but not slept with her, and I've sworn to make the 'not' last for ever. You'll hear that I have run away; know it before the news arrives. If the world is wide enough, I'll keep a long way off. My respects to you. Your unhappy son, Bertram.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["This is not well, rash and unbridled boy,","To fly the favours of so good a king,","To pluck his indignation on thy head","By the misprizing of a maid too virtuous","For the contempt of empire."],"id":494,"m":"This is wrong, you rash, uncontrolled boy, to run away from the favours of so good a king, and bring his anger down on your head by undervaluing a girl too virtuous to be scorned even by an emperor."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Clown.","id":495},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["O madam, yonder is heavy news within between two soldiers and my young","lady."],"id":496,"m":"Oh, madam, there's sad news in there, between two soldiers and my young lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["What is the matter?"],"id":497,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Nay, there is some comfort in the news, some comfort; your son will not","be kill'd so soon as I thought he would."],"id":498,"m":"No, there's some comfort in the news, some comfort: your son won't be killed as soon as I thought."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Why should he be kill'd?"],"id":499,"m":"Why should he be killed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["So say I, madam, if he run away, as I hear he does; the danger is in","standing to't; that's the loss of men, though it be the getting of","children. Here they come will tell you more. For my part, I only hear","your son was run away."],"id":500,"m":"That's what I say, madam, if he runs away, as I hear he does. The danger is in standing your ground: that's what loses men, though it's also what gets children. Here come people who will tell you more. For my part, I only hear that your son has run away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":501},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Helena and the two Gentlemen.","id":502},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Save you, good madam."],"id":503,"m":"God save you, good madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Madam, my lord is gone, for ever gone."],"id":504,"m":"Madam, my lord is gone, gone for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Do not say so."],"id":505,"m":"Don't say that."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Think upon patience. Pray you, gentlemen,—","I have felt so many quirks of joy and grief","That the first face of neither on the start","Can woman me unto 't. Where is my son, I pray you?"],"id":506,"m":"Think about patience. Please, gentlemen: I have felt so many sudden turns of joy and grief that neither one can make me cry like a woman at first sight. Where is my son, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Madam, he's gone to serve the Duke of Florence;","We met him thitherward, for thence we came,","And, after some despatch in hand at court,","Thither we bend again."],"id":507,"m":"Madam, he's gone to serve the Duke of Florence. We met him on his way there, because that's where we came from, and once we've done some business at court we're heading back there."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Look on this letter, madam; here's my passport."],"id":508,"m":"Look at this letter, madam. Here's my passport."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["[Reads] When thou canst get the ring upon my finger, which never","shall come off, and show me a child begotten of thy body that I am","father to, then call me husband; but in such a \"then\" I write a","\"never\".","This is a dreadful sentence."],"id":509,"m":"[Reads] \"When you can get the ring from my finger, which will never come off, and show me a child born from your body that I am the father of, then call me husband. But in such a 'then' I write a 'never'.\" This is a terrible sentence."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Brought you this letter, gentlemen?"],"id":510,"m":"Did you bring this letter, gentlemen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Ay, madam; And for the contents' sake, are sorry for our pains."],"id":511,"m":"Yes, madam, and because of what's in it, we're sorry we took the trouble."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["I pr'ythee, lady, have a better cheer;","If thou engrossest all the griefs are thine,","Thou robb'st me of a moiety. He was my son,","But I do wash his name out of my blood,","And thou art all my child. Towards Florence is he?"],"id":512,"m":"Please, lady, cheer up. If you take all the griefs on yourself, you rob me of my half. He was my son, but I wash his name out of my blood, and you are my only child. Is he going to Florence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Ay, madam."],"id":513,"m":"Yes, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["And to be a soldier?"],"id":514,"m":"And to be a soldier?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["Such is his noble purpose, and, believe't,","The duke will lay upon him all the honour","That good convenience claims."],"id":515,"m":"That's his noble intention, and believe me, the Duke will give him all the honour that's fitting."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Return you thither?"],"id":516,"m":"Are you going back there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Ay, madam, with the swiftest wing of speed."],"id":517,"m":"Yes, madam, as fast as we can fly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["[Reads] Till I have no wife, I have nothing in France.","'Tis bitter."],"id":518,"m":"[Reads] \"Until I have no wife, I have nothing in France.\" That's bitter."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Find you that there?"],"id":519,"m":"Does it say that there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Ay, madam."],"id":520,"m":"Yes, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["'Tis but the boldness of his hand haply, which his heart was not","consenting to."],"id":521,"m":"Perhaps it's just his hand being bold, and his heart didn't agree."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Nothing in France until he have no wife!","There's nothing here that is too good for him","But only she, and she deserves a lord","That twenty such rude boys might tend upon,","And call her hourly mistress. Who was with him?"],"id":522,"m":"Nothing in France until he has no wife! There's nothing here that is too good for him except her, and she deserves a lord whom twenty rude boys like him should serve and call her mistress every hour. Who was with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["A servant only, and a gentleman which I have sometime known."],"id":523,"m":"Only a servant, and a gentleman I once knew."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Parolles, was it not?"],"id":524,"m":"Parolles, wasn't it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Ay, my good lady, he."],"id":525,"m":"Yes, my good lady, him."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["A very tainted fellow, and full of wickedness.","My son corrupts a well-derived nature","With his inducement."],"id":526,"m":"A thoroughly corrupt fellow, full of wickedness. My son spoils his good inherited nature by listening to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST GENTLEMAN","lines":["Indeed, good lady,","The fellow has a deal of that too much,","Which holds him much to have."],"id":527,"m":"Indeed, good lady, the fellow has a great deal too much of that wickedness, which serves him very well."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Y'are welcome, gentlemen.","I will entreat you, when you see my son,","To tell him that his sword can never win","The honour that he loses: more I'll entreat you","Written to bear along."],"id":528,"m":"You're welcome, gentlemen. I'll ask you, when you see my son, to tell him that his sword can never win back the honour he is losing. I'll ask you to take more of my message in writing."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND GENTLEMAN","lines":["We serve you, madam,","In that and all your worthiest affairs."],"id":529,"m":"We are at your service, madam, in that and in all your most worthy business."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Not so, but as we change our courtesies.","Will you draw near?"],"id":530,"m":"Not that, but only as we exchange kindnesses. Will you come with me?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Countess and Gentlemen.","id":531},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["\"Till I have no wife, I have nothing in France.\"","Nothing in France until he has no wife!","Thou shalt have none, Rossillon, none in France;","Then hast thou all again. Poor lord, is't I","That chase thee from thy country, and expose","Those tender limbs of thine to the event","Of the none-sparing war? And is it I","That drive thee from the sportive court, where thou","Wast shot at with fair eyes, to be the mark","Of smoky muskets? O you leaden messengers,","That ride upon the violent speed of fire,","Fly with false aim; move the still-peering air,","That sings with piercing; do not touch my lord.","Whoever shoots at him, I set him there;","Whoever charges on his forward breast,","I am the caitiff that do hold him to't;","And though I kill him not, I am the cause","His death was so effected. Better 'twere","I met the ravin lion when he roar'd","With sharp constraint of hunger; better 'twere","That all the miseries which nature owes","Were mine at once. No; come thou home, Rossillon,","Whence honour but of danger wins a scar,","As oft it loses all. I will be gone;","My being here it is that holds thee hence.","Shall I stay here to do't? No, no, although","The air of paradise did fan the house,","And angels offic'd all. I will be gone,","That pitiful rumour may report my flight","To consolate thine ear. Come, night; end, day;","For with the dark, poor thief, I'll steal away."],"id":532,"m":"\"Until I have no wife, I have nothing in France.\" Nothing in France until he has no wife! You'll have none, Rossillon, none in France; then you'll have everything again. Poor lord, is it me who is chasing you out of your country, and exposing your tender body to the chances of merciless war? And is it me who drives you from the playful court, where beautiful eyes shot at you, to be the target of smoking muskets? Oh, you lead bullets that ride on the violent speed of fire, fly wide of your aim! Pierce the air that whistles as you cut through it, and closes up behind you; don't touch my lord. Whoever shoots at him, I put him there; whoever charges at his brave chest, I'm the wretch who holds him to it. And though I don't kill him, I'm the cause of his death if it happens. It would be better for me to meet the ravenous lion when he roars with the sharp pain of hunger; better that all the miseries nature has to give were mine at once. No: come home, Rossillon, from where honour wins only a scar from danger, and often loses everything. I'll go. It's my being here that keeps you away. Shall I stay here and do that? No, no, even if the air of paradise cooled the house and angels did all the work. I'll go, so that rumour can pityingly report my running away and comfort your ears. Come, night; end, day; because with the dark I'll steal away like a poor thief."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":533},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"Florence. Before the Duke's palace.","id":534},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter the Duke of Florence, Bertram, drum and trumpets, Soldiers, Parolles.","id":535},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["The general of our horse thou art, and we,","Great in our hope, lay our best love and credence","Upon thy promising fortune."],"id":536,"m":"You are general of our cavalry, and we, full of hope, place our deepest love and trust in your promising fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Sir, it is","A charge too heavy for my strength; but yet","We'll strive to bear it for your worthy sake","To th'extreme edge of hazard."],"id":537,"m":"Sir, it's a responsibility too heavy for my strength, but we'll do our best to carry it, for your worthy sake, to the very edge of danger."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUKE","lines":["Then go thou forth;","And fortune play upon thy prosperous helm,","As thy auspicious mistress!"],"id":538,"m":"Then go out, and may Fortune smile on your successful helmet like a lucky mistress!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["This very day,","Great Mars, I put myself into thy file;","Make me but like my thoughts, and I shall prove","A lover of thy drum, hater of love."],"id":539,"m":"This very day, great Mars, I join your ranks. Only make me as good as my thoughts, and I'll prove a lover of your drum and a hater of love."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":540},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"Rossillon. A room in the Countess's palace.","id":541},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Countess and Steward.","id":542},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Alas! and would you take the letter of her?","Might you not know she would do as she has done,","By sending me a letter? Read it again."],"id":543,"m":"Oh no! And did you take the letter from her? Couldn't you tell she would do what she has done, if she was sending me a letter? Read it again."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEWARD","lines":["[Reads] I am Saint Jaques' pilgrim, thither gone.","Ambitious love hath so in me offended","That barefoot plod I the cold ground upon,","With sainted vow my faults to have amended.","Write, write, that from the bloody course of war","My dearest master, your dear son, may hie.","Bless him at home in peace, whilst I from far","His name with zealous fervour sanctify.","His taken labours bid him me forgive;","I, his despiteful Juno, sent him forth","From courtly friends, with camping foes to live,","Where death and danger dog the heels of worth.","He is too good and fair for death and me;","Whom I myself embrace to set him free."],"id":544,"m":"[Reads] \"I'm a pilgrim of Saint Jaques, and gone there. My ambitious love has sinned so badly that I'm trudging barefoot on the cold ground, with a holy vow to have my faults put right. Write, write, so that my dearest master, your dear son, may hurry home from the bloody course of war. Bless him at home in peace, while I, far away, honour his name with my devoted prayers. Tell him to forgive me for the hardships he has taken on. I, his cruel Juno, sent him away from his friends at court to live among enemies in camp, where death and danger chase at the heels of the brave. He is too good and too handsome for death and me. I embrace death myself to set him free.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Ah, what sharp stings are in her mildest words!","Rynaldo, you did never lack advice so much","As letting her pass so; had I spoke with her,","I could have well diverted her intents,","Which thus she hath prevented."],"id":545,"m":"Ah, what sharp stings there are in her gentlest words! Rinaldo, you never showed such poor judgement as letting her go like that. If I had spoken with her, I could easily have turned her from her plans, which she has now got ahead of."},{"t":"sp","who":"STEWARD","lines":["Pardon me, madam;","If I had given you this at over-night,","She might have been o'erta'en; and yet she writes","Pursuit would be but vain."],"id":546,"m":"Forgive me, madam. If I had given you this last night, she might have been caught up with. And yet she writes that chasing her would be useless."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["What angel shall","Bless this unworthy husband? He cannot thrive,","Unless her prayers, whom heaven delights to hear","And loves to grant, reprieve him from the wrath","Of greatest justice. Write, write, Rynaldo,","To this unworthy husband of his wife;","Let every word weigh heavy of her worth,","That he does weigh too light; my greatest grief,","Though little he do feel it, set down sharply.","Dispatch the most convenient messenger.","When haply he shall hear that she is gone","He will return; and hope I may that she,","Hearing so much, will speed her foot again,","Led hither by pure love. Which of them both","Is dearest to me I have no skill in sense","To make distinction. Provide this messenger.","My heart is heavy, and mine age is weak;","Grief would have tears, and sorrow bids me speak."],"id":547,"m":"What angel will bless this unworthy husband? He can't prosper unless her prayers, which heaven delights to hear and loves to grant, save him from the anger of the highest justice. Write, write, Rinaldo, to this husband unworthy of his wife. Let every word weigh heavy with her worth, which he values too lightly. Write down sharply my great grief, though he feels it little. Send the quickest messenger. Perhaps when he hears she has gone he will come back, and I can hope that she, hearing that, will hurry back here too, led by pure love. Which of them is dearer to me I can't tell. Get this messenger ready. My heart is heavy, and my age is weak. Grief wants tears, and sorrow makes me speak."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":548},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"V","loc":"Without the walls of Florence.","id":549},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter an old Widow of Florence, Diana, Violenta, Mariana and other Citizens.","id":550},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Nay, come; for if they do approach the city, we shall lose all the","sight."],"id":551,"m":"No, come on, because if they get to the city we'll miss the whole show."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["They say the French count has done most honourable service."],"id":552,"m":"They say the French count has done very honourable service."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["It is reported that he has taken their great'st commander, and that","with his own hand he slew the duke's brother."],"id":553,"m":"It's reported that he has captured their most important commander, and killed the Duke's brother with his own hand."},{"t":"sd","text":"A tucket afar off.","id":554},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","cont":true,"lines":["We have lost our labour; they are gone a contrary way. Hark! you may","know by their trumpets."],"id":555,"m":"We've wasted our effort; they've gone the other way. Listen! You can tell by their trumpets."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["Come, let's return again, and suffice ourselves with the report of it.","Well, Diana, take heed of this French earl; the honour of a maid is her","name; and no legacy is so rich as honesty."],"id":556,"m":"Come, let's go back and be content with hearing about it. Well, Diana, watch out for this French count. A girl's honour is her good name, and no inheritance is as rich as chastity."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["I have told my neighbour how you have been solicited by a gentleman his","companion."],"id":557,"m":"I've told my neighbour how you've been pestered by a gentleman, his companion."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["I know that knave; hang him! one Parolles; a filthy officer he is in","those suggestions for the young earl. Beware of them, Diana; their","promises, enticements, oaths, tokens, and all these engines of lust,","are not the things they go under; many a maid hath been seduced by","them; and the misery is, example, that so terrible shows in the wreck","of maidenhood, cannot for all that dissuade succession, but that they","are limed with the twigs that threaten them. I hope I need not to","advise you further; but I hope your own grace will keep you where you","are, though there were no further danger known but the modesty which is","so lost."],"id":558,"m":"I know that rogue, hang him! A man called Parolles: a filthy go-between for the young count in these proposals. Watch out for them, Diana. Their promises, temptations, oaths, love tokens and all these weapons of lust are not what they pretend to be. Many a girl has been seduced by them; and the misery of it is that the example, which looks so terrible in the wreck of a girl's virginity, still can't stop others following, but they're caught in the very traps that threaten them. I hope I don't need to advise you further, and I hope your own goodness will keep you as you are, even if the only danger were the loss of modesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["You shall not need to fear me."],"id":559,"m":"You needn't worry about me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Helena in the dress of a pilgrim.","id":560},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["I hope so. Look, here comes a pilgrim. I know she will lie at my house;","thither they send one another; I'll question her. God save you,","pilgrim! Whither are bound?"],"id":561,"m":"I hope so. Look, here comes a pilgrim. I'm sure she'll stay at my house; they send each other there. I'll ask her. God save you, pilgrim! Where are you heading?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["To Saint Jaques le Grand.","Where do the palmers lodge, I do beseech you?"],"id":562,"m":"To Saint Jaques the Great. Where do the pilgrims stay, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["At the Saint Francis here, beside the port."],"id":563,"m":"At the Saint Francis inn here, beside the gate."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Is this the way?"],"id":564,"m":"Is this the way?"},{"t":"sd","text":"A march afar.","id":565},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Ay, marry, is't. Hark you, they come this way.","If you will tarry, holy pilgrim,","But till the troops come by,","I will conduct you where you shall be lodg'd;","The rather for I think I know your hostess","As ample as myself."],"id":566,"m":"Yes, it is indeed. Listen, they're coming this way. If you'll wait, holy pilgrim, just until the troops go by, I'll take you to where you'll stay, all the more since I think I know your landlady as well as I know myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Is it yourself?"],"id":567,"m":"Is it you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["If you shall please so, pilgrim."],"id":568,"m":"If you like, pilgrim."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I thank you, and will stay upon your leisure."],"id":569,"m":"Thank you. I'll wait until you're ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["You came, I think, from France?"],"id":570,"m":"You've come from France, I think?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I did so."],"id":571,"m":"I have."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Here you shall see a countryman of yours","That has done worthy service."],"id":572,"m":"Here you'll see a countryman of yours who has done fine service."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["His name, I pray you."],"id":573,"m":"What's his name, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["The Count Rossillon. Know you such a one?"],"id":574,"m":"The Count of Rossillon. Do you know him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["But by the ear, that hears most nobly of him;","His face I know not."],"id":575,"m":"Only by hearsay, and I hear very well of him. I don't know his face."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Whatsome'er he is,","He's bravely taken here. He stole from France,","As 'tis reported, for the king had married him","Against his liking. Think you it is so?"],"id":576,"m":"Whatever he is, he's thought very highly of here. He ran away from France, they say, because the King married him against his will. Do you think that's true?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Ay, surely, mere the truth; I know his lady."],"id":577,"m":"Yes, certainly, the plain truth. I know his wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["There is a gentleman that serves the count","Reports but coarsely of her."],"id":578,"m":"There's a gentleman who serves the count who speaks very rudely of her."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["What's his name?"],"id":579,"m":"What's his name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Monsieur Parolles."],"id":580,"m":"Monsieur Parolles."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O, I believe with him,","In argument of praise, or to the worth","Of the great count himself, she is too mean","To have her name repeated; all her deserving","Is a reserved honesty, and that","I have not heard examin'd."],"id":581,"m":"Oh, I agree with him that, when it comes to praise, or compared with the worth of the great count himself, she's too low to have her name mentioned. All her merit is a modest chastity, and I haven't heard that questioned."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Alas, poor lady!","'Tis a hard bondage to become the wife","Of a detesting lord."],"id":582,"m":"Oh, poor lady! It's a hard slavery to be the wife of a lord who hates you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Ay, right; good creature, wheresoe'er she is,","Her heart weighs sadly. This young maid might do her","A shrewd turn, if she pleas'd."],"id":583,"m":"Yes, that's right. The good creature, wherever she is, her heart must be sad. This young girl could do her a nasty turn if she wanted."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["How do you mean?","Maybe the amorous count solicits her","In the unlawful purpose."],"id":584,"m":"What do you mean? Perhaps the lovesick count is chasing her for an unlawful purpose?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["He does indeed,","And brokes with all that can in such a suit","Corrupt the tender honour of a maid;","But she is arm'd for him, and keeps her guard","In honestest defence."],"id":585,"m":"He is indeed, and he's bargaining with everything that, in such wooing, can corrupt a girl's tender honour. But she is armed against him and keeps up her guard in the most honest defence."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, with a drum and colours, a party of the Florentine army, Bertram and Parolles.","id":586},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["The gods forbid else!"],"id":587,"m":"God forbid it were otherwise!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["So, now they come.","That is Antonio, the Duke's eldest son;","That Escalus."],"id":588,"m":"So, here they come now. That's Antonio, the Duke's eldest son; that's Escalus."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Which is the Frenchman?"],"id":589,"m":"Which one is the Frenchman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["He;","That with the plume; 'tis a most gallant fellow.","I would he lov'd his wife; if he were honester","He were much goodlier. Is't not a handsome gentleman?"],"id":590,"m":"Him, with the feather. He's a most dashing fellow. I wish he loved his wife. If he were more honest he'd be much better looking. Isn't he a handsome gentleman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I like him well."],"id":591,"m":"I like him very much."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["'Tis pity he is not honest. Yond's that same knave","That leads him to these places. Were I his lady","I would poison that vile rascal."],"id":592,"m":"It's a pity he isn't honest. That's the same rogue over there who leads him to these places. If I were his wife I'd poison that vile scoundrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Which is he?"],"id":593,"m":"Which one is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["That jack-an-apes with scarfs. Why is he melancholy?"],"id":594,"m":"That monkey with the scarves. Why is he so gloomy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Perchance he's hurt i' the battle."],"id":595,"m":"Perhaps he was hurt in the battle."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Lose our drum! Well."],"id":596,"m":"Lose our drum! Well."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["He's shrewdly vex'd at something. Look, he has spied us."],"id":597,"m":"He's badly upset about something. Look, he's seen us."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Marry, hang you!"],"id":598,"m":"Oh, go hang yourself!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARIANA","lines":["And your courtesy, for a ring-carrier!"],"id":599,"m":"And your fine manners too, you pimp!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bertram, Parolles, Officers and Soldiers.","id":600},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["The troop is past. Come, pilgrim, I will bring you","Where you shall host; of enjoin'd penitents","There's four or five, to great Saint Jaques bound,","Already at my house."],"id":601,"m":"The soldiers have gone past. Come, pilgrim, I'll take you to where you'll stay. There are four or five penitents already at my house, on their way to great Saint Jaques."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I humbly thank you.","Please it this matron and this gentle maid","To eat with us tonight; the charge and thanking","Shall be for me; and, to requite you further,","I will bestow some precepts of this virgin,","Worthy the note."],"id":602,"m":"Thank you humbly. If this lady and this gentle girl would eat with us tonight, the cost and the thanks will be mine. And to repay you further, I'll give some advice about this young woman that's worth hearing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH","lines":["We'll take your offer kindly."],"id":603,"m":"We gladly accept your offer."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":604},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"VI","loc":"Camp before Florence.","id":605},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram and the two French Lords.","id":606},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Nay, good my lord, put him to't; let him have his way."],"id":607,"m":"No, my good lord, put him to the test. Let him do it his way."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["If your lordship find him not a hilding, hold me no more in your","respect."],"id":608,"m":"If your lordship doesn't find him a good-for-nothing, stop respecting me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["On my life, my lord, a bubble."],"id":609,"m":"On my life, my lord, he's a bubble."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Do you think I am so far deceived in him?"],"id":610,"m":"Do you think I'm so badly deceived in him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Believe it, my lord, in mine own direct knowledge, without any malice,","but to speak of him as my kinsman, he's a most notable coward, an","infinite and endless liar, an hourly promise-breaker, the owner of no","one good quality worthy your lordship's entertainment."],"id":611,"m":"Believe it, my lord. I know it directly, without any malice, and speak of him as if he were my own relative: he's a notorious coward, an infinite and endless liar, a promise-breaker every hour, and has not one good quality worth your lordship's notice."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["It were fit you knew him; lest, reposing too far in his virtue, which","he hath not, he might at some great and trusty business, in a main","danger fail you."],"id":612,"m":"You ought to know him, in case you rely too much on a virtue he doesn't have, and he lets you down in some great and important business, in serious danger."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I would I knew in what particular action to try him."],"id":613,"m":"I wish I knew some particular action to test him with."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["None better than to let him fetch off his drum, which you hear him so","confidently undertake to do."],"id":614,"m":"Nothing better than letting him go and recover his drum, which you heard him so confidently promise to do."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I with a troop of Florentines will suddenly surprise him; such I will","have whom I am sure he knows not from the enemy; we will bind and","hoodwink him so that he shall suppose no other but that he is carried","into the leaguer of the adversaries when we bring him to our own tents.","Be but your lordship present at his examination; if he do not for the","promise of his life, and in the highest compulsion of base fear, offer","to betray you, and deliver all the intelligence in his power against","you, and that with the divine forfeit of his soul upon oath, never","trust my judgment in anything."],"id":615,"m":"I'll ambush him suddenly with a troop of Florentines, men I'm sure he can't tell apart from the enemy. We'll tie him up and blindfold him, so that he'll think he's being carried into the enemy camp when we take him to our own tents. Just be there, your lordship, when we question him. If he doesn't, for the promise of his life and driven by the lowest fear, offer to betray you and tell all he knows against you, and swear it on his immortal soul, never trust my judgement in anything again."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["O, for the love of laughter, let him fetch his drum; he says he has a","stratagem for't. When your lordship sees the bottom of his success","in't, and to what metal this counterfeit lump of ore will be melted, if","you give him not John Drum's entertainment, your inclining cannot be","removed. Here he comes."],"id":616,"m":"Oh, for the love of laughter, let him fetch his drum; he says he has a plan for it. When your lordship sees how his success turns out, and what metal this fake lump of ore melts down to, if you don't give him a good drumming out, your liking for him can never be shifted. Here he comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Parolles.","id":617},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["O, for the love of laughter, hinder not the honour of his design: let","him fetch off his drum in any hand."],"id":618,"m":"Oh, for the love of laughter, don't get in the way of his honourable plan. Let him fetch his drum by any means."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["How now, monsieur! This drum sticks sorely in your disposition."],"id":619,"m":"Hello, monsieur! This drum sticks badly in your mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["A pox on 't; let it go; 'tis but a drum."],"id":620,"m":"To hell with it, let it go. It's only a drum."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["But a drum! Is't but a drum? A drum so lost! There was excellent","command, to charge in with our horse upon our own wings, and to rend","our own soldiers."],"id":621,"m":"Only a drum! Is it only a drum? A drum lost like that! That was a fine command, to charge our cavalry into our own wings and tear apart our own soldiers!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["That was not to be blam'd in the command of the service; it was a","disaster of war that Caesar himself could not have prevented, if he had","been there to command."],"id":622,"m":"The commanders weren't to blame for that. It was a disaster of war that Caesar himself couldn't have prevented if he had been in command."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Well, we cannot greatly condemn our success: some dishonour we had in","the loss of that drum, but it is not to be recovered."],"id":623,"m":"Well, we can't complain much about how it went. We had some disgrace in losing that drum, but it can't be recovered."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["It might have been recovered."],"id":624,"m":"It might have been recovered."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["It might, but it is not now."],"id":625,"m":"It might, but it can't be now."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["It is to be recovered. But that the merit of service is seldom","attributed to the true and exact performer, I would have that drum or","another, or hic jacet."],"id":626,"m":"It can be recovered. If it weren't that credit for service is so rarely given to the man who actually does it, I'd have that drum or another one, or \"here lies\" on my gravestone."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Why, if you have a stomach, to't, monsieur, if you think your mystery","in stratagem can bring this instrument of honour again into his native","quarter, be magnanimous in the enterprise, and go on; I will grace the","attempt for a worthy exploit; if you speed well in it, the duke shall","both speak of it and extend to you what further becomes his greatness,","even to the utmost syllable of your worthiness."],"id":627,"m":"Why, if you have the appetite for it, go to it, monsieur. If you think your skill in strategy can bring this symbol of honour back to its proper quarters, be bold in the attempt and go ahead. I'll honour the attempt as a worthy exploit. If you succeed, the Duke will both speak of it and give you whatever further reward suits his greatness, to the last syllable of what you deserve."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["By the hand of a soldier, I will undertake it."],"id":628,"m":"On a soldier's hand, I'll do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["But you must not now slumber in it."],"id":629,"m":"But you mustn't go to sleep on it now."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I'll about it this evening; and I will presently pen down my dilemmas,","encourage myself in my certainty, put myself into my mortal","preparation; and by midnight look to hear further from me."],"id":630,"m":"I'll be about it this evening. I'll write down my plans at once, encourage myself in my certainty and prepare myself for death. Expect to hear more from me by midnight."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["May I be bold to acquaint his grace you are gone about it?"],"id":631,"m":"May I tell his grace that you have gone about it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I know not what the success will be, my lord, but the attempt I vow."],"id":632,"m":"I don't know how it will turn out, my lord, but I swear I'll attempt it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I know th'art valiant; and to the possibility of thy soldiership, will","subscribe for thee. Farewell."],"id":633,"m":"I know you're brave, and I'll vouch for your soldiership to the limit of its possibility. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I love not many words."],"id":634,"m":"I don't like many words."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":635},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["No more than a fish loves water. Is not this a strange fellow, my lord,","that so confidently seems to undertake this business, which he knows is","not to be done; damns himself to do, and dares better be damn'd than to","do't."],"id":636,"m":"No more than a fish likes water. Isn't this a strange fellow, my lord, to seem so confident in taking on this business, which he knows can't be done? He damns himself by swearing to do it, and would rather be damned than do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["You do not know him, my lord, as we do; certain it is that he will","steal himself into a man's favour, and for a week escape a great deal","of discoveries, but when you find him out, you have him ever after."],"id":637,"m":"You don't know him as we do, my lord. It's certain that he can creep into a man's favour and for a week avoid being found out, but once you've found him out, you have him for ever after."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Why, do you think he will make no deed at all of this, that so","seriously he does address himself unto?"],"id":638,"m":"Why, do you think he won't do anything at all about this, when he commits himself to it so seriously?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["None in the world; but return with an invention, and clap upon you two","or three probable lies; but we have almost embossed him; you shall see","his fall tonight; for indeed he is not for your lordship's respect."],"id":639,"m":"Nothing in the world, except come back with a made-up story and hit you with two or three likely lies. But we've almost run him to ground. You'll see his fall tonight, because really he doesn't deserve your lordship's respect."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["We'll make you some sport with the fox ere we case him. He was first","smok'd by the old Lord Lafew; when his disguise and he is parted, tell","me what a sprat you shall find him; which you shall see this very","night."],"id":640,"m":"We'll give you some fun with the fox before we skin him. He was first sniffed out by old Lord Lafew. When his disguise and he are separated, tell me what a little fish you find him. You'll see it this very night."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I must go look my twigs. He shall be caught."],"id":641,"m":"I must go and set my traps. He'll be caught."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Your brother, he shall go along with me."],"id":642,"m":"Your brother can come with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["As't please your lordship. I'll leave you."],"id":643,"m":"As your lordship pleases. I'll leave you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":644},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Now will I lead you to the house, and show you","The lass I spoke of."],"id":645,"m":"Now I'll take you to the house and show you the girl I spoke about."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["But you say she's honest."],"id":646,"m":"But you say she's chaste."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["That's all the fault. I spoke with her but once,","And found her wondrous cold, but I sent to her","By this same coxcomb that we have i' the wind","Tokens and letters which she did re-send,","And this is all I have done. She's a fair creature;","Will you go see her?"],"id":647,"m":"That's her only fault. I spoke to her only once, and found her amazingly cold. But I sent her love tokens and letters through this same fool we're hunting, and she sent them back. That's all I've done. She's a beautiful creature. Will you come and see her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["With all my heart, my lord."],"id":648,"m":"With all my heart, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":649},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"VII","loc":"Florence. A room in the Widow's house.","id":650},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Helena and Widow.","id":651},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["If you misdoubt me that I am not she,","I know not how I shall assure you further,","But I shall lose the grounds I work upon."],"id":652,"m":"If you doubt that I am who I say, I don't know how I can convince you further without losing the ground my plan stands on."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Though my estate be fall'n, I was well born,","Nothing acquainted with these businesses,","And would not put my reputation now","In any staining act."],"id":653,"m":"Though I've come down in the world, I was born a gentlewoman. I know nothing of these sorts of business, and I wouldn't risk my reputation now on any act that might stain it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Nor would I wish you.","First give me trust, the count he is my husband,","And what to your sworn counsel I have spoken","Is so from word to word; and then you cannot,","By the good aid that I of you shall borrow,","Err in bestowing it."],"id":654,"m":"Nor would I want you to. First, believe that the count is my husband, and that what I've told you under your oath of secrecy is true, word for word. Then you can't go wrong in giving me the good help I'll borrow from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["I should believe you,","For you have show'd me that which well approves","Y'are great in fortune."],"id":655,"m":"I believe you, because you've shown me enough to prove you're a woman of great fortune."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Take this purse of gold,","And let me buy your friendly help thus far,","Which I will over-pay, and pay again","When I have found it. The count he woos your daughter","Lays down his wanton siege before her beauty,","Resolv'd to carry her; let her in fine consent,","As we'll direct her how 'tis best to bear it.","Now his important blood will naught deny","That she'll demand; a ring the county wears,","That downward hath succeeded in his house","From son to son, some four or five descents","Since the first father wore it. This ring he holds","In most rich choice; yet, in his idle fire,","To buy his will, it would not seem too dear,","Howe'er repented after."],"id":656,"m":"Take this purse of gold, and let me buy your friendly help so far. I'll pay you more, and pay you again, once I have it. The count is wooing your daughter, laying his lustful siege to her beauty, determined to conquer her. Let her finally agree, and we'll tell her the best way to handle it. His urgent desire won't refuse anything she asks for. The count wears a ring that has been handed down in his family from son to son for four or five generations since the first ancestor wore it. He values this ring very highly. Yet in his foolish passion, to get what he wants, it won't seem too high a price, however much he regrets it afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Now I see","The bottom of your purpose."],"id":657,"m":"Now I see what you're aiming at."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["You see it lawful then; it is no more","But that your daughter, ere she seems as won,","Desires this ring; appoints him an encounter;","In fine, delivers me to fill the time,","Herself most chastely absent. After,","To marry her, I'll add three thousand crowns","To what is pass'd already."],"id":658,"m":"Then you see it's lawful. It's only that your daughter, before she seems to give in, asks for this ring and arranges to meet him; and in the end hands me over to take her place, while she herself stays chastely away. Afterwards, for her marriage, I'll add three thousand crowns to what I've already given."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["I have yielded.","Instruct my daughter how she shall persever,","That time and place with this deceit so lawful","May prove coherent. Every night he comes","With musics of all sorts, and songs compos'd","To her unworthiness: it nothing steads us","To chide him from our eaves; for he persists","As if his life lay on 't."],"id":659,"m":"I agree. Teach my daughter how to behave so that time and place fit this lawful trick. Every night he comes with all kinds of music, and songs written about her unworthy self. It does us no good to scold him away from under our windows, because he persists as if his life depended on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Why then tonight","Let us assay our plot; which, if it speed,","Is wicked meaning in a lawful deed,","And lawful meaning in a lawful act,","Where both not sin, and yet a sinful fact.","But let's about it."],"id":660,"m":"Why then, tonight let's try our plan. If it succeeds, it is a wicked intention in a lawful deed and a lawful intention in a lawful act: neither of them sins, and yet the deed looks sinful. But let's get to it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":661},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Without the Florentine camp.","id":662},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter first Lord with five or six Soldiers in ambush.","id":663},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["He can come no other way but by this hedge-corner. When you sally upon","him, speak what terrible language you will; though you understand it","not yourselves, no matter; for we must not seem to understand him,","unless someone among us, whom we must produce for an interpreter."],"id":664,"m":"He can't come any other way but past this corner of the hedge. When you jump out at him, speak whatever terrible language you like. It doesn't matter if you don't understand it yourselves, because we mustn't seem to understand him, except for one of us, whom we'll set up as an interpreter."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Good captain, let me be th' interpreter."],"id":665,"m":"Good captain, let me be the interpreter."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Art not acquainted with him? Knows he not thy voice?"],"id":666,"m":"Don't you know him? Doesn't he know your voice?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["No sir, I warrant you."],"id":667,"m":"No, sir, I promise you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["But what linsey-woolsey has thou to speak to us again?"],"id":668,"m":"But what mixed-up gibberish will you speak back to us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["E'en such as you speak to me."],"id":669,"m":"The same kind you speak to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["He must think us some band of strangers i' the adversary's","entertainment. Now he hath a smack of all neighbouring languages,","therefore we must every one be a man of his own fancy; not to know what","we speak one to another, so we seem to know, is to know straight our","purpose: choughs' language, gabble enough, and good enough. As for you,","interpreter, you must seem very politic. But couch, ho! Here he comes;","to beguile two hours in a sleep, and then to return and swear the lies","he forges."],"id":670,"m":"He must think we're some band of foreign soldiers in the enemy's pay. Now, he has a taste of all the neighbouring languages, so each of us must make up his own. As long as we seem to understand each other, not knowing what we're saying to one another serves our purpose perfectly: jackdaw talk, gabble enough, and good enough. As for you, interpreter, you must look very cunning. But down, hide! Here he comes, to waste two hours sleeping, and then go back and swear to the lies he makes up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Parolles.","id":671},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Ten o'clock. Within these three hours 'twill be time enough to go home.","What shall I say I have done? It must be a very plausive invention that","carries it. They begin to smoke me, and disgraces have of late knock'd","too often at my door. I find my tongue is too foolhardy, but my heart","hath the fear of Mars before it, and of his creatures, not daring the","reports of my tongue."],"id":672,"m":"Ten o'clock. In three hours it'll be late enough to go home. What shall I say I've done? It will have to be a very convincing story to get away with it. They're starting to smell me out, and disgrace has knocked at my door too often lately. I find my tongue is too reckless, but my heart is afraid of Mars and his soldiers, and doesn't dare do what my tongue promises."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["[Aside] This is the first truth that e'er thine own tongue was","guilty of."],"id":673,"m":"[Aside] That's the first truth your tongue was ever guilty of."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["What the devil should move me to undertake the recovery of this drum,","being not ignorant of the impossibility, and knowing I had no such","purpose? I must give myself some hurts, and say I got them in exploit;","yet slight ones will not carry it. They will say \"Came you off with so","little?\" and great ones I dare not give. Wherefore, what's the","instance? Tongue, I must put you into a butter-woman's mouth, and buy","myself another of Bajazet's mule, if you prattle me into these perils."],"id":674,"m":"What the devil made me take on recovering this drum, when I knew it was impossible and I had no intention of doing it? I must give myself some wounds and say I got them in action. But small ones won't do it. They'll say, \"Did you get off so lightly?\" And I don't dare give myself big ones. So what's the proof to be? Tongue, I'll have to put you in a milkmaid's mouth and buy myself a mule's tongue instead, if you chatter me into these dangers."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["[Aside] Is it possible he should know what he is, and be that he is?"],"id":675,"m":"[Aside] Is it possible he can know what he is, and still be what he is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I would the cutting of my garments would serve the turn, or the","breaking of my Spanish sword."],"id":676,"m":"I wish cutting my clothes would do the trick, or breaking my Spanish sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["[Aside] We cannot afford you so."],"id":677,"m":"[Aside] We can't let you off like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Or the baring of my beard, and to say it was in stratagem."],"id":678,"m":"Or shaving off my beard, and saying it was part of a strategy."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["[Aside] 'Twould not do."],"id":679,"m":"[Aside] That wouldn't do."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Or to drown my clothes, and say I was stripped."],"id":680,"m":"Or soaking my clothes, and saying I was stripped."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["[Aside] Hardly serve."],"id":681,"m":"[Aside] That will hardly work."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Though I swore I leap'd from the window of the citadel,—"],"id":682,"m":"Even if I swore I jumped from the citadel window..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["[Aside] How deep?"],"id":683,"m":"[Aside] How high?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Thirty fathom."],"id":684,"m":"Thirty fathoms."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["[Aside] Three great oaths would scarce make that be believed."],"id":685,"m":"[Aside] Three great oaths would hardly make anyone believe that."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I would I had any drum of the enemy's; I would swear I recover'd it."],"id":686,"m":"I wish I had any drum of the enemy's. I'd swear I recovered it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["[Aside] You shall hear one anon."],"id":687,"m":"[Aside] You'll hear one in a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["A drum now of the enemy's!"],"id":688,"m":"An enemy drum, now!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum within.","id":689},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Throca movousus, cargo, cargo, cargo."],"id":690,"m":"Throca movousus, cargo, cargo, cargo."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Cargo, cargo, cargo, villianda par corbo, cargo."],"id":691,"m":"Cargo, cargo, cargo, villianda par corbo, cargo."},{"t":"sd","text":"They seize and blindfold him.","id":692},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["O, ransom, ransom! Do not hide mine eyes."],"id":693,"m":"Oh, ransom, ransom! Don't cover my eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Boskos thromuldo boskos."],"id":694,"m":"Boskos thromuldo boskos."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I know you are the Muskos' regiment,","And I shall lose my life for want of language.","If there be here German, or Dane, Low Dutch,","Italian, or French, let him speak to me,","I'll discover that which shall undo the Florentine."],"id":695,"m":"I know you're the Muskos regiment, and I'll lose my life because I can't speak the language. If there's anyone here who speaks German or Danish, Dutch, Italian or French, let him speak to me. I'll reveal what will ruin the Florentines."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Boskos vauvado. I understand thee, and can speak thy tongue.","Kerelybonto. Sir, Betake thee to thy faith, for seventeen poniards","are at thy bosom."],"id":696,"m":"Boskos vauvado. I understand you and can speak your language. Kerelybonto. Sir, say your prayers, because seventeen daggers are at your chest."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["O!"],"id":697,"m":"Oh!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["O, pray, pray, pray!","Manka revania dulche."],"id":698,"m":"Oh, pray, pray, pray! Manka revania dulche."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Oscorbidulchos volivorco."],"id":699,"m":"Oscorbidulchos volivorco."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["The General is content to spare thee yet;","And, hoodwink'd as thou art, will lead thee on","To gather from thee. Haply thou mayst inform","Something to save thy life."],"id":700,"m":"The General agrees to spare you for now, and will lead you away blindfolded as you are, to get information out of you. Perhaps you can tell something to save your life."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["O, let me live,","And all the secrets of our camp I'll show,","Their force, their purposes; nay, I'll speak that","Which you will wonder at."],"id":701,"m":"Oh, let me live, and I'll reveal all the secrets of our camp, their strength, their plans. Yes, I'll tell you things that will amaze you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["But wilt thou faithfully?"],"id":702,"m":"But will you do it truthfully?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["If I do not, damn me."],"id":703,"m":"If I don't, damn me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Acordo linta.","Come on; thou art granted space."],"id":704,"m":"Acordo linta. Come on. You're given time."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, with Parolles guarded.","id":705},{"t":"sd","text":"A short alarum within.","id":706},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Go tell the Count Rossillon and my brother","We have caught the woodcock, and will keep him muffled","Till we do hear from them."],"id":707,"m":"Go and tell the Count of Rossillon and my brother that we've caught the woodcock and will keep him blindfolded until we hear from them."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SOLDIER","lines":["Captain, I will."],"id":708,"m":"Captain, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["'A will betray us all unto ourselves;","Inform on that."],"id":709,"m":"He will betray all of us to ourselves. Tell them that."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SOLDIER","lines":["So I will, sir."],"id":710,"m":"I will, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Till then I'll keep him dark, and safely lock'd."],"id":711,"m":"Until then I'll keep him in the dark and safely locked up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":712},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Florence. A room in the Widow's house.","id":713},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram and Diana.","id":714},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["They told me that your name was Fontybell."],"id":715,"m":"They told me your name was Fontibell."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["No, my good lord, Diana."],"id":716,"m":"No, my good lord, Diana."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Titled goddess;","And worth it, with addition! But, fair soul,","In your fine frame hath love no quality?","If the quick fire of youth light not your mind,","You are no maiden but a monument;","When you are dead, you should be such a one","As you are now; for you are cold and stern,","And now you should be as your mother was","When your sweet self was got."],"id":717,"m":"Named after a goddess, and worth it, and more! But, beautiful soul, is there no love at all in your lovely body? If the lively fire of youth doesn't light up your mind, you're not a girl but a statue on a tomb. When you're dead you should be as you are now, because now you're cold and hard. Now you should be as your mother was when your sweet self was conceived."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["She then was honest."],"id":718,"m":"She was chaste then."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["So should you be."],"id":719,"m":"And so you would be."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["No.","My mother did but duty; such, my lord,","As you owe to your wife."],"id":720,"m":"No. My mother was only doing her duty, the same duty, my lord, that you owe your wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["No more a' that!","I pr'ythee do not strive against my vows;","I was compell'd to her; but I love thee","By love's own sweet constraint, and will for ever","Do thee all rights of service."],"id":721,"m":"No more of that! Please don't fight against my vows. I was forced into marrying her, but I love you by love's own sweet compulsion, and I'll always serve you in every way."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Ay, so you serve us","Till we serve you; but when you have our roses,","You barely leave our thorns to prick ourselves,","And mock us with our bareness."],"id":722,"m":"Yes, you serve us like that until we give in to you. But when you've had our roses, you leave us only the thorns to prick ourselves, and mock us for being stripped bare."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["How have I sworn?"],"id":723,"m":"Haven't I sworn it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["'Tis not the many oaths that makes the truth,","But the plain single vow that is vow'd true.","What is not holy, that we swear not by,","But take the highest to witness: then, pray you, tell me,","If I should swear by Jove's great attributes","I lov'd you dearly, would you believe my oaths","When I did love you ill? This has no holding,","To swear by him whom I protest to love","That I will work against him. Therefore your oaths","Are words and poor conditions; but unseal'd,—","At least in my opinion."],"id":724,"m":"It isn't a lot of oaths that makes something true, but one simple vow sincerely made. We don't swear by what is not holy, but call on the highest to witness. Then tell me, please: if I swore by all Jove's great powers that I loved you dearly, would you believe my oaths when I was loving you badly? It makes no sense to swear by God, whom I claim to love, that I'll act against him. So your oaths are just words and empty conditions, unsealed, at least in my opinion."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Change it, change it.","Be not so holy-cruel. Love is holy;","And my integrity ne'er knew the crafts","That you do charge men with. Stand no more off,","But give thyself unto my sick desires,","Who then recovers. Say thou art mine, and ever","My love as it begins shall so persever."],"id":725,"m":"Change your mind, change it. Don't be so holy and so cruel. Love is holy, and my honesty never knew the tricks you accuse men of. Don't hold back any more, but give yourself to my sick desires, and then they'll recover. Say you're mine, and my love will always last as it begins."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I see that men make hopes in such a case,","That we'll forsake ourselves. Give me that ring."],"id":726,"m":"I see that in such a case men hope we'll give ourselves up. Give me that ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I'll lend it thee, my dear, but have no power","To give it from me."],"id":727,"m":"I'll lend it to you, my dear, but I'm not able to give it away."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Will you not, my lord?"],"id":728,"m":"You won't, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["It is an honour 'longing to our house,","Bequeathed down from many ancestors,","Which were the greatest obloquy i' the world","In me to lose."],"id":729,"m":"It's an honour belonging to my family, handed down from many ancestors. It would be the greatest disgrace in the world for me to lose it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Mine honour's such a ring;","My chastity's the jewel of our house,","Bequeathed down from many ancestors,","Which were the greatest obloquy i' the world","In me to lose. Thus your own proper wisdom","Brings in the champion honour on my part","Against your vain assault."],"id":730,"m":"My honour is a ring like that. My chastity is the jewel of my family, handed down from many ancestors. It would be the greatest disgrace in the world for me to lose it. So your own wisdom brings honour in as a champion on my side against your empty attack."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Here, take my ring;","My house, mine honour, yea, my life be thine,","And I'll be bid by thee."],"id":731,"m":"Here, take my ring. My house, my honour, yes, my life are yours, and I'll do whatever you tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["When midnight comes, knock at my chamber window;","I'll order take my mother shall not hear.","Now will I charge you in the band of truth,","When you have conquer'd my yet maiden-bed,","Remain there but an hour, nor speak to me.","My reasons are most strong; and you shall know them","When back again this ring shall be deliver'd;","And on your finger in the night, I'll put","Another ring, that what in time proceeds","May token to the future our past deeds.","Adieu till then; then fail not. You have won","A wife of me, though there my hope be done."],"id":732,"m":"When midnight comes, knock at my bedroom window. I'll make sure my mother doesn't hear. Now I'll bind you by your promise of truth: when you have conquered my still virgin bed, stay only an hour and don't speak to me. My reasons are very strong, and you'll learn them when this ring is given back to you. And in the night I'll put another ring on your finger, so that whatever happens later may be a sign to the future of what we did. Goodbye until then. Don't fail. You've won a wife in me, though my hope ends there."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["A heaven on earth I have won by wooing thee."],"id":733,"m":"I've won a heaven on earth by wooing you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":734},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["For which live long to thank both heaven and me!","You may so in the end.","My mother told me just how he would woo,","As if she sat in's heart. She says all men","Have the like oaths. He had sworn to marry me","When his wife's dead; therefore I'll lie with him","When I am buried. Since Frenchmen are so braid,","Marry that will, I live and die a maid.","Only, in this disguise, I think't no sin","To cozen him that would unjustly win."],"id":735,"m":"May you live long to thank both heaven and me for it! You may do, in the end. My mother told me exactly how he would woo me, as if she were inside his heart. She says all men swear the same oaths. He has sworn to marry me when his wife's dead, so I'll sleep with him when I'm buried. Since Frenchmen are so deceitful, let those who want to marry; I'll live and die a virgin. Only, in this disguise, I don't think it's a sin to cheat a man who would win unfairly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":736},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"The Florentine camp.","id":737},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the two French Lords and two or three Soldiers.","id":738},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["You have not given him his mother's letter?"],"id":739,"m":"You haven't given him his mother's letter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["I have deliv'red it an hour since; there is something in't that stings","his nature; for on the reading it, he chang'd almost into another man."],"id":740,"m":"I delivered it an hour ago. There's something in it that stings him, because when he read it he changed almost into a different man."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["He has much worthy blame laid upon him for shaking off so good a wife","and so sweet a lady."],"id":741,"m":"He deserves a lot of the blame he gets for throwing off so good a wife and so sweet a lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Especially he hath incurred the everlasting displeasure of the king,","who had even tun'd his bounty to sing happiness to him. I will tell you","a thing, but you shall let it dwell darkly with you."],"id":742,"m":"Above all he has earned the King's lasting displeasure, when the King had tuned his generosity to sing happiness to him. I'll tell you something, but you must keep it hidden."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["When you have spoken it, 'tis dead, and I am the grave of it."],"id":743,"m":"Once you've told me, it's dead, and I'm its grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["He hath perverted a young gentlewoman here in Florence, of a most","chaste renown, and this night he fleshes his will in the spoil of her","honour; he hath given her his monumental ring, and thinks himself made","in the unchaste composition."],"id":744,"m":"He has corrupted a young gentlewoman here in Florence with a reputation for great chastity, and tonight he satisfies his lust on the spoils of her honour. He has given her his family ring, and thinks he has made a great bargain in this unchaste deal."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Now, God delay our rebellion! As we are ourselves, what things are we!"],"id":745,"m":"Now God hold back our sinful nature! Left to ourselves, what creatures we are!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Merely our own traitors. And as in the common course of all treasons,","we still see them reveal themselves till they attain to their abhorr'd","ends; so he that in this action contrives against his own nobility, in","his proper stream, o'erflows himself."],"id":746,"m":"Nothing but traitors to ourselves. And just as we always see treasons reveal themselves before they reach their hateful ends, so he who plots against his own nobility in this action overflows his own banks and drowns himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Is it not meant damnable in us to be trumpeters of our unlawful","intents? We shall not then have his company tonight?"],"id":747,"m":"Isn't it damnable of us to trumpet our own unlawful plans? So we won't have his company tonight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Not till after midnight; for he is dieted to his hour."],"id":748,"m":"Not until after midnight, because he's held to his appointment."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["That approaches apace. I would gladly have him see his company","anatomized, that he might take a measure of his own judgments, wherein","so curiously he had set this counterfeit."],"id":749,"m":"That's coming fast. I'd love him to see his companion dissected, so that he can measure his own judgement, in which he set such a high value on this fake."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["We will not meddle with him till he come; for his presence must be the","whip of the other."],"id":750,"m":"We won't touch Parolles until Bertram comes, because his presence must be the whip for the other."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["In the meantime, what hear you of these wars?"],"id":751,"m":"Meanwhile, what do you hear about these wars?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["I hear there is an overture of peace."],"id":752,"m":"I hear there's an offer of peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Nay, I assure you, a peace concluded."],"id":753,"m":"No, I assure you, peace has been agreed."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["What will Count Rossillon do then? Will he travel higher, or return","again into France?"],"id":754,"m":"What will Count Rossillon do then? Will he travel further, or go back to France?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I perceive by this demand, you are not altogether of his council."],"id":755,"m":"I see from your question that you're not entirely in his confidence."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Let it be forbid, sir! So should I be a great deal of his act."],"id":756,"m":"God forbid, sir! Then I'd have a great share in what he does."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Sir, his wife some two months since fled from his house. Her pretence","is a pilgrimage to Saint Jaques le Grand; which holy undertaking with","most austere sanctimony she accomplished; and there residing, the","tenderness of her nature became as a prey to her grief; in fine, made a","groan of her last breath, and now she sings in heaven."],"id":757,"m":"Sir, his wife ran away from his house about two months ago. She claimed to be going on a pilgrimage to Saint Jaques the Great, and she completed that holy journey with the strictest devotion. While she was staying there, her gentle nature fell prey to her grief. In short, she groaned her last breath, and now she sings in heaven."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["How is this justified?"],"id":758,"m":"How is this proved?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["The stronger part of it by her own letters, which makes her story true,","even to the point of her death. Her death itself, which could not be","her office to say is come, was faithfully confirm'd by the rector of","the place."],"id":759,"m":"Most of it by her own letters, which tell her story right up to the point of her death. Her death itself, which she couldn't report herself, was reliably confirmed by the priest of the place."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Hath the count all this intelligence?"],"id":760,"m":"Does the count know all this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Ay, and the particular confirmations, point from point, to the full","arming of the verity."],"id":761,"m":"Yes, and all the detailed proofs, point by point, which fully arm the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["I am heartily sorry that he'll be glad of this."],"id":762,"m":"I'm truly sorry that he'll be glad about this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["How mightily sometimes we make us comforts of our losses!"],"id":763,"m":"How strongly we sometimes make comforts out of our losses!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["And how mightily some other times we drown our gain in tears! The great","dignity that his valour hath here acquir'd for him shall at home be","encountered with a shame as ample."],"id":764,"m":"And how strongly at other times we drown our gains in tears! The great honour his courage has won him here will be met at home with an equally great shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["The web of our life is of a mingled yarn, good and ill together; our","virtues would be proud if our faults whipped them not; and our crimes","would despair if they were not cherish'd by our virtues."],"id":765,"m":"The fabric of our life is woven from mixed thread, good and bad together. Our virtues would be proud if our faults didn't whip them, and our crimes would despair if our virtues didn't comfort them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":766},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","cont":true,"lines":["How now? Where's your master?"],"id":767,"m":"Hello! Where's your master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["He met the duke in the street, sir; of whom he hath taken a solemn","leave: his lordship will next morning for France. The duke hath offered","him letters of commendations to the king."],"id":768,"m":"He met the Duke in the street, sir, and has taken a formal leave of him. His lordship goes to France tomorrow morning. The Duke has offered him letters recommending him to the King."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["They shall be no more than needful there, if they were more than they","can commend."],"id":769,"m":"He'll need them there, even if they praised him more than he deserves."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram.","id":770},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["They cannot be too sweet for the king's tartness. Here's his lordship","now. How now, my lord, is't not after midnight?"],"id":771,"m":"They can't be too sweet for the King's sour mood. Here's his lordship now. Well, my lord, isn't it past midnight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I have tonight despatch'd sixteen businesses, a month's length apiece;","by an abstract of success: I have congied with the duke, done my adieu","with his nearest; buried a wife, mourn'd for her, writ to my lady","mother I am returning, entertained my convoy, and between these main","parcels of despatch effected many nicer needs: the last was the","greatest, but that I have not ended yet."],"id":772,"m":"Tonight I have dealt with sixteen pieces of business, each of which would take a month, listed by how they went. I've taken leave of the Duke, said goodbye to those closest to him, buried a wife, mourned for her, written to my lady mother that I'm coming home, arranged my escort, and between these main items done many smaller ones. The last was the most important, but I haven't finished that yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["If the business be of any difficulty and this morning your departure","hence, it requires haste of your lordship."],"id":773,"m":"If the business is at all difficult and you leave this morning, your lordship will have to hurry."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I mean the business is not ended, as fearing to hear of it hereafter.","But shall we have this dialogue between the Fool and the Soldier? Come,","bring forth this counterfeit module has deceiv'd me like a","double-meaning prophesier."],"id":774,"m":"I mean the business isn't finished, because I'm afraid of hearing about it later. But shall we have this dialogue between the Fool and the Soldier? Come on, bring out this fake model of a soldier who has fooled me like a two-faced prophet."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Bring him forth."],"id":775,"m":"Bring him out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Soldiers.","id":776},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","cont":true,"lines":["Has sat i' the stocks all night, poor gallant knave."],"id":777,"m":"He's sat in the stocks all night, the poor dashing rogue."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["No matter; his heels have deserv'd it, in usurping his spurs so long.","How does he carry himself?"],"id":778,"m":"No matter. His heels have earned it, wearing spurs they had no right to for so long. How is he bearing up?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I have told your lordship already; the stocks carry him. But to answer","you as you would be understood: he weeps like a wench that had shed her","milk; he hath confessed himself to Morgan, whom he supposes to be a","friar, from the time of his remembrance to this very instant disaster","of his setting i' the stocks. And what think you he hath confessed?"],"id":779,"m":"I've already told your lordship: the stocks are bearing him up. But to answer you as you mean it: he's crying like a girl who has spilt her milk. He has confessed to Morgan, whom he thinks is a friar, everything from the earliest time he can remember to this present disaster of being put in the stocks. And what do you think he's confessed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Nothing of me, has he?"],"id":780,"m":"Nothing about me, has he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["His confession is taken, and it shall be read to his face; if your","lordship be in't, as I believe you are, you must have the patience to","hear it."],"id":781,"m":"His confession has been written down, and it will be read to his face. If your lordship is in it, as I believe you are, you must be patient enough to listen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Soldiers with Parolles.","id":782},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["A plague upon him! muffled! he can say nothing of me; hush, hush!"],"id":783,"m":"Damn him! Blindfolded! He can't say anything about me. Hush, hush!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Hoodman comes! Portotartarossa."],"id":784,"m":"Here comes blind man's buff! Portotartarossa."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["He calls for the tortures. What will you say without 'em?"],"id":785,"m":"He's calling for the instruments of torture. What will you say without them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I will confess what I know without constraint. If ye pinch me like a","pasty I can say no more."],"id":786,"m":"I'll confess what I know without being forced. If you squeezed me like a pie, I couldn't say any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Bosko chimurcho."],"id":787,"m":"Bosko chimurcho."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Boblibindo chicurmurco."],"id":788,"m":"Boblibindo chicurmurco."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["You are a merciful general. Our general bids you answer to what I shall","ask you out of a note."],"id":789,"m":"You have a merciful general. Our general orders you to answer what I ask you from a list."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["And truly, as I hope to live."],"id":790,"m":"And truthfully, as I hope to live."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["'First demand of him how many horse the duke is strong.' What say you","to that?"],"id":791,"m":"\"First ask him how many cavalry the Duke has.\" What do you say to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Five or six thousand; but very weak and unserviceable: the troops are","all scattered, and the commanders very poor rogues, upon my reputation","and credit, and as I hope to live."],"id":792,"m":"Five or six thousand, but very weak and useless. The troops are all scattered, and the commanders are very poor rogues, on my reputation and credit, and as I hope to live."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Shall I set down your answer so?"],"id":793,"m":"Shall I write your answer down like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Do. I'll take the sacrament on 't, how and which way you will."],"id":794,"m":"Do. I'll swear it on the sacrament, any way you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["All's one to him. What a past-saving slave is this!"],"id":795,"m":"It's all the same to him. What a hopeless villain this is!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["You are deceived, my lord; this is Monsieur Parolles, the gallant","militarist (that was his own phrase), that had the whole theoric of war","in the knot of his scarf, and the practice in the chape of his dagger."],"id":796,"m":"You're mistaken, my lord. This is Monsieur Parolles, the \"gallant militarist\" (that was his own phrase), who had the whole theory of war in the knot of his scarf and its practice in the tip of his dagger sheath."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["I will never trust a man again for keeping his sword clean, nor believe","he can have everything in him by wearing his apparel neatly."],"id":797,"m":"I'll never trust a man again just because he keeps his sword clean, or believe he has every quality because he dresses neatly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Well, that's set down."],"id":798,"m":"Well, that's written down."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["'Five or six thousand horse' I said—I will say true—or thereabouts, set","down,—for I'll speak truth."],"id":799,"m":"\"Five or six thousand horse,\" I said (I'll tell the truth), \"or thereabouts.\" Write it down, because I'll speak the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["He's very near the truth in this."],"id":800,"m":"He's very near the truth there."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["But I con him no thanks for't in the nature he delivers it."],"id":801,"m":"But I don't thank him for it, the way he tells it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Poor rogues, I pray you say."],"id":802,"m":"\"Poor rogues,\" please write."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Well, that's set down."],"id":803,"m":"Well, that's written down."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I humbly thank you, sir; a truth's a truth, the rogues are marvellous","poor."],"id":804,"m":"Thank you humbly, sir. A truth's a truth: the rogues are amazingly poor."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["'Demand of him of what strength they are a-foot.' What say you to that?"],"id":805,"m":"\"Ask him how strong the infantry is.\" What do you say to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["By my troth, sir, if I were to live this present hour, I will tell","true. Let me see: Spurio, a hundred and fifty, Sebastian, so many;","Corambus, so many; Jaques, so many; Guiltian, Cosmo, Lodowick, and","Gratii, two hundred fifty each; mine own company, Chitopher, Vaumond,","Bentii, two hundred fifty each: so that the muster-file, rotten and","sound, upon my life, amounts not to fifteen thousand poll; half of the","which dare not shake the snow from off their cassocks lest they shake","themselves to pieces."],"id":806,"m":"Honestly, sir, if I had only an hour to live, I'll tell the truth. Let me see: Spurio, a hundred and fifty; Sebastian, the same; Corambus, the same; Jaques, the same; Guiltian, Cosmo, Lodowick and Gratii, two hundred and fifty each; my own company, Chitopher, Vaumond and Bentii, two hundred and fifty each. So the whole muster, rotten and sound, on my life, doesn't come to fifteen thousand heads, and half of them don't dare shake the snow off their cloaks in case they shake themselves to pieces."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["What shall be done to him?"],"id":807,"m":"What shall be done to him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Nothing, but let him have thanks. Demand of him my condition, and what","credit I have with the duke."],"id":808,"m":"Nothing, except thank him. Ask him about me, and what standing I have with the Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Well, that's set down. 'You shall demand of him whether one Captain","Dumaine be i' the camp, a Frenchman; what his reputation is with the","duke, what his valour, honesty and expertness in wars; or whether he","thinks it were not possible with well-weighing sums of gold to corrupt","him to a revolt.' What say you to this? What do you know of it?"],"id":809,"m":"Well, that's written down. \"You shall ask him whether a certain Captain Dumaine, a Frenchman, is in the camp; what his reputation is with the Duke; what his courage, honesty and skill in war are; or whether he thinks it would be possible to bribe him into treason with heavy sums of gold.\" What do you say to this? What do you know about it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I beseech you, let me answer to the particular of the inter'gatories.","Demand them singly."],"id":810,"m":"Please, let me answer each of the questions separately. Ask them one at a time."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Do you know this Captain Dumaine?"],"id":811,"m":"Do you know this Captain Dumaine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I know him: he was a botcher's 'prentice in Paris, from whence he was","whipped for getting the shrieve's fool with child, a dumb innocent that","could not say him nay."],"id":812,"m":"I know him. He was a tailor's apprentice in Paris, and was whipped out of there for getting the sheriff's simple-minded girl pregnant, a poor dumb innocent who couldn't say no to him."},{"t":"sd","text":"First Lord lifts up his hand in anger.","id":813},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Nay, by your leave, hold your hands; though I know his brains are","forfeit to the next tile that falls."],"id":814,"m":"No, if you don't mind, hold your hands, though I know his brains are forfeit to the next tile that falls off a roof."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Well, is this captain in the Duke of Florence's camp?"],"id":815,"m":"Well, is this captain in the Duke of Florence's camp?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Upon my knowledge, he is, and lousy."],"id":816,"m":"To my knowledge he is, and covered in lice."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Nay, look not so upon me; we shall hear of your lordship anon."],"id":817,"m":"No, don't look at me like that. We'll hear about your lordship soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["What is his reputation with the duke?"],"id":818,"m":"What is his reputation with the Duke?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["The duke knows him for no other but a poor officer of mine, and writ to","me this other day to turn him out o' the band. I think I have his","letter in my pocket."],"id":819,"m":"The Duke knows him only as a poor officer of mine, and wrote to me the other day to throw him out of the company. I think I have his letter in my pocket."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Marry, we'll search."],"id":820,"m":"Well, we'll search."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["In good sadness, I do not know; either it is there or it is upon a","file, with the duke's other letters, in my tent."],"id":821,"m":"In all seriousness, I don't know. Either it's there, or it's on a file with the Duke's other letters in my tent."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Here 'tis; here's a paper; shall I read it to you?"],"id":822,"m":"Here it is. Here's a paper. Shall I read it to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I do not know if it be it or no."],"id":823,"m":"I don't know if it's the one or not."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Our interpreter does it well."],"id":824,"m":"Our interpreter is doing it well."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Excellently."],"id":825,"m":"Excellently."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["[Reads] Dian, the Count's a fool, and full of gold."],"id":826,"m":"[Reads] \"Diana, the Count's a fool, and full of gold.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["That is not the duke's letter, sir; that is an advertisement to a","proper maid in Florence, one Diana, to take heed of the allurement of","one Count Rossillon, a foolish idle boy, but for all that very ruttish.","I pray you, sir, put it up again."],"id":827,"m":"That isn't the Duke's letter, sir. That's a warning to a decent girl in Florence called Diana, to watch out for the temptations of a certain Count Rossillon, a foolish, idle boy, but very lustful all the same. Please, sir, put it away again."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Nay, I'll read it first by your favour."],"id":828,"m":"No, I'll read it first, with your permission."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["My meaning in't, I protest, was very honest in the behalf of the maid;","for I knew the young count to be a dangerous and lascivious boy, who is","a whale to virginity, and devours up all the fry it finds."],"id":829,"m":"I swear my purpose in it was very honest, on the girl's behalf, because I knew the young count was a dangerous and lecherous boy, a whale to virginity, who swallows up all the little fish he finds."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Damnable both sides rogue!"],"id":830,"m":"Damned two-faced rogue!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["[Reads]","When he swears oaths, bid him drop gold, and take it;","After he scores, he never pays the score.","Half won is match well made; match, and well make it;","He ne'er pays after-debts, take it before.","And say a soldier, 'Dian,' told thee this:","Men are to mell with, boys are not to kiss;","For count of this, the count's a fool, I know it,","Who pays before, but not when he does owe it.","Thine, as he vow'd to thee in thine ear,","PAROLLES."],"id":831,"m":"[Reads] \"When he swears oaths, tell him to drop gold, and take it. Once he has run up a bill, he never pays it. A bargain half won is a bargain well made; make the bargain, and make it well. He never pays debts afterwards; get paid first. And tell yourself, Diana, a soldier told you this: men are for sleeping with, boys are not to be kissed. Because take note of this: the count's a fool, I know it, who pays in advance but not when he owes. Yours, as he swore to you in your ear, Parolles.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["He shall be whipped through the army with this rhyme in's forehead."],"id":832,"m":"He'll be whipped through the army with this rhyme stuck on his forehead."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["This is your devoted friend, sir, the manifold linguist, and the","armipotent soldier."],"id":833,"m":"This is your devoted friend, sir, the man of many languages and the mighty soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I could endure anything before but a cat, and now he's a cat to me."],"id":834,"m":"I could put up with anything before except a cat, and now he's a cat to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["I perceive, sir, by our general's looks we shall be fain to hang you."],"id":835,"m":"I can see from our general's looks, sir, that we'll have to hang you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["My life, sir, in any case. Not that I am afraid to die, but that, my","offences being many, I would repent out the remainder of nature. Let me","live, sir, in a dungeon, i' the stocks, or anywhere, so I may live."],"id":836,"m":"My life, sir, at any cost! Not that I'm afraid to die, but since my sins are many, I'd like to spend the rest of my natural life repenting. Let me live, sir, in a dungeon, in the stocks, anywhere, as long as I live."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["We'll see what may be done, so you confess freely. Therefore, once more","to this Captain Dumaine: you have answer'd to his reputation with the","duke, and to his valour. What is his honesty?"],"id":837,"m":"We'll see what can be done, as long as you confess freely. So, once more about this Captain Dumaine: you've answered about his reputation with the Duke and his courage. What about his honesty?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["He will steal, sir, an egg out of a cloister: for rapes and ravishments","he parallels Nessus. He professes not keeping of oaths; in breaking","them he is stronger than Hercules. He will lie, sir, with such","volubility that you would think truth were a fool: drunkenness is his","best virtue, for he will be swine-drunk, and in his sleep he does","little harm, save to his bedclothes about him; but they know his","conditions and lay him in straw. I have but little more to say, sir, of","his honesty; he has everything that an honest man should not have; what","an honest man should have, he has nothing."],"id":838,"m":"He would steal an egg out of a monastery, sir. For rape and abduction he's the equal of Nessus. He makes a point of not keeping oaths; in breaking them he's stronger than Hercules. He'll lie, sir, so fluently that you'd think truth was a fool. Drunkenness is his best virtue, because he'll get pig-drunk, and in his sleep he does little harm, except to his bedclothes; but they know his habits and lay him on straw. I have little more to say about his honesty, sir: he has everything an honest man shouldn't have, and nothing an honest man should."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I begin to love him for this."],"id":839,"m":"I'm starting to love him for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["For this description of thine honesty? A pox upon him for me, he's more","and more a cat."],"id":840,"m":"For this description of your honesty? Damn him, as far as I'm concerned he's more and more a cat."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["What say you to his expertness in war?"],"id":841,"m":"What do you say about his skill in war?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Faith, sir, has led the drum before the English tragedians,—to belie","him I will not,—and more of his soldiership I know not, except in that","country he had the honour to be the officer at a place there called","Mile-end, to instruct for the doubling of files. I would do the man","what honour I can, but of this I am not certain."],"id":842,"m":"Honestly, sir, he has led the drum in front of the English touring actors (I won't lie about him), and I know nothing more of his soldiering, except that in that country he had the honour to be the officer at a place called Mile End, teaching men to march in double file. I'd like to give the man what honour I can, but I'm not sure of this."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["He hath out-villain'd villainy so far that the rarity redeems him."],"id":843,"m":"He has outdone villainy so far that his rarity redeems him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["A pox on him! He's a cat still."],"id":844,"m":"Damn him! He's still a cat."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["His qualities being at this poor price, I need not to ask you if gold","will corrupt him to revolt."],"id":845,"m":"Since his qualities are so cheap, I needn't ask you if gold would bribe him into treason."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Sir, for a quart d'ecu he will sell the fee-simple of his salvation,","the inheritance of it, and cut the entail from all remainders, and a","perpetual succession for it perpetually."],"id":846,"m":"Sir, for a quarter of a crown he'd sell the freehold of his salvation, the inheritance of it, cut off all future heirs from it, and sell the right of succession to it for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["What's his brother, the other Captain Dumaine?"],"id":847,"m":"What about his brother, the other Captain Dumaine?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Why does he ask him of me?"],"id":848,"m":"Why is he asking him about me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["What's he?"],"id":849,"m":"What's he like?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["E'en a crow o' the same nest; not altogether so great as the first in","goodness, but greater a great deal in evil. He excels his brother for a","coward, yet his brother is reputed one of the best that is. In a","retreat he outruns any lackey; marry, in coming on he has the cramp."],"id":850,"m":"Just a crow from the same nest. Not quite as great as the first in goodness, but a great deal greater in evil. He beats his brother as a coward, and yet his brother is thought one of the best cowards there is. In a retreat he outruns any footman, but when it comes to advancing he gets cramp."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["If your life be saved, will you undertake to betray the Florentine?"],"id":851,"m":"If your life is saved, will you agree to betray the Duke of Florence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Ay, and the captain of his horse, Count Rossillon."],"id":852,"m":"Yes, and the commander of his cavalry, Count Rossillon."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["I'll whisper with the general, and know his pleasure."],"id":853,"m":"I'll whisper with the general and find out what he wants."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["[Aside] I'll no more drumming; a plague of all drums! Only to seem","to deserve well, and to beguile the supposition of that lascivious","young boy the count, have I run into this danger: yet who would have","suspected an ambush where I was taken?"],"id":854,"m":"[Aside] No more drumming for me. A plague on all drums! Just to seem to deserve well, and to fool that lecherous young boy the count into thinking well of me, I've run into this danger. Yet who would have suspected an ambush where I was caught?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["There is no remedy, sir, but you must die. The general says you that","have so traitorously discovered the secrets of your army, and made such","pestiferous reports of men very nobly held, can serve the world for no","honest use; therefore you must die. Come, headsman, off with his head."],"id":855,"m":"There's no help for it, sir: you must die. The general says that you, who have so treacherously revealed your army's secrets and made such poisonous reports about men of very noble reputation, can't be of any honest use to the world. So you must die. Come, executioner, off with his head."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["O Lord! sir, let me live, or let me see my death."],"id":856,"m":"Oh Lord, sir! Let me live, or let me see my death."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["That shall you, and take your leave of all your friends."],"id":857,"m":"So you shall, and say goodbye to all your friends."},{"t":"sd","text":"Unmuffling him.","id":858},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","cont":true,"lines":["So, look about you; know you any here?"],"id":859,"m":"There, look around you. Do you know anyone here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Good morrow, noble captain."],"id":860,"m":"Good morning, noble captain."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["God bless you, Captain Parolles."],"id":861,"m":"God bless you, Captain Parolles."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["God save you, noble captain."],"id":862,"m":"God save you, noble captain."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Captain, what greeting will you to my Lord Lafew? I am for France."],"id":863,"m":"Captain, what message would you like to send to my Lord Lafew? I'm going to France."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Good Captain, will you give me a copy of the sonnet you writ to Diana","in behalf of the Count Rossillon? And I were not a very coward I'd","compel it of you; but fare you well."],"id":864,"m":"Good captain, will you give me a copy of the poem you wrote to Diana for Count Rossillon? If I weren't such a coward I'd force it out of you. But goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bertram, Lords &c.","id":865},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["You are undone, captain: all but your scarf; that has a knot on't yet."],"id":866,"m":"You're finished, captain, all except your scarf. That still has a knot in it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Who cannot be crushed with a plot?"],"id":867,"m":"Who couldn't be crushed by a plot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["If you could find out a country where but women were that had received","so much shame, you might begin an impudent nation. Fare ye well, sir. I","am for France too; we shall speak of you there."],"id":868,"m":"If you could find a country where only women lived who had suffered so much shame, you could start a shameless nation. Goodbye, sir. I'm going to France too. We'll talk about you there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":869},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Yet am I thankful. If my heart were great","'Twould burst at this. Captain I'll be no more,","But I will eat, and drink, and sleep as soft","As captain shall. Simply the thing I am","Shall make me live. Who knows himself a braggart,","Let him fear this; for it will come to pass","That every braggart shall be found an ass.","Rust, sword; cool, blushes; and, Parolles live","Safest in shame; being fool'd, by foolery thrive.","There's place and means for every man alive.","I'll after them."],"id":870,"m":"Still, I'm thankful. If my heart were proud, this would make it burst. I'll be a captain no more, but I'll eat and drink and sleep as comfortably as any captain. Simply being what I am will keep me alive. Anyone who knows he's a braggart, let him fear this, because it will come to pass that every braggart is found out to be an ass. Rust, sword; cool down, blushes; and, Parolles, live safest in shame. Having been made a fool of, prosper by foolery. There's a place and a living for every man alive. I'll go after them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":871},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"Florence. A room in the Widow's house.","id":872},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Helena, Widow and Diana.","id":873},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["That you may well perceive I have not wrong'd you","One of the greatest in the Christian world","Shall be my surety; fore whose throne 'tis needful,","Ere I can perfect mine intents, to kneel.","Time was I did him a desired office,","Dear almost as his life; which gratitude","Through flinty Tartar's bosom would peep forth,","And answer thanks. I duly am inform'd","His grace is at Marseilles; to which place","We have convenient convoy. You must know","I am supposed dead. The army breaking,","My husband hies him home, where, heaven aiding,","And by the leave of my good lord the king,","We'll be before our welcome."],"id":874,"m":"So that you can see clearly I haven't wronged you, one of the greatest men in the Christian world will guarantee my word. Before I can complete my plans, I must kneel before his throne. Once I did him a service he longed for, almost as dear to him as his life. Gratitude for that would show itself even through the stony heart of a Tartar and answer with thanks. I'm reliably told his majesty is at Marseilles, and we have a convenient escort there. You must know that I'm thought to be dead. Now the army is breaking up, my husband is hurrying home, where, with heaven's help and the permission of my good lord the King, we'll arrive before we're expected."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Gentle madam,","You never had a servant to whose trust","Your business was more welcome."],"id":875,"m":"Gentle madam, you never had a servant who was more glad to be trusted with your business."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Nor you, mistress,","Ever a friend whose thoughts more truly labour","To recompense your love. Doubt not but heaven","Hath brought me up to be your daughter's dower,","As it hath fated her to be my motive","And helper to a husband. But, O strange men!","That can such sweet use make of what they hate,","When saucy trusting of the cozen'd thoughts","Defiles the pitchy night; so lust doth play","With what it loathes, for that which is away.","But more of this hereafter. You, Diana,","Under my poor instructions yet must suffer","Something in my behalf."],"id":876,"m":"Nor, mistress, did you ever have a friend whose thoughts worked harder to repay your love. Don't doubt that heaven has raised me up to provide your daughter's dowry, just as it has destined her to be my means and my helper to a husband. But oh, strange men, who can make such sweet use of what they hate, when the lustful trust of their deceived thoughts stains the pitch-black night! So lust plays with what it loathes, in place of what is absent. But more of this later. You, Diana, must still put up with something more for my sake, under my poor instructions."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Let death and honesty","Go with your impositions, I am yours","Upon your will to suffer."],"id":877,"m":"As long as death and chastity go along with what you ask of me, I'm yours to suffer as you wish."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Yet, I pray you;","But with the word the time will bring on summer,","When briers shall have leaves as well as thorns,","And be as sweet as sharp. We must away;","Our waggon is prepar'd, and time revives us.","All's well that ends well; still the fine's the crown.","Whate'er the course, the end is the renown."],"id":878,"m":"Wait a little more, I beg you. With time, summer will come, when briars will have leaves as well as thorns, and be as sweet as they are sharp. We must go. Our carriage is ready, and time calls us on. All's well that ends well; the ending is always the crown. Whatever the route, it's the end that brings the glory."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":879},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"Rossillon. A room in the Countess's palace.","id":880},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Clown, Countess and Lafew.","id":881},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["No, no, no, your son was misled with a snipt-taffeta fellow there,","whose villanous saffron would have made all the unbak'd and doughy","youth of a nation in his colour. Your daughter-in-law had been alive at","this hour, and your son here at home, more advanc'd by the king than by","that red-tail'd humble-bee I speak of."],"id":882,"m":"No, no, no, your son was led astray by a fellow in slashed silk over there, whose wicked yellow would have dyed all the half-baked, doughy young men of a nation his colour. Your daughter-in-law would be alive at this moment, and your son here at home, promoted further by the King, if it weren't for that red-tailed bumblebee I'm talking about."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["I would I had not known him; it was the death of the most virtuous","gentlewoman that ever nature had praise for creating. If she had","partaken of my flesh and cost me the dearest groans of a mother, I","could not have owed her a more rooted love."],"id":883,"m":"I wish I'd never known him. He was the death of the most virtuous young woman nature was ever praised for creating. If she had been my own flesh and blood and cost me a mother's dearest groans in childbirth, I couldn't have loved her more deeply."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["'Twas a good lady, 'twas a good lady. We may pick a thousand salads ere","we light on such another herb."],"id":884,"m":"She was a good lady, she was a good lady. We could pick a thousand salads before we came across another herb like her."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Indeed, sir, she was the sweet marjoram of the salad, or, rather, the","herb of grace."],"id":885,"m":"Indeed, sir, she was the sweet marjoram of the salad, or rather the herb of grace."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["They are not herbs, you knave; they are nose-herbs."],"id":886,"m":"They're not salad herbs, you rogue; they're herbs for smelling."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I am no great Nebuchadnezzar, sir; I have not much skill in grass."],"id":887,"m":"I'm no great Nebuchadnezzar, sir. I don't know much about grass."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Whether dost thou profess thyself,—a knave or a fool?"],"id":888,"m":"Which do you claim to be, a rogue or a fool?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["A fool, sir, at a woman's service, and a knave at a man's."],"id":889,"m":"A fool, sir, in a woman's service, and a rogue in a man's."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Your distinction?"],"id":890,"m":"What's the difference?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I would cozen the man of his wife, and do his service."],"id":891,"m":"I'd cheat the man of his wife, and do his job for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["So you were a knave at his service indeed."],"id":892,"m":"Then you'd be a rogue in his service indeed."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["And I would give his wife my bauble, sir, to do her service."],"id":893,"m":"And I'd give his wife my fool's stick, sir, to do her service."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I will subscribe for thee; thou art both knave and fool."],"id":894,"m":"I'll vouch for you: you're both rogue and fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["At your service."],"id":895,"m":"At your service."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["No, no, no."],"id":896,"m":"No, no, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Why, sir, if I cannot serve you, I can serve as great a prince as you","are."],"id":897,"m":"Why, sir, if I can't serve you, I can serve as great a prince as you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Who's that? a Frenchman?"],"id":898,"m":"Who's that? A Frenchman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Faith, sir, 'a has an English name; but his phisnomy is more hotter in","France than there."],"id":899,"m":"Well, sir, he has an English name, but his face burns hotter in France than in England."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["What prince is that?"],"id":900,"m":"What prince is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["The black prince, sir; alias the prince of darkness; alias the devil."],"id":901,"m":"The Black Prince, sir, otherwise known as the Prince of Darkness, otherwise known as the devil."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Hold thee, there's my purse. I give thee not this to suggest thee from","thy master thou talk'st of; serve him still."],"id":902,"m":"Wait, here's my purse. I'm not giving you this to tempt you away from that master you talk about. Go on serving him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["I am a woodland fellow, sir, that always loved a great fire, and the","master I speak of ever keeps a good fire. But sure he is the prince of","the world; let his nobility remain in's court. I am for the house with","the narrow gate, which I take to be too little for pomp to enter: some","that humble themselves may, but the many will be too chill and tender,","and they'll be for the flow'ry way that leads to the broad gate and the","great fire."],"id":903,"m":"I'm a country fellow, sir, who always loved a big fire, and the master I'm talking about always keeps a good fire. But surely he's the prince of this world; let his nobles stay at his court. I'm for the house with the narrow gate, which I think is too small for pomp to get through. Some who humble themselves may, but most will be too chilly and soft, and they'll go for the flowery path that leads to the wide gate and the great fire."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Go thy ways, I begin to be a-weary of thee; and I tell thee so before,","because I would not fall out with thee. Go thy ways; let my horses be","well look'd to, without any tricks."],"id":904,"m":"Off you go. I'm beginning to get tired of you, and I'm telling you so in advance because I don't want to quarrel with you. Off you go, and make sure my horses are well looked after, without any tricks."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["If I put any tricks upon 'em, sir, they shall be jades' tricks, which","are their own right by the law of nature."],"id":905,"m":"If I play any tricks on them, sir, they'll be horse tricks, which they have a right to by the law of nature."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":906},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["A shrewd knave, and an unhappy."],"id":907,"m":"A sharp rogue, and a mischievous one."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["So he is. My lord that's gone made himself much sport out of him; by","his authority he remains here, which he thinks is a patent for his","sauciness; and indeed he has no pace, but runs where he will."],"id":908,"m":"So he is. My late husband used to have great fun with him. He stays here because my husband wanted it, and he thinks that gives him a licence for his cheek. In fact he keeps no proper pace, but runs wherever he likes."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I like him well; 'tis not amiss. And I was about to tell you, since I","heard of the good lady's death, and that my lord your son was upon his","return home, I moved the king my master to speak in the behalf of my","daughter; which, in the minority of them both, his majesty out of a","self-gracious remembrance did first propose. His highness hath promis'd","me to do it; and, to stop up the displeasure he hath conceived against","your son, there is no fitter matter. How does your ladyship like it?"],"id":909,"m":"I like him very much; there's no harm in him. I was about to tell you: since I heard of the good lady's death, and that my lord your son was on his way home, I asked the King my master to speak on behalf of my daughter, a match his majesty himself first proposed, out of his own kindness, when they were both children. His highness has promised me to do it, and there's no better way to settle the displeasure he has felt against your son. How does your ladyship like it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["With very much content, my lord, and I wish it happily effected."],"id":910,"m":"Very much, my lord, and I hope it happily comes about."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["His highness comes post from Marseilles, of as able body as when he","number'd thirty; he will be here tomorrow, or I am deceived by him that","in such intelligence hath seldom fail'd."],"id":911,"m":"His highness is riding fast from Marseilles, as healthy as when he was thirty. He'll be here tomorrow, or I'm misled by a man who has rarely been wrong in such news."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["It rejoices me that I hope I shall see him ere I die. I have letters","that my son will be here tonight. I shall beseech your lordship to","remain with me till they meet together."],"id":912,"m":"I'm glad that I may hope to see him before I die. I have letters saying my son will be here tonight. I beg your lordship to stay with me until they meet."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Madam, I was thinking with what manners I might safely be admitted."],"id":913,"m":"Madam, I was wondering how I could politely be allowed to stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["You need but plead your honourable privilege."],"id":914,"m":"You need only claim your honourable privilege."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Lady, of that I have made a bold charter; but, I thank my God, it holds","yet."],"id":915,"m":"Lady, I've made bold use of that privilege, but, thank God, it still holds."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Clown.","id":916},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["O madam, yonder's my lord your son with a patch of velvet on's face;","whether there be a scar under't or no, the velvet knows; but 'tis a","goodly patch of velvet. His left cheek is a cheek of two pile and a","half, but his right cheek is worn bare."],"id":917,"m":"Oh, madam, there's my lord your son over there with a patch of velvet on his face. Whether there's a scar under it or not, the velvet knows, but it's a fine patch of velvet. His left cheek is two-and-a-half-pile velvet, but his right cheek is worn bare."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["A scar nobly got, or a noble scar, is a good livery of honour; so","belike is that."],"id":918,"m":"A scar nobly won, or a noble scar, is a fine uniform of honour, and that's probably one."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["But it is your carbonado'd face."],"id":919,"m":"But it's a face slashed like a grilled steak."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Let us go see your son, I pray you. I long to talk with the young noble","soldier."],"id":920,"m":"Let's go and see your son, please. I long to talk with the noble young soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Faith, there's a dozen of 'em, with delicate fine hats, and most","courteous feathers, which bow the head and nod at every man."],"id":921,"m":"Honestly, there are a dozen of them, with delicate fine hats and the politest feathers, which bow their heads and nod at everyone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":922},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Marseilles. A street.","id":923},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Helena, Widow and Diana with two Attendants.","id":924},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["But this exceeding posting day and night","Must wear your spirits low. We cannot help it.","But since you have made the days and nights as one,","To wear your gentle limbs in my affairs,","Be bold you do so grow in my requital","As nothing can unroot you. In happy time;—"],"id":925,"m":"But this extreme rushing day and night must be wearing you out. We can't help it. But since you have made days and nights the same, wearing out your gentle bodies in my affairs, be sure you are growing so firmly in my gratitude that nothing can uproot you. Just at the right moment..."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Gentleman.","id":926},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","cont":true,"lines":["This man may help me to his majesty's ear,","If he would spend his power. God save you, sir."],"id":927,"m":"This man may help me to get the King to listen, if he would use his influence. God save you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["And you."],"id":928,"m":"And you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Sir, I have seen you in the court of France."],"id":929,"m":"Sir, I have seen you at the French court."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["I have been sometimes there."],"id":930,"m":"I've been there sometimes."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I do presume, sir, that you are not fallen","From the report that goes upon your goodness;","And therefore, goaded with most sharp occasions,","Which lay nice manners by, I put you to","The use of your own virtues, for the which","I shall continue thankful."],"id":931,"m":"I take it, sir, that you haven't fallen short of your reputation for goodness. So, driven by very urgent circumstances that push aside polite manners, I call on you to use your own virtues, and I'll be grateful to you for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["What's your will?"],"id":932,"m":"What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["That it will please you","To give this poor petition to the king,","And aid me with that store of power you have","To come into his presence."],"id":933,"m":"That you will be kind enough to give this humble petition to the King, and use what influence you have to help me come before him."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["The king's not here."],"id":934,"m":"The King isn't here."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["Not here, sir?"],"id":935,"m":"Not here, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["Not indeed.","He hence remov'd last night, and with more haste","Than is his use."],"id":936,"m":"Not indeed. He left here last night, and in more of a hurry than usual."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Lord, how we lose our pains!"],"id":937,"m":"Lord, how our efforts are wasted!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["All's well that ends well yet,","Though time seem so adverse and means unfit.","I do beseech you, whither is he gone?"],"id":938,"m":"All's well that ends well still, though time seems so against us and our means so unsuitable. Please, where has he gone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["Marry, as I take it, to Rossillon;","Whither I am going."],"id":939,"m":"Well, as I understand it, to Rossillon, where I'm going."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["I do beseech you, sir,","Since you are like to see the king before me,","Commend the paper to his gracious hand,","Which I presume shall render you no blame,","But rather make you thank your pains for it.","I will come after you with what good speed","Our means will make us means."],"id":940,"m":"I beg you, sir, since you are likely to see the King before me, put this paper into his gracious hand. I'm sure it will bring you no blame, but rather make you glad you took the trouble. I'll follow you as fast as our means allow."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["This I'll do for you."],"id":941,"m":"I'll do this for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["And you shall find yourself to be well thank'd,","Whate'er falls more. We must to horse again.","Go, go, provide."],"id":942,"m":"And you'll find yourself well thanked, whatever else happens. We must get on our horses again. Go, go, get ready."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":943},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Rossillon. The inner court of the Countess's palace.","id":944},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Clown and Parolles.","id":945},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Good Monsieur Lavache, give my Lord Lafew this letter; I have ere now,","sir, been better known to you, when I have held familiarity with","fresher clothes; but I am now, sir, muddied in Fortune's mood, and","smell somewhat strong of her strong displeasure."],"id":946,"m":"Good Monsieur Lavatch, give my Lord Lafew this letter. In the past, sir, you knew me better, when I wore fresher clothes. But now, sir, I've been muddied in Fortune's moat, and smell rather strongly of her strong displeasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Truly, Fortune's displeasure is but sluttish, if it smell so strongly","as thou speak'st of. I will henceforth eat no fish of Fortune's","buttering. Pr'ythee, allow the wind."],"id":947,"m":"Honestly, Fortune's displeasure must be filthy if it smells as strongly as you say. From now on I'll eat no fish cooked in Fortune's butter. Please, stand downwind."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Nay, you need not to stop your nose, sir. I spake but by a metaphor."],"id":948,"m":"No, you needn't hold your nose, sir. I was only speaking in a metaphor."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Indeed, sir, if your metaphor stink, I will stop my nose, or against","any man's metaphor. Pr'ythee, get thee further."],"id":949,"m":"Indeed, sir, if your metaphor stinks, I'll hold my nose, as I would against any man's metaphor. Please, get further away."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Pray you, sir, deliver me this paper."],"id":950,"m":"Please, sir, deliver this paper for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","lines":["Foh, pr'ythee stand away. A paper from Fortune's close-stool to give to","a nobleman! Look here he comes himself."],"id":951,"m":"Pah! Please, stand back. A paper from Fortune's toilet to give to a nobleman! Look, here he comes himself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lafew.","id":952},{"t":"sp","who":"CLOWN","cont":true,"lines":["Here is a pur of Fortune's, sir, or of Fortune's cat, but not a","musk-cat, that has fallen into the unclean fishpond of her displeasure,","and as he says, is muddied withal. Pray you, sir, use the carp as you","may, for he looks like a poor, decayed, ingenious, foolish, rascally","knave. I do pity his distress in my similes of comfort, and leave him","to your lordship."],"id":953,"m":"Here's one of Fortune's purrs, sir, or one of Fortune's cats (but not a musk-cat), who has fallen into the dirty fishpond of her displeasure and, as he says, is muddied with it. Please, sir, treat this carp as you can, because he looks like a poor, ruined, clever, foolish, rascally rogue. I pity his distress with my comforting comparisons, and leave him to your lordship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":954},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["My lord, I am a man whom Fortune hath cruelly scratch'd."],"id":955,"m":"My lord, I am a man whom Fortune has cruelly scratched."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["And what would you have me to do? 'Tis too late to pare her nails now.","Wherein have you played the knave with Fortune that she should scratch","you, who of herself is a good lady, and would not have knaves thrive","long under her? There's a quart d'ecu for you. Let the justices make","you and Fortune friends; I am for other business."],"id":956,"m":"And what do you want me to do? It's too late to cut her nails now. How have you played the rogue with Fortune that she should scratch you, when she's a good lady herself and won't let rogues prosper under her for long? There's a quarter crown for you. Let the magistrates make you and Fortune friends; I have other business."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I beseech your honour to hear me one single word."],"id":957,"m":"I beg your honour to hear me say one single word."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["You beg a single penny more. Come, you shall ha't; save your word."],"id":958,"m":"You're begging one single penny more. Come on, you shall have it; save your word."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["My name, my good lord, is Parolles."],"id":959,"m":"My name, my good lord, is Parolles."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["You beg more than word then. Cox my passion! Give me your hand. How","does your drum?"],"id":960,"m":"Then you're begging for more than one word. God's passion! Give me your hand. How's your drum?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["O my good lord, you were the first that found me."],"id":961,"m":"Oh, my good lord, you were the first who found me out."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Was I, in sooth? And I was the first that lost thee."],"id":962,"m":"Was I, really? And I was the first who lost you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["It lies in you, my lord, to bring me in some grace, for you did bring","me out."],"id":963,"m":"It's in your power, my lord, to bring me back into favour, since you brought me out of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Out upon thee, knave! dost thou put upon me at once both the office of","God and the devil? One brings thee in grace, and the other brings thee","out."],"id":964,"m":"Shame on you, rogue! Do you give me the jobs of both God and the devil at once? One brings you into grace, and the other brings you out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets sound.","id":965},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","cont":true,"lines":["The king's coming; I know by his trumpets. Sirrah, inquire further","after me. I had talk of you last night; though you are a fool and a","knave, you shall eat. Go to; follow."],"id":966,"m":"The King's coming; I can tell by his trumpets. Fellow, come and see me later. I was talking about you last night. Though you're a fool and a rogue, you shall eat. Go on, follow me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I praise God for you."],"id":967,"m":"I praise God for you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":968},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"The same. A room in the Countess's palace.","id":969},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter King, Countess, Lafew, Lords, Gentlemen, Guards &c.","id":970},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["We lost a jewel of her, and our esteem","Was made much poorer by it; but your son,","As mad in folly, lack'd the sense to know","Her estimation home."],"id":971,"m":"We lost a jewel in her, and our worth was made much poorer by it. But your son, mad in his folly, didn't have the sense to understand her full value."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["'Tis past, my liege,","And I beseech your majesty to make it","Natural rebellion, done i' the blaze of youth,","When oil and fire, too strong for reason's force,","O'erbears it and burns on."],"id":972,"m":"It's past, my lord, and I beg your majesty to treat it as natural rebellion, done in the heat of youth, when passion, like oil and fire, is too strong for reason, overpowers it and burns on."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["My honour'd lady,","I have forgiven and forgotten all,","Though my revenges were high bent upon him,","And watch'd the time to shoot."],"id":973,"m":"My honoured lady, I have forgiven and forgotten everything, though my revenge was aimed high at him and waited for the moment to shoot."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["This I must say,—","But first, I beg my pardon,—the young lord","Did to his majesty, his mother, and his lady,","Offence of mighty note; but to himself","The greatest wrong of all. He lost a wife","Whose beauty did astonish the survey","Of richest eyes; whose words all ears took captive;","Whose dear perfection hearts that scorn'd to serve","Humbly call'd mistress."],"id":974,"m":"I must say this (but first I beg your pardon): the young lord did great wrong to his majesty, his mother and his wife, but the greatest wrong of all to himself. He lost a wife whose beauty amazed the most experienced eyes, whose words captivated every ear, and whose precious perfection made hearts too proud to serve humbly call her mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Praising what is lost","Makes the remembrance dear. Well, call him hither;","We are reconcil'd, and the first view shall kill","All repetition. Let him not ask our pardon;","The nature of his great offence is dead,","And deeper than oblivion do we bury","Th' incensing relics of it. Let him approach","A stranger, no offender; and inform him","So 'tis our will he should."],"id":975,"m":"Praising what is lost makes the memory precious. Well, call him here. We are reconciled, and the first sight of him will put an end to going over it again. He mustn't ask our pardon. His great offence is dead, and we bury its angering remains deeper than forgetfulness. Let him come to us as a stranger, not an offender, and tell him that is our wish."},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["I shall, my liege."],"id":976,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Gentleman.","id":977},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What says he to your daughter? Have you spoke?"],"id":978,"m":"What does he say about your daughter? Have you spoken?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["All that he is hath reference to your highness."],"id":979,"m":"Everything about him is at your highness's command."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Then shall we have a match. I have letters sent me","That sets him high in fame."],"id":980,"m":"Then we'll have a match. I've been sent letters that speak very highly of him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram.","id":981},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["He looks well on 't."],"id":982,"m":"He looks well on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I am not a day of season,","For thou mayst see a sunshine and a hail","In me at once. But to the brightest beams","Distracted clouds give way; so stand thou forth;","The time is fair again."],"id":983,"m":"I'm not a day of one season, because you can see sunshine and hail in me at once. But scattered clouds give way to the brightest rays, so step forward. The weather is fine again."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["My high-repented blames","Dear sovereign, pardon to me."],"id":984,"m":"Dear sovereign, forgive me the faults I deeply repent."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["All is whole.","Not one word more of the consumed time.","Let's take the instant by the forward top;","For we are old, and on our quick'st decrees","Th'inaudible and noiseless foot of time","Steals ere we can effect them. You remember","The daughter of this lord?"],"id":985,"m":"All is mended. Not one more word about the time that's gone. Let's seize the moment by the forelock, because we are old, and the silent, noiseless foot of time creeps up on our quickest decisions before we can carry them out. You remember this lord's daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Admiringly, my liege. At first","I stuck my choice upon her, ere my heart","Durst make too bold herald of my tongue:","Where the impression of mine eye infixing,","Contempt his scornful perspective did lend me,","Which warp'd the line of every other favour,","Scorn'd a fair colour, or express'd it stolen,","Extended or contracted all proportions","To a most hideous object. Thence it came","That she whom all men prais'd, and whom myself,","Since I have lost, have lov'd, was in mine eye","The dust that did offend it."],"id":986,"m":"With admiration, my lord. I fixed my choice on her at first, before my heart dared send my tongue out as too bold a messenger. When her image was fixed in my eye, Contempt lent me his scornful lens, which twisted the shape of every other face, scorned a fair complexion or claimed it was painted, and stretched or shrank all proportions into a hideous object. That's how it came about that she whom all men praised, and whom I myself have loved since I lost her, was to my eye the speck of dust that hurt it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Well excus'd:","That thou didst love her, strikes some scores away","From the great compt: but love that comes too late,","Like a remorseful pardon slowly carried,","To the great sender turns a sour offence,","Crying, That's good that's gone. Our rash faults","Make trivial price of serious things we have,","Not knowing them until we know their grave.","Oft our displeasures, to ourselves unjust,","Destroy our friends, and after weep their dust:","Our own love waking cries to see what's done,","While shameful hate sleeps out the afternoon.","Be this sweet Helen's knell, and now forget her.","Send forth your amorous token for fair Maudlin.","The main consents are had, and here we'll stay","To see our widower's second marriage-day."],"id":987,"m":"Well excused. That you did love her cancels some of the great debt. But love that comes too late, like a pardon brought slowly and with regret, turns into a bitter offence against the great one who sent it, crying, \"What's gone was good.\" Our rash faults put a low price on the serious things we have, not knowing their worth until we know their grave. Often our anger, unjust to ourselves, destroys our friends, and afterwards we weep over their dust. Our own love wakes and weeps to see what's been done, while shameful hate sleeps through the afternoon. Let this be sweet Helen's funeral bell, and now forget her. Send your love token to fair Maudlin. The main agreements have been made, and we'll stay here to see our widower's second wedding day."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Which better than the first, O dear heaven, bless!","Or, ere they meet, in me, O nature, cesse!"],"id":988,"m":"Dear heaven, bless it better than the first! Or, before they come together, let nature stop in me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Come on, my son, in whom my house's name","Must be digested; give a favour from you,","To sparkle in the spirits of my daughter,","That she may quickly come."],"id":989,"m":"Come on, my son, into whom my family's name must be absorbed. Give a love token from you to sparkle in my daughter's spirits, so that she comes quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Bertram gives a ring to Lafew.","id":990},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","cont":true,"lines":["By my old beard,","And ev'ry hair that's on 't, Helen that's dead","Was a sweet creature: such a ring as this,","The last that e'er I took her leave at court,","I saw upon her finger."],"id":991,"m":"By my old beard and every hair on it, Helen, who is dead, was a sweet creature. I saw a ring like this on her finger the last time I said goodbye to her at court."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Hers it was not."],"id":992,"m":"It wasn't hers."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Now, pray you, let me see it; for mine eye,","While I was speaking, oft was fasten'd to it.","This ring was mine; and when I gave it Helen","I bade her, if her fortunes ever stood","Necessitied to help, that by this token","I would relieve her. Had you that craft to 'reave her","Of what should stead her most?"],"id":993,"m":"Now, please let me see it, because while I was speaking my eye was often fixed on it. This ring was mine, and when I gave it to Helen I told her that if she ever needed help, I would help her if she sent this token. Were you so cunning as to rob her of what would help her most?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["My gracious sovereign,","Howe'er it pleases you to take it so,","The ring was never hers."],"id":994,"m":"My gracious sovereign, however you choose to take it, the ring was never hers."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Son, on my life,","I have seen her wear it; and she reckon'd it","At her life's rate."],"id":995,"m":"Son, on my life, I have seen her wear it, and she valued it as highly as her life."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I am sure I saw her wear it."],"id":996,"m":"I'm sure I saw her wear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["You are deceiv'd, my lord; she never saw it.","In Florence was it from a casement thrown me,","Wrapp'd in a paper, which contain'd the name","Of her that threw it. Noble she was, and thought","I stood engag'd; but when I had subscrib'd","To mine own fortune, and inform'd her fully","I could not answer in that course of honour","As she had made the overture, she ceas'd,","In heavy satisfaction, and would never","Receive the ring again."],"id":997,"m":"You're mistaken, my lord: she never saw it. In Florence it was thrown to me from a window, wrapped in a paper with the name of the woman who threw it. She was noble, and thought I was free to marry. But when I had explained my situation and told her fully that I couldn't respond honourably to the offer she had made, she gave up, sadly accepting it, and would never take the ring back."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Plutus himself,","That knows the tinct and multiplying medicine,","Hath not in nature's mystery more science","Than I have in this ring. 'Twas mine, 'twas Helen's,","Whoever gave it you. Then if you know","That you are well acquainted with yourself,","Confess 'twas hers, and by what rough enforcement","You got it from her. She call'd the saints to surety","That she would never put it from her finger","Unless she gave it to yourself in bed,","Where you have never come, or sent it us","Upon her great disaster."],"id":998,"m":"Plutus himself, who knows the secret of turning metal to gold, doesn't understand nature's mysteries better than I know this ring. It was mine, it was Helen's, whoever gave it to you. So if you know yourself at all, confess that it was hers, and by what brutal force you got it from her. She called on the saints to witness that she would never take it off her finger unless she gave it to you yourself in bed (where you have never been), or sent it to us in some great disaster."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["She never saw it."],"id":999,"m":"She never saw it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thou speak'st it falsely, as I love mine honour,","And mak'st conjectural fears to come into me","Which I would fain shut out. If it should prove","That thou art so inhuman,—'twill not prove so:","And yet I know not, thou didst hate her deadly.","And she is dead; which nothing but to close","Her eyes myself, could win me to believe","More than to see this ring. Take him away."],"id":1000,"m":"You're lying, as surely as I love my honour, and you put fearful suspicions into my mind that I'd gladly shut out. If it should turn out that you are so inhuman... it won't turn out like that. And yet I don't know: you hated her mortally. And she is dead, and nothing but closing her eyes myself could convince me of it more than seeing this ring. Take him away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Guards seize Bertram.","id":1001},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":true,"lines":["My fore-past proofs, howe'er the matter fall,","Shall tax my fears of little vanity,","Having vainly fear'd too little. Away with him.","We'll sift this matter further."],"id":1002,"m":"My earlier evidence, however this turns out, will clear my fears of being foolish, since I was foolish to fear too little. Away with him. We'll look into this further."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["If you shall prove","This ring was ever hers, you shall as easy","Prove that I husbanded her bed in Florence,","Where she yet never was."],"id":1003,"m":"If you can prove this ring was ever hers, you can just as easily prove that I slept with her as her husband in Florence, where she never was."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, guarded.","id":1004},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I am wrapp'd in dismal thinkings."],"id":1005,"m":"I'm wrapped in dark thoughts."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Gentleman.","id":1006},{"t":"sp","who":"GENTLEMAN","lines":["Gracious sovereign,","Whether I have been to blame or no, I know not:","Here's a petition from a Florentine,","Who hath for four or five removes come short","To tender it herself. I undertook it,","Vanquish'd thereto by the fair grace and speech","Of the poor suppliant, who by this, I know,","Is here attending: her business looks in her","With an importing visage, and she told me","In a sweet verbal brief, it did concern","Your highness with herself."],"id":1007,"m":"Gracious sovereign, I don't know whether I've done wrong or not. Here's a petition from a Florentine woman who has missed you at four or five stops on the way and so couldn't hand it to you herself. I took it on, won over by the poor petitioner's grace and speech. I know she is here by now, waiting. Her business shows in her face as something urgent, and she told me in a sweet short speech that it concerned your highness and herself."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["[Reads] Upon his many protestations to marry me when his wife was","dead, I blush to say it, he won me. Now is the Count Rossillon a","widower; his vows are forfeited to me, and my honour's paid to him. He","stole from Florence, taking no leave, and I follow him to his country","for justice. Grant it me, O king, in you it best lies; otherwise a","seducer flourishes, and a poor maid is undone.","DIANA CAPILET."],"id":1008,"m":"[Reads] \"Because he swore so many times to marry me when his wife was dead, I blush to say it, he won me. Now the Count of Rossillon is a widower: his vows are owed to me, and my honour has been paid to him. He slipped away from Florence without saying goodbye, and I follow him to his country for justice. Grant it to me, O King; it is most in your power. Otherwise a seducer prospers and a poor girl is ruined. Diana Capilet.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I will buy me a son-in-law in a fair, and toll for this. I'll none of","him."],"id":1009,"m":"I'll buy myself a son-in-law at a fair and pay the market toll for him. I'll have none of this one."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The heavens have thought well on thee, Lafew,","To bring forth this discovery. Seek these suitors.","Go speedily, and bring again the count."],"id":1010,"m":"The heavens have favoured you, Lafew, by bringing this discovery to light. Find these petitioners. Go quickly, and bring the count back again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gentleman and some Attendants.","id":1011},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","cont":true,"lines":["I am afeard the life of Helen, lady,","Was foully snatch'd."],"id":1012,"m":"I'm afraid, lady, that Helen's life was foully taken."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["Now, justice on the doers!"],"id":1013,"m":"Then justice on those who did it!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bertram, guarded.","id":1014},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I wonder, sir, since wives are monsters to you,","And that you fly them as you swear them lordship,","Yet you desire to marry. What woman's that?"],"id":1015,"m":"I wonder, sir, since wives are monsters to you and you run away from them as soon as you swear to be their husband, that you still want to marry. Who is that woman?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Widow and Diana.","id":1016},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I am, my lord, a wretched Florentine,","Derived from the ancient Capilet;","My suit, as I do understand, you know,","And therefore know how far I may be pitied."],"id":1017,"m":"I am, my lord, a wretched Florentine, descended from the ancient Capilet family. I understand you know what I'm asking for, and so you know how much I deserve pity."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["I am her mother, sir, whose age and honour","Both suffer under this complaint we bring,","And both shall cease, without your remedy."],"id":1018,"m":"I'm her mother, sir. My age and my honour both suffer from this complaint we bring, and both will die unless you put it right."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Come hither, count; do you know these women?"],"id":1019,"m":"Come here, Count. Do you know these women?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["My lord, I neither can nor will deny","But that I know them. Do they charge me further?"],"id":1020,"m":"My lord, I can't and won't deny that I know them. Do they accuse me of anything more?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Why do you look so strange upon your wife?"],"id":1021,"m":"Why do you look at your wife as if she were a stranger?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["She's none of mine, my lord."],"id":1022,"m":"She's no wife of mine, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["If you shall marry,","You give away this hand, and that is mine,","You give away heaven's vows, and those are mine,","You give away myself, which is known mine;","For I by vow am so embodied yours","That she which marries you must marry me,","Either both or none."],"id":1023,"m":"If you marry, you give away this hand, and that is mine; you give away vows made to heaven, and those are mine; you give away me, who am known to be yours. Because by your vow I am so made one with you that whoever marries you must marry me: both of us or neither."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["[To Bertram] Your reputation comes too short for my daughter; you are","no husband for her."],"id":1024,"m":"[To Bertram] Your reputation falls too short for my daughter. You're no husband for her."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["My lord, this is a fond and desperate creature","Whom sometime I have laugh'd with. Let your highness","Lay a more noble thought upon mine honour","Than for to think that I would sink it here."],"id":1025,"m":"My lord, this is a foolish, desperate creature I've sometimes laughed with. Let your highness think more nobly of my honour than to believe I'd sink it so low."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Sir, for my thoughts, you have them ill to friend","Till your deeds gain them; fairer prove your honour","Than in my thought it lies!"],"id":1026,"m":"Sir, as for my thoughts, they won't be your friends until your actions win them. May your honour prove better than I now think it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Good my lord,","Ask him upon his oath, if he does think","He had not my virginity."],"id":1027,"m":"My good lord, ask him on his oath whether he doesn't think he took my virginity."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What say'st thou to her?"],"id":1028,"m":"What do you say to her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["She's impudent, my lord,","And was a common gamester to the camp."],"id":1029,"m":"She's shameless, my lord, and was a common whore to the army."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["He does me wrong, my lord; if I were so","He might have bought me at a common price.","Do not believe him. O, behold this ring,","Whose high respect and rich validity","Did lack a parallel; yet for all that","He gave it to a commoner o' the camp,","If I be one."],"id":1030,"m":"He wrongs me, my lord. If I were, he could have bought me at the common price. Don't believe him. Oh, look at this ring, whose high value and rich worth had no equal. And yet he gave it to a common woman of the camp, if that's what I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"COUNTESS","lines":["He blushes, and 'tis it.","Of six preceding ancestors, that gem","Conferr'd by testament to th' sequent issue,","Hath it been owed and worn. This is his wife;","That ring's a thousand proofs."],"id":1031,"m":"He's blushing, and it's the ring. That jewel has been owned and worn by six ancestors, each leaving it in his will to the next heir. This is his wife. That ring is a thousand proofs."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Methought you said","You saw one here in court could witness it."],"id":1032,"m":"I thought you said you saw someone here at court who could witness it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I did, my lord, but loath am to produce","So bad an instrument; his name's Parolles."],"id":1033,"m":"I did, my lord, but I'm reluctant to bring forward such a bad instrument. His name's Parolles."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["I saw the man today, if man he be."],"id":1034,"m":"I saw the man today, if he is a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Find him, and bring him hither."],"id":1035,"m":"Find him and bring him here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit an Attendant.","id":1036},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["What of him?","He's quoted for a most perfidious slave,","With all the spots o' the world tax'd and debauch'd:","Whose nature sickens but to speak a truth.","Am I or that or this for what he'll utter,","That will speak anything?"],"id":1037,"m":"What about him? He's known as a most treacherous slave, accused of and corrupted by every stain in the world, whose nature is sickened by just speaking a truth. Am I to be judged this or that by what he says, when he'll say anything?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["She hath that ring of yours."],"id":1038,"m":"She has that ring of yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I think she has. Certain it is I lik'd her","And boarded her i' the wanton way of youth.","She knew her distance, and did angle for me,","Madding my eagerness with her restraint,","As all impediments in fancy's course","Are motives of more fancy; and in fine,","Her infinite cunning with her modern grace,","Subdu'd me to her rate; she got the ring,","And I had that which any inferior might","At market-price have bought."],"id":1039,"m":"I think she has. It's true that I liked her and made advances to her in the reckless way of youth. She knew how to keep her distance, and fished for me, maddening my eagerness with her restraint, since every obstacle in the course of desire makes desire stronger. In the end her endless cunning, with her ordinary charms, brought me down to her price. She got the ring, and I got what any lower man could have bought at the market price."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I must be patient.","You that have turn'd off a first so noble wife","May justly diet me. I pray you yet,—","Since you lack virtue, I will lose a husband—","Send for your ring, I will return it home,","And give me mine again."],"id":1040,"m":"I must be patient. You, who turned off a first wife so noble, may well starve me too. Still, I ask you (since you lack virtue, I'll lose a husband): send for your ring, I'll give it back, and give me mine again."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["I have it not."],"id":1041,"m":"I don't have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["What ring was yours, I pray you?"],"id":1042,"m":"Which ring was yours, may I ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Sir, much like","The same upon your finger."],"id":1043,"m":"Sir, very like the one on your finger."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Know you this ring? This ring was his of late."],"id":1044,"m":"Do you know this ring? This ring was his until just now."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["And this was it I gave him, being abed."],"id":1045,"m":"And that's the one I gave him, in bed."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The story then goes false you threw it him","Out of a casement."],"id":1046,"m":"Then the story that you threw it to him out of a window is false."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I have spoke the truth."],"id":1047,"m":"I have told the truth."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Attendant with Parolles.","id":1048},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["My lord, I do confess the ring was hers."],"id":1049,"m":"My lord, I confess the ring was hers."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["You boggle shrewdly; every feather starts you.","Is this the man you speak of?"],"id":1050,"m":"You shy badly; every feather startles you. Is this the man you spoke of?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Ay, my lord."],"id":1051,"m":"Yes, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Tell me, sirrah, but tell me true I charge you,","Not fearing the displeasure of your master,","Which on your just proceeding, I'll keep off,—","By him and by this woman here what know you?"],"id":1052,"m":"Tell me, fellow, but tell me the truth, I order you, without fearing your master's anger, which I'll keep off you if you behave honestly: what do you know about him and this woman here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["So please your majesty, my master hath been an honourable gentleman.","Tricks he hath had in him, which gentlemen have."],"id":1053,"m":"If it please your majesty, my master has been an honourable gentleman. He has had tricks in him, as gentlemen do."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Come, come, to the purpose. Did he love this woman?"],"id":1054,"m":"Come, come, to the point. Did he love this woman?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Faith, sir, he did love her; but how?"],"id":1055,"m":"Well, sir, he did love her; but how?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How, I pray you?"],"id":1056,"m":"How, may I ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["He did love her, sir, as a gentleman loves a woman."],"id":1057,"m":"He loved her, sir, as a gentleman loves a woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["How is that?"],"id":1058,"m":"How is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["He lov'd her, sir, and lov'd her not."],"id":1059,"m":"He loved her, sir, and didn't love her."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["As thou art a knave and no knave.","What an equivocal companion is this!"],"id":1060,"m":"Just as you're a rogue and not a rogue. What a slippery fellow this is!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["I am a poor man, and at your majesty's command."],"id":1061,"m":"I'm a poor man, and at your majesty's command."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["He's a good drum, my lord, but a naughty orator."],"id":1062,"m":"He's a good drum, my lord, but a bad speaker."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Do you know he promised me marriage?"],"id":1063,"m":"Do you know he promised to marry me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Faith, I know more than I'll speak."],"id":1064,"m":"Honestly, I know more than I'll say."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["But wilt thou not speak all thou know'st?"],"id":1065,"m":"But won't you say all you know?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAROLLES","lines":["Yes, so please your majesty. I did go between them as I said; but more","than that, he loved her, for indeed he was mad for her, and talked of","Satan, and of Limbo, and of furies, and I know not what: yet I was in","that credit with them at that time that I knew of their going to bed;","and of other motions, as promising her marriage, and things which would","derive me ill will to speak of; therefore I will not speak what I know."],"id":1066,"m":"Yes, if your majesty pleases. I did go between them, as I said, but more than that, he loved her, because indeed he was mad about her, and talked of Satan and Limbo and the Furies and I don't know what. Yet I was so trusted by them at that time that I knew about their going to bed, and other moves, such as promising to marry her, and things that would earn me ill will if I spoke of them. So I won't say what I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Thou hast spoken all already, unless thou canst say they are married;","but thou art too fine in thy evidence; therefore stand aside. This","ring, you say, was yours?"],"id":1067,"m":"You've said everything already, unless you can say they're married. But you're too clever in your evidence, so stand aside. You say this ring was yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Ay, my good lord."],"id":1068,"m":"Yes, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Where did you buy it? Or who gave it you?"],"id":1069,"m":"Where did you buy it? Or who gave it to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["It was not given me, nor I did not buy it."],"id":1070,"m":"It wasn't given to me, and I didn't buy it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Who lent it you?"],"id":1071,"m":"Who lent it to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["It was not lent me neither."],"id":1072,"m":"It wasn't lent to me either."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Where did you find it then?"],"id":1073,"m":"Where did you find it, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I found it not."],"id":1074,"m":"I didn't find it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["If it were yours by none of all these ways,","How could you give it him?"],"id":1075,"m":"If it wasn't yours by any of these ways, how could you give it to him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I never gave it him."],"id":1076,"m":"I never gave it to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["This woman's an easy glove, my lord; she goes off and on at pleasure."],"id":1077,"m":"This woman is a loose glove, my lord: she slips off and on as she pleases."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["This ring was mine, I gave it his first wife."],"id":1078,"m":"This ring was mine. I gave it to his first wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["It might be yours or hers for ought I know."],"id":1079,"m":"It might be yours or hers, for all I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Take her away, I do not like her now.","To prison with her. And away with him.","Unless thou tell'st me where thou hadst this ring,","Thou diest within this hour."],"id":1080,"m":"Take her away. I don't like her now. To prison with her. And take him away too. Unless you tell me where you got this ring, you'll die within the hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I'll never tell you."],"id":1081,"m":"I'll never tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Take her away."],"id":1082,"m":"Take her away."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["I'll put in bail, my liege."],"id":1083,"m":"I'll put up bail, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["I think thee now some common customer."],"id":1084,"m":"Now I think you're some common prostitute."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["By Jove, if ever I knew man, 'twas you."],"id":1085,"m":"By Jove, if I ever slept with a man, it was you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Wherefore hast thou accus'd him all this while?"],"id":1086,"m":"Then why have you accused him all this time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Because he's guilty, and he is not guilty.","He knows I am no maid, and he'll swear to't:","I'll swear I am a maid, and he knows not.","Great King, I am no strumpet, by my life;","I am either maid, or else this old man's wife."],"id":1087,"m":"Because he's guilty, and he's not guilty. He knows I'm not a virgin, and he'll swear to it. I'll swear I am a virgin, and he doesn't know. Great King, I'm no whore, on my life. I'm either a virgin or else this old man's wife."},{"t":"sd","text":"Pointing to Lafew.","id":1088},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["She does abuse our ears; to prison with her."],"id":1089,"m":"She's playing with our ears. To prison with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","lines":["Good mother, fetch my bail. Stay, royal sir;"],"id":1090,"m":"Good mother, fetch my bail. Wait, royal sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Widow.","id":1091},{"t":"sp","who":"DIANA","cont":true,"lines":["The jeweller that owes the ring is sent for,","And he shall surety me. But for this lord","Who hath abus'd me as he knows himself,","Though yet he never harm'd me, here I quit him.","He knows himself my bed he hath defil'd;","And at that time he got his wife with child.","Dead though she be, she feels her young one kick;","So there's my riddle: one that's dead is quick,","And now behold the meaning."],"id":1092,"m":"The jeweller who owns the ring has been sent for, and he'll vouch for me. But as for this lord, who has wronged me, as he knows himself, though he never actually harmed me, here I let him off. He thinks he has defiled my bed, and at that very time he got his wife pregnant. Though she's dead, she feels her baby kick. So there's my riddle: someone dead is alive. And now see the answer."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Widow with Helena.","id":1093},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Is there no exorcist","Beguiles the truer office of mine eyes?","Is't real that I see?"],"id":1094,"m":"Is there some magician deceiving my eyes? Is what I'm seeing real?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["No, my good lord;","'Tis but the shadow of a wife you see,","The name, and not the thing."],"id":1095,"m":"No, my good lord. It's only the shadow of a wife you see: the name, and not the thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["Both, both. O, pardon!"],"id":1096,"m":"Both, both. Oh, forgive me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["O, my good lord, when I was like this maid;","I found you wondrous kind. There is your ring,","And, look you, here's your letter. This it says,","'When from my finger you can get this ring,","And is by me with child, &c.' This is done;","Will you be mine now you are doubly won?"],"id":1097,"m":"Oh, my good lord, when I was taking this girl's place, I found you wonderfully kind. There's your ring, and look, here's your letter. It says, \"When you can get this ring from my finger, and are pregnant by me...\" and so on. This is done. Will you be mine now that you've been won twice over?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BERTRAM","lines":["If she, my liege, can make me know this clearly,","I'll love her dearly, ever, ever dearly."],"id":1098,"m":"If she can make me understand this clearly, my lord, I'll love her dearly, for ever, for ever dearly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENA","lines":["If it appear not plain, and prove untrue,","Deadly divorce step between me and you!","O my dear mother, do I see you living?"],"id":1099,"m":"If it isn't made plain and proves untrue, let deadly divorce come between you and me! Oh, my dear mother, do I see you alive?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LAFEW","lines":["Mine eyes smell onions; I shall weep anon.","[To Parolles] Good Tom Drum, lend me a handkercher.","So, I thank thee. Wait on me home, I'll make sport with thee.","Let thy courtesies alone, they are scurvy ones."],"id":1100,"m":"My eyes smell onions; I'll be crying in a minute. [To Parolles] Good Tom Drum, lend me a handkerchief. So, thank you. Come home with me; I'll have some fun with you. Never mind your bowing and scraping; it's a sorry business."},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["Let us from point to point this story know,","To make the even truth in pleasure flow.","[To Diana] If thou beest yet a fresh uncropped flower,","Choose thou thy husband, and I'll pay thy dower;","For I can guess that by thy honest aid,","Thou kept'st a wife herself, thyself a maid.","Of that and all the progress more and less,","Resolvedly more leisure shall express.","All yet seems well, and if it end so meet,","The bitter past, more welcome is the sweet."],"id":1101,"m":"Let us hear this story point by point, so that the plain truth can flow with pleasure. [To Diana] If you are still a fresh, unpicked flower, choose your husband, and I'll pay your dowry, because I can guess that by your honest help you kept a wife a wife and yourself a virgin. Of that, and of everything that happened, great and small, we'll hear in full when there's more time. All still seems well, and if it ends so fittingly, the bitter past makes the sweet more welcome."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish.","id":1102},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Epilogue.","id":1103},{"t":"sp","who":"KING","lines":["The king's a beggar, now the play is done;","All is well ended if this suit be won,","That you express content; which we will pay","With strife to please you, day exceeding day.","Ours be your patience then, and yours our parts;","Your gentle hands lend us, and take our hearts."],"id":1104,"m":"The King is a beggar, now that the play is over. All has ended well if we win this request: that you show you are satisfied. We will repay you by striving to please you, more every day. So let us have your patience, and you take our parts. Lend us your gentle hands in applause, and take our hearts."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt omnes.","id":1105}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Helena","group":"Rossillon","desc":"The orphaned daughter of the famous doctor Gerard de Narbon, brought up in the Countess's household. She loves Bertram, who is far above her in rank. Using one of her father's prescriptions she cures the King, who lets her choose a husband; she chooses Bertram. Abandoned on her wedding day, she follows him in pilgrim's dress to Florence, takes Diana's place in his bed and wins his ring, fulfilling the 'impossible' conditions in his letter. Clever, determined and loved by almost everyone except the man she wants."},{"name":"Bertram","group":"Rossillon","desc":"The young Count of Rossillon, whose father has just died. Handsome and brave, he is also proud and immature. Forced by the King to marry Helena, he refuses to sleep with her, runs away to the Florentine war with Parolles and sends her a letter of impossible conditions. In Florence he wins honour as a soldier and tries to seduce Diana. Back in France he lies about the ring until Helena appears, and then promises to love her."},{"name":"The Countess of Rossillon","group":"Rossillon","desc":"Bertram's widowed mother and Helena's guardian. Wise and warm, she guesses Helena's love, encourages her to go to Paris, and is delighted by the marriage. When Bertram deserts his wife she sides with Helena, calling her 'all my child'. She mourns Helena as dead and agrees to Lafew's plan to marry Bertram to his daughter."},{"name":"The King of France","group":"The court","desc":"Dying of a fistula when the play begins, he has given up on his doctors. Helena's cure restores him, and he keeps his promise by ordering Bertram to marry her, arguing that virtue matters more than birth. He keeps France out of the Florentine war but lets his young lords fight. In the last scene his anger over the ring drives the plot to its revelation, and he speaks the epilogue."},{"name":"Lafew","group":"The court","desc":"An old lord, friend of the Countess and adviser to the King. He brings Helena to the King, mocks the young lords who will not choose her, and sees through Parolles from the start. He proposes Bertram as a husband for his daughter Maudlin after Helena's supposed death, then takes the ruined Parolles into his household."},{"name":"Parolles","group":"The court","desc":"Bertram's flashy companion, a self-styled captain whose name means 'words'. He brags of war, dresses in scarves and ribbons, and urges Bertram to run away from his marriage. The French lords expose him by ambushing him blindfolded: to save his life he betrays the army's secrets and insults his friends. Disgraced, he decides simply to survive, and ends as Lafew's hanger-on."},{"name":"Lavatch (the Clown)","group":"Rossillon","desc":"The Countess's jester, kept on because her late husband enjoyed him. Bawdy and cynical, he jokes about marriage, cuckolds and the court, carries messages between Rossillon and Paris, and mocks the muddied Parolles. Parolles calls him 'Monsieur Lavache'."},{"name":"Rinaldo (the Steward)","group":"Rossillon","desc":"The Countess's steward. He overhears Helena confessing her love for Bertram and reports it, and later reads out Helena's letter saying she has become a pilgrim."},{"name":"Diana","group":"Florence","desc":"The Widow's daughter in Florence, a chaste young woman whom Bertram tries to seduce. She agrees to Helena's plan, gets Bertram's ancestral ring from him and arranges the night meeting at which Helena takes her place. In the last scene she accuses Bertram before the King and answers in riddles until Helena appears. The King offers her a husband of her choice."},{"name":"The Widow","group":"Florence","desc":"Diana's mother, a gentlewoman who has come down in the world. She lodges pilgrims, believes Helena's story, accepts her gold and helps arrange the bed-trick. She travels with Helena and Diana to France."},{"name":"Mariana","group":"Florence","desc":"A neighbour of the Widow who warns Diana against Bertram and Parolles and the false promises of seducers."},{"name":"The Duke of Florence","group":"Florence","desc":"Ruler of Florence, at war with Siena. He welcomes the French volunteers and makes Bertram general of his cavalry."},{"name":"First Lord (Captain Dumaine)","group":"The army","desc":"One of two French brothers serving in Florence. He knows Parolles for a coward and leads the ambush, inventing a nonsense language. Parolles, blindfolded, slanders him as a thief, a liar and a drunk, and he begins to 'love him' for the sheer villainy."},{"name":"Second Lord (Captain Dumaine)","group":"The army","desc":"The First Lord's brother. He brings Bertram his mother's letter, hears of Bertram's affair with Diana and comments on how men betray themselves. He goes with Bertram to see Diana and is described by Parolles as an even worse coward than his brother."},{"name":"First Soldier (the interpreter)","group":"The army","desc":"The soldier chosen to pretend to be the enemy's interpreter at Parolles's interrogation. He 'translates' the nonsense language and reads out Parolles's answers and his letter to Diana."},{"name":"Gentleman (the Astringer)","group":"The court","desc":"A gentleman of the court whom Helena meets in Marseilles. He takes her petition to the King at Rossillon."},{"name":"Two French Gentlemen","group":"The court","desc":"They bring Helena and the Countess news that Bertram has gone to fight for Florence, and carry Bertram's letter."},{"name":"Gerard de Narbon","group":"Rossillon","desc":"Helena's father, a famous doctor who died before the play begins. His secret prescription cures the King."},{"name":"Maudlin","group":"The court","desc":"Lafew's daughter, who never appears. After Helena's supposed death the King agrees she shall marry Bertram, until the ring is recognised."},{"name":"Violenta","group":"Florence","desc":"A neighbour of the Widow listed in the cast who enters with her in 3.5 but does not speak."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"In Rossillon everyone is in mourning. Bertram, the young Count, is leaving for the King's court in Paris, where he is now the King's ward. Lafew reports that the King is dying of a fistula and has given up on his doctors; the Countess wishes Helena's father, the great doctor Gerard de Narbon, were still alive. The Countess blesses Bertram with advice and he leaves. Alone, Helena admits that her tears are for Bertram, not her father: she loves a man as far above her as a star. Parolles jokes crudely with her about virginity before following his master. Helena resolves that 'our remedies oft in ourselves do lie' and thinks of the King's illness.","key":["Death and illness hang over the opening: two dead fathers and a dying King.","Helena's love for Bertram is hopeless because of the gap in rank.","Helena decides to act rather than wait on the stars."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Bertram leaving Rossillon?","o":["To fight in Italy for Florence","To serve the King, whose ward he now is","To study medicine","To find a wife"],"a":1},{"q":"What is wrong with the King?","o":["A fever","A fistula","A wound from battle","The plague"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom is Helena really crying for?","o":["Her father","The King","Bertram","The Countess"],"a":2}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"In Paris the King reads that the Florentines and the Sienese are at war. He will not send help to Florence, but French gentlemen may volunteer on either side. Bertram arrives and the King praises his late father at length, remembering how courteous he was to those below him. He asks about Gerard de Narbon, who died six months ago, and says he would try him if he were alive. He welcomes Bertram as dearly as a son.","key":["The war gives the young lords a place to win honour.","The King longs for the older generation's virtues.","The King's illness makes Gerard de Narbon's skill important."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which cities are at war?","o":["Florence and Siena","Paris and Florence","Rome and Venice","Florence and Milan"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the King decide about the war?","o":["France will send a whole army to fight for Florence","France stays out, but volunteers may go","France will fight for Siena","He will lead the army himself"],"a":1}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"The Clown asks the Countess's permission to marry Isbel and jokes about cuckolds. The Steward reports that he overheard Helena confessing her love for Bertram and complaining that Fortune had put such a gap between them. When Helena comes in, the Countess calls herself her mother; Helena insists Bertram cannot be her brother. Pressed, she confesses on her knees that she loves him but asks for nothing. She admits she meant to go to Paris with a prescription of her father's that could cure the King. The Countess gives her blessing, money and servants.","key":["The Countess guesses Helena's love and supports it.","'Mother' and 'daughter' become a riddle about marriage.","Helena's plan to cure the King is also a way to reach Bertram."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who tells the Countess that Helena loves Bertram?","o":["Lavache, the Clown","Lord Lafew","The Steward","Parolles"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Helena object to calling the Countess 'mother'?","o":["She dislikes the Countess and her proud household","It would make Bertram her brother","Her real mother is still alive in Paris","It is too formal for a servant to use"],"a":1},{"q":"How does the Countess react to Helena's plan?","o":["She forbids it","She tells the King herself","She gives her leave, money and servants","She sends Bertram to stop her"],"a":2}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"The King says goodbye to the young lords leaving for the war. Bertram is ordered to stay behind as 'too young' and talks of slipping away; Parolles brags about his scar-giving sword. Lafew introduces Helena. The King refuses at first, since his learned doctors have given up, but she argues that God often works through the weakest instruments. She promises to cure him within two days and stakes her life on it. In return she asks to choose a husband from among his subjects, not from the royal family. The King agrees.","key":["Helena argues that heaven can work through a young woman.","She risks her life and reputation on the cure.","The bargain gives her the power to choose a husband."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't Bertram go to the war?","o":["He is ill with the same fever as the King","The King says he is too young","His mother forbids it","He has no money"],"a":1},{"q":"How soon does Helena say the King will be cured?","o":["Within a week","Within two days","Within a month","At once"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Helena ask for if she succeeds?","o":["Gold enough to live on","A title at court","A husband of her choice","Her father's lands"],"a":2}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"At Rossillon the Countess tests the Clown's claim that he has one answer that fits every question at court: 'O Lord, sir!' The joke backfires when she asks whether he was recently whipped. She sends him to Paris with a letter for Helena and greetings for Bertram.","key":["A comic interlude mocking empty court manners.","The Clown's all-purpose phrase shows how hollow courtly talk can be.","Time passes while the cure works in Paris."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the Clown's answer to every question?","o":["'Indeed, madam'","'O Lord, sir!'","'I know not'","'By my troth'"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does the Countess send the Clown?","o":["To Florence","To Paris","To Marseilles","To the Widow"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Lafew and Parolles marvel that the King is cured; Parolles keeps agreeing with Lafew's words. The King, healthy again, gathers young lords and lets Helena choose. She speaks to several, who would gladly have her, then gives herself to Bertram. He refuses: she is only a poor doctor's daughter. The King argues that virtue, not birth, makes honour, offers to give her title and wealth, and finally threatens Bertram with his hatred. Bertram gives in and they are married that night. Lafew mocks and insults Parolles, seeing through his scarves and bluster. Bertram tells Parolles he will never sleep with his wife and will run off to the Tuscan wars.","key":["The King's speech on true honour is the play's argument about birth and worth.","Bertram obeys only under threat.","Lafew exposes Parolles for the first time."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Bertram refuse Helena?","o":["He loves Diana","She is a poor doctor's daughter","She is too old","She cured the King by magic"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the King threaten if Bertram refuses?","o":["Exile to Italy for the rest of his life","Throwing him out of his care and loosing his hatred on him","Taking his mother's lands from her","Prison"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Bertram tell Parolles after the wedding?","o":["He will learn to love her in time, once they are home at Rossillon","He will never sleep with her and will go to war","He will divorce her","He will take her to Florence"],"a":1}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"The Clown brings Helena the Countess's greetings and jokes with Parolles. Parolles tells Helena that Bertram must leave tonight on serious business; she must take leave of the King, present the haste as her own idea, and then wait on her husband. Helena obeys.","key":["Helena is kept from her wedding night by a lie.","Parolles is Bertram's go-between.","Helena's obedience is total."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who tells Helena that Bertram must leave tonight?","o":["Lafew","The King","Parolles","The Clown"],"a":2},{"q":"What must Helena do before leaving?","o":["Take leave of the King","Write to the Countess","Visit Florence","Return the King's ring"],"a":0}]},"a2s5":{"sum":"Lafew warns Bertram that Parolles is no soldier, calls him a nut with no kernel whose soul is his clothes, and leaves. Helena comes to say she has the King's leave to go. Bertram gives her a letter for his mother and tells her to go home at once, saying he will see her in two days. She asks shyly for a kiss; he refuses and sends her off, then tells Parolles he will never go home while he can fight.","key":["Bertram ignores Lafew's warning about Parolles.","The refused kiss shows Bertram's coldness.","Bertram lies that he will follow in two days."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Lafew say Parolles's soul is?","o":["His sword","His clothes","His tongue","His purse"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Helena ask Bertram for before parting?","o":["Money","A ring","A kiss","His blessing"],"a":2},{"q":"How long does Bertram say before they meet again?","o":["Two days","Two weeks","A year","Never"],"a":0}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"In Florence the Duke explains the reasons for the war and regrets that the King of France will not help him. The French lords say young Frenchmen will come anyway, and the Duke welcomes them.","key":["The war is a place for young men tired of ease.","The Duke has the 'holy' side of the quarrel, say the lords.","Sets the scene for Bertram's arrival."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the Duke of Florence surprised?","o":["France has joined Siena","The King of France refused to help","Bertram has deserted","Parolles has been captured"],"a":1},{"q":"Who do the lords say will come to fight?","o":["The King of France himself, now that he is cured","Young Frenchmen tired of their ease","English soldiers","The Countess's servants"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"At Rossillon the Countess reads Bertram's letter: he has married Helena but not slept with her and has run away. Helena returns with two gentlemen and reads her own letter: when she can get the ring from his finger and show him a child he has fathered, she may call him husband, but he writes 'never'. He adds that until he has no wife he has nothing in France. The Countess disowns her son and calls Helena her only child, and blames Parolles. Alone, Helena decides that her presence is driving Bertram into danger, and resolves to leave so that he can come home.","key":["Bertram's letter sets the 'impossible' conditions: the ring and a child.","The Countess takes Helena's side against her son.","Helena chooses to leave Rossillon for Bertram's sake."],"quiz":[{"q":"What two conditions does Bertram's letter set?","o":["Gold and land enough to keep him in Florence for life, sent from home","The ring from his finger and a child he fathered","A title at court and a dowry from the King","His mother's consent and the King's"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does the Countess blame for corrupting Bertram?","o":["Lafew","Helena","Parolles","The Duke"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Helena decide to leave?","o":["She hates the Countess","Her presence keeps Bertram away from home and in danger","The King banishes her","She wants to study medicine"],"a":1}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"In Florence the Duke makes Bertram general of his cavalry. Bertram swears to be a lover of Mars's drum and a hater of love.","key":["Bertram rises quickly as a soldier.","He openly rejects love for war.","A short scene that contrasts with Helena's sacrifice."],"quiz":[{"q":"What post does Bertram receive?","o":["General of the cavalry","Governor of the city of Florence","Ambassador to France","Captain of the drum"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Bertram swear to be?","o":["A lover of Mars's drum and hater of love","A faithful husband to Helena when the war is over","A friend to Parolles for as long as he lives","The Duke's son-in-law within a year"],"a":0}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"The Steward reads Helena's letter to the Countess: she has gone barefoot as a pilgrim to Saint Jaques, asking Bertram to come home from the wars and blaming herself as his 'despiteful Juno'. The Countess orders a letter sent to Bertram praising his wife's worth, hoping he will come home and Helena will return when she hears.","key":["Helena's sonnet-letter takes all the blame on herself.","Saint Jaques is in Spain, the opposite direction from Florence.","The Countess hopes both will come home."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Helena's letter say she is going?","o":["To Paris","To Saint Jaques as a pilgrim","To a convent in Florence","To Marseilles"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Countess order the Steward to do?","o":["Chase Helena and bring her home","Write to Bertram","Tell the King","Sell her jewels"],"a":1}]},"a3s5":{"sum":"Outside Florence the Widow, Diana and Mariana hope to see the army. They talk of the French count who has fought bravely and who is trying to seduce Diana through Parolles; Mariana warns Diana against men's oaths. Helena arrives dressed as a pilgrim and is offered lodging at the Widow's house. They tell her about Count Rossillon, who ran from a wife the King forced on him. The army passes with Bertram and the gloomy Parolles, who has lost a drum. Helena invites the women to supper.","key":["Helena turns up in Florence, where Bertram is, not in Spain.","She learns at once that Bertram is pursuing Diana.","Helena hides her identity and praises 'his lady'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is Bertram trying to seduce?","o":["Mariana","Diana","The Widow","Violenta"],"a":1},{"q":"How is Helena dressed?","o":["As a man","As a pilgrim","As a nun","As a soldier"],"a":1},{"q":"What has Parolles lost?","o":["His sword","His scarf","A drum","His horse"],"a":2}]},"a3s6":{"sum":"The two French lords tell Bertram that Parolles is a coward, a liar and a promise-breaker. They plan to test him: when he goes to recover the lost drum, they will ambush him blindfolded with soldiers he does not know and see if he betrays Bertram. Parolles boasts that he will recover the drum or die. When he has gone, the First Lord leaves to set the trap; Bertram takes the Second Lord to see Diana, who has sent back his love letters.","key":["The lords set a trap to expose Parolles.","Parolles boasts himself into danger.","Bertram admits Diana has refused him."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Parolles promise to do?","o":["Kill the Duke of Siena","Recover the lost drum","Bring Diana to Bertram","Return to France"],"a":1},{"q":"How will the lords trap him?","o":["With a false letter from the Duke of Florence","By ambushing and blindfolding him","By getting him drunk at supper in the camp","By telling the Duke"],"a":1}]},"a3s7":{"sum":"Helena reveals to the Widow that she is Bertram's wife and offers her a purse of gold. Her plan: Diana will agree to Bertram's suit, ask for his ancestral ring, and arrange a night meeting; Helena will take her place in the dark. Helena will add three thousand crowns for Diana's dowry. The Widow agrees.","key":["The bed-trick is planned.","Helena pays for help: money drives the plot here.","Helena insists the deed is lawful because they are married."],"quiz":[{"q":"What must Diana get from Bertram?","o":["A letter","His family ring","His sword","A promise of money"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Helena offer for Diana's marriage?","o":["Three thousand crowns","A house in Paris","A title","Her own ring"],"a":0}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"The First Lord and his soldiers hide by a hedge and agree to speak gibberish, with one soldier acting as interpreter. Parolles arrives and, thinking himself alone, wonders how to fake wounds to prove he tried to get the drum. The soldiers seize and blindfold him shouting nonsense. Terrified, he offers to reveal all the secrets of the Florentine camp to save his life.","key":["Parolles admits to himself that his tongue is braver than his heart.","The nonsense language is pure comedy.","He offers treachery at the first threat."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Parolles plan to do instead of fetching the drum?","o":["Run home to France","Fake some wounds and tell a story","Buy a new drum in the town","Ask Bertram for help"],"a":1},{"q":"What language do the ambushers use?","o":["Italian, from the Florentine soldiers","Latin","Made-up gibberish","German"],"a":2}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"Bertram woos Diana with vows of love. She points out that he has a wife and that oaths sworn to get what is unholy are worthless. When she asks for his ring he refuses, since it is an heirloom of his house; she answers that her chastity is just such a jewel. He gives her the ring. She tells him to come to her window at midnight, stay only an hour and not speak, and promises to put another ring on his finger. Alone, Diana says her mother foretold exactly how he would woo.","key":["Diana turns Bertram's own words about family honour against him.","Bertram gives away his ancestral ring for lust.","The rules of silence and darkness make the bed-trick possible."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Bertram first refuse to give the ring?","o":["It belongs to the King","It is an heirloom of his family","It is too small","He has already given it to Helena"],"a":1},{"q":"How long must Bertram stay that night?","o":["All night","Only an hour","Until dawn","Ten minutes"],"a":1},{"q":"What will Diana give him in return?","o":["A letter","A kiss","Another ring","A lock of hair"],"a":2}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"The French lords discuss Bertram's mother's letter, his seduction of Diana and the news that Helena died at Saint Jaques. Peace has been made, and Bertram will return to France. Bertram comes in boasting of sixteen pieces of business done in one night. Parolles is brought in blindfolded. Questioned through the 'interpreter', he gives away the army's numbers and slanders Captain Dumaine and his brother while they listen. A letter in his pocket warns Diana that the Count is a fool, to take his gold first. Told he must die, he begs for life; the blindfold is removed and his victims mock him. Alone, he decides to live on in shame: 'simply the thing I am shall make me live'.","key":["News of Helena's 'death' frees Bertram to go home.","Parolles betrays everyone to save himself.","His last speech is a strange, honest acceptance of what he is."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is Helena said to have died?","o":["At Rossillon, in the Countess's house","At Saint Jaques le Grand","In Florence","At sea"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Parolles's letter to Diana say?","o":["That Bertram loves her truly and will marry her when his wife is dead","That the Count is a fool and she should take his gold first","That Helena is alive and on her way","That he loves her himself"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Parolles react once exposed?","o":["He kills himself","He challenges Bertram","He decides to live on in shame","He flees to Siena"],"a":2}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Helena tells the Widow and Diana that she must see the King, who is at Marseilles, to prove she has not wronged them. Bertram thinks she is dead and is heading home. She marvels that men can make 'sweet use' of what they hate, asks Diana to bear a little more for her sake, and says 'All's well that ends well'.","key":["Helena's plan now depends on the King.","Her comment on lust in the dark is bitter.","The title phrase appears for the first time."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Helena think the King is?","o":["In Paris","Marseilles","At Rossillon","In Florence"],"a":1},{"q":"What phrase does Helena use to sum up her hope?","o":["'Love conquers all, even the proud'","'All's well that ends well'","'Time will tell and time will mend'","'Fortune favours the bold and the brave'"],"a":1}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"At Rossillon Lafew blames Parolles for leading Bertram astray, and the Countess mourns Helena as the most virtuous woman ever made. The Clown jokes about serving the devil. Lafew reveals that he has asked the King to marry Bertram to his own daughter, and the Countess agrees. The King is on his way from Marseilles; Bertram arrives with a velvet patch on his face.","key":["Helena is mourned as dead.","Lafew arranges a second marriage for Bertram.","The Clown's talk of the broad gate and the narrow gate is a sermon in jest."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does Lafew want Bertram to marry?","o":["Diana","His own daughter","The Duke's daughter","The King's niece"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Bertram have on his face?","o":["A scar with no cover","A velvet patch","A beard","A mask"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Helena, the Widow and Diana reach Marseilles, worn out, only to learn from a gentleman that the King has left for Rossillon. Helena gives him a petition to carry to the King and says they will follow as fast as they can.","key":["A last obstacle: the King has moved on.","Helena keeps going: 'All's well that ends well yet'.","The petition prepares the final scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't Helena see the King in Marseilles?","o":["He is ill","He has gone to Rossillon","He refuses","He is at war"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Helena give the gentleman?","o":["A ring","A petition","A purse","A letter for Bertram"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Parolles, ragged and muddy, asks the Clown to give Lafew a letter; the Clown holds his nose. Lafew arrives, gives Parolles a coin, recognises him as 'Tom Drum' and, when the King's trumpets sound, promises he will eat at his table though he is a fool and a knave.","key":["Parolles has fallen as low as he can.","Lafew, his first enemy, now takes pity on him.","The court is gathering at Rossillon."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Parolles claim has happened to him?","o":["Fortune has scratched and muddied him","He was robbed","He was knighted","He lost his drum again"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Lafew promise Parolles?","o":["A captain's post","That he shall eat","A duel","To send him to prison"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"The King forgives Bertram and agrees to his marriage to Lafew's daughter Maudlin. Bertram claims he always loved Helena too late. He gives a ring as a token for Maudlin; Lafew, the Countess and the King recognise it as the ring the King gave Helena. Bertram swears it was thrown to him from a window in Florence. The King, suspecting he killed Helena, has him arrested. Diana's petition arrives, claiming Bertram promised to marry her when his wife died. Diana and the Widow accuse Bertram; he calls Diana a camp whore, but she shows his family ring, and Parolles gives slippery evidence. Diana then speaks in riddles about the King's ring and is about to be sent to prison when the Widow brings in Helena, pregnant. Helena shows that both conditions in his letter are fulfilled; Bertram asks pardon and promises to love her. The King offers Diana a husband of her choice.","key":["Two rings expose Bertram's lies.","Diana's riddles keep the truth back until Helena appears.","The ending is happy on the surface but full of 'ifs'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose ring does Bertram offer as a token for Maudlin?","o":["His father's ring, an heirloom of the house","The ring the King gave Helena","Diana's own ring from Florence","Lafew's"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Bertram first claim about the ring?","o":["Helena gave it to him","It was thrown to him from a window in Florence","He bought it","Parolles stole it"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the King offer Diana?","o":["Money only, and a place in the Countess's house","A husband of her choice and a dowry","Prison for lying to the King","A place at court"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"The actor who played the King steps forward: now the play is done, the King is a beggar asking for applause. If the audience is content, all is well ended.","key":["The King becomes an actor asking for applause.","The title is turned into a request to the audience.","Whether all really ends well is left to us."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who speaks the epilogue?","o":["Helena","The King","Parolles","Lafew"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the speaker ask for?","o":["Silence","Applause","Money","Forgiveness for Bertram"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"The play opens in black: Bertram's father has just died and the Countess feels she is losing her son as well. The first word picture is a burial.","4":"Bertram is a royal ward: as an under-age noble heir, his upbringing and marriage are in the King's control. This is why the King can later force a wife on him.","7":"The Countess's 'O that \"had\"!' plays on the past tense: Helena's father is dead. The skill she praises will matter soon.","12":"A fistula is a deep ulcer or abscess. Its unglamorous nature keeps the King's illness earthy, not romantic.","15":"The Countess separates what Helena inherits (honesty) from what she earns (goodness). Merit versus birth is the play's central argument.","23":"The Countess's advice to Bertram ('Love all, trust a few') is like Polonius's to Laertes, but wiser. Bertram will ignore all of it, trusting Parolles.","30":"Helena's first soliloquy turns from grief to love. The star image sets up the gap in rank: Bertram is 'so above me'.","32":"Before we hear Parolles speak, Helena has judged him: a liar, a fool and a coward. She sees clearly what Bertram cannot.","37":"The virginity debate is full of siege metaphors: undermining, blowing up, breaches. Love is pictured as war from the start.","45":"Parolles's case against virginity is a set piece of mock logic. His name means 'words', and he has plenty of them.","48":"Helena's list of pet names lovers use at court shows her fear of losing Bertram to fashionable women.","70":"Helena's rhyming couplets mark a turning point: she decides to act. 'Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie' is her motto for the play.","76":"The King's 'cousin Austria' is a fellow ruler, not a relative. The war gives young nobles somewhere to prove themselves.","85":"The King's portrait of Bertram's father is an ideal of noble courtesy to inferiors. Bertram will fail to live up to it.","87":"The image of the drone in the hive (no wax, no honey) shows how useless the old King feels.","98":"The Clown is an 'allowed fool' kept by the household. His bawdy jokes about cuckoldry echo the Countess's later fears for marriage.","121":"The Clown's song is about Helen of Troy. He changes 'one bad in ten' into 'one good in ten', a joke against women.","130":"The Steward's report gives us Helena's complaint against Fortune, Love and Diana, goddess of chastity. Her love is a secret revealed by eavesdropping.","138":"The Countess deliberately teases Helena with the word 'mother'. If Helena is her daughter, Bertram is her brother; if her daughter-in-law, he is her husband.","151":"Helena's confession compares her to a sun-worshipper and her love to water poured into a sieve. She admits both humility and stubbornness.","155":"Helena's father's prescriptions are her only inheritance. In a world of titles and land, her wealth is knowledge.","168":"The King's farewell warns the lords to 'wed' honour, not just woo it. Marriage and war keep swapping places in the play's language.","180":"Bertram resents being kept at court 'forehorse to a smock' (leading a woman's skirt). He wants manly honour, not women's company.","187":"Parolles's tale of Captain Spurio's scar is pure invention. The name Spurio suggests 'spurious', fake.","204":"Lafew's fox who will 'eat no grapes' comes from Aesop: the fox pretends not to want what it cannot reach. He teases the King for refusing a cure.","214":"Lafew calls himself 'Cressid's uncle', Pandarus, the go-between of lovers. He jokes that leaving Helena with the King is risky.","223":"Helena argues from the Bible: God reveals wisdom to babies and works through the weakest. The doctors' learning is not the last word.","227":"Helena switches into rhyme, like a spell or an oracle. 'Twice the horses of the sun' means two days.","229":"Helena stakes not just her life but her reputation. A woman healer could be called a witch or a whore.","235":"Helena excludes the royal family from her choice: she aims high, but within limits.","272":"Lafew attacks philosophers who explain away miracles. The cure is presented as a wonder, which raises its stakes.","276":"Galen and Paracelsus were rival schools of medicine. Parolles name-drops both without understanding either.","298":"'Lustique' (lusty) is Lafew's word for the King's recovered energy. Now he can 'lead her a coranto', a lively dance.","312":"Helena calls herself 'a simple maid' and says she is 'simply' a maid: her only wealth is her virtue.","317":"Ames-ace is the lowest throw at dice. Lafew would rather take this chance than gamble his life.","321":"Lafew, standing at a distance, thinks the young lords are refusing Helena. In fact they seem willing; she is the one passing them by.","327":"Helena does not say 'I take you' but 'I give me'. She offers herself rather than claiming Bertram.","333":"Bertram's objection is pure snobbery: 'A poor physician's daughter my wife!' He also says she was raised 'at my father's charge'.","334":"The King's speech on honour is the heart of the play's debate. Honour should come from deeds, not from titles or ancestors.","338":"The King moves from argument to threat. He reminds Bertram that a ward has no right to refuse a match his guardian chooses.","339":"Bertram's submission is polite but hollow. He says the King's praise makes Helena 'as 'twere born so', not that he loves her.","356":"Lafew's insults (a lattice window he can see through, a ship too small for its sails) all say Parolles is empty and transparent.","376":"Lafew's charge that Parolles was beaten in Italy for stealing a pomegranate seed makes him a petty thief.","388":"'The dark house and the detested wife': for Bertram, marriage is a prison and war is freedom.","407":"The Clown plays on 'nothing': Parolles says nothing, knows nothing and is close to nothing.","445":"Lafew's 'no kernel in this light nut' and 'the soul of this man is his clothes' sum up Parolles in two images.","453":"Bertram's speech is all evasion: his 'respects are better than they seem'. He hides his plan to run away.","464":"Helena's broken lines and long pauses make the kiss she asks for painfully hard to say. Bertram simply tells her to go.","493":"Bertram's letter plays on 'wedded' and 'bedded' and makes 'not' eternal. His wit is cruel.","509":"The conditions are the sort set in folk tales: impossible tasks that the heroine then performs.","512":"The Countess now treats Helena as her own child and washes Bertram's name 'out of my blood'.","532":"Helena's soliloquy pictures the bullets ('leaden messengers') flying at Bertram and blames herself. Her answer is to remove herself.","539":"Bertram's couplet makes him 'a lover of thy drum, hater of love'. The drum will become Parolles's undoing.","544":"Helena's letter is a sonnet. Juno, queen of the gods, sent Hercules on dangerous labours; Helena calls herself his 'despiteful Juno'.","558":"Mariana warns that seducers' 'engines of lust' are not what they seem, and that girls ignore the example of others' ruin.","562":"The shrine of Saint Jaques (Santiago de Compostela) is in Spain. Florence is in the other direction, so audiences ask whether Helena planned to follow Bertram.","581":"Helena praises herself in the third person while hiding her identity. The irony is gentle and sad.","611":"The French lords know Parolles as well as Lafew does. Only Bertram is fooled.","621":"Losing a regiment's drum was a disgrace because drums carried signals and the regiment's honour.","656":"Helena buys the Widow's help with gold and a promised dowry. Money, not just love, makes the plot work.","660":"Helena's riddle: Bertram means to sin, but the act is lawful because they are married. Neither woman actually sins.","670":"'Choughs' language' is jackdaw chatter. The plan relies on Parolles's claim to know many languages.","674":"Parolles's soliloquy is honest at last: his tongue promises what his heart will not do.","696":"The nonsense words are invented for comedy. Parolles, the man of words, is defeated by meaningless words.","724":"Diana uses religious argument: an oath sworn to do wrong cannot be holy. She out-argues Bertram as Helena out-argued the King.","730":"Diana echoes Bertram's own words about his ring, line for line, and applies them to her chastity.","732":"The rules (darkness, silence, one hour) are what make the bed-trick plausible on stage.","744":"'Monumental ring': a ring that is a memorial of his ancestors. Bertram thinks he has made a good bargain.","757":"The report of Helena's death is false, but the audience only learns how she spread it in 4.4.","765":"'The web of our life is of a mingled yarn': one of the play's most quoted lines, spoken by a minor character. It sums up the play's mixed tone.","772":"Bertram lists 'buried a wife, mourn'd for her' as just two items among sixteen pieces of business.","779":"Parolles has confessed to 'Morgan', the soldier he thinks is a friar.","812":"Parolles's slander of Captain Dumaine is outrageous, and the man himself is listening.","831":"Parolles's poem warns Diana to take Bertram's gold before he gets what he wants. It insults Bertram as a foolish boy.","839":"The First Lord's 'I begin to love him for this' shows how Parolles's shamelessness becomes almost admirable.","846":"Parolles uses legal language about land (fee-simple, entail) to say the captain would sell his soul for a small coin.","870":"Parolles's last soliloquy: he has lost his reputation but not his will to live. 'Simply the thing I am shall make me live.'","876":"Helena's lines on 'strange men' who make 'sweet use of what they hate' are bitter: Bertram enjoyed in the dark the wife he hates by day.","878":"Helena speaks the title, 'All's well that ends well', and adds 'the fine's the crown': the ending crowns the deed.","882":"Lafew's 'snipt-taffeta fellow' and 'red-tailed humble-bee' are Parolles in his slashed silk and bright colours.","903":"The Clown's narrow gate and broad gate come from the Gospel: the narrow way leads to life, the broad way to destruction.","917":"The velvet patch may cover a war wound or, the Clown hints, a sign of venereal disease. Its meaning is left open.","938":"Helena repeats the proverb 'All's well that ends well yet', the word 'yet' showing she is not sure.","953":"The Clown's jokes compare Parolles to a fish that has fallen into Fortune's fishpond, muddy and smelly.","960":"Lafew's 'How does your drum?' shows the story of the drum has followed Parolles home.","986":"Bertram claims he loved Lafew's daughter first, and that this made him despise Helena. He now says he loved Helena since he lost her.","987":"The King warns that love that comes too late is a sour offence. His speech is about regret.","993":"The King's ring was meant as a token of help: if Helena needed him, she would send it. Now it seems to prove her death.","997":"Bertram's story of the ring thrown from a window is a lie, and the audience knows it.","1008":"Diana's petition calls Bertram a seducer and her honour 'paid to him'. The trap is closing.","1029":"Bertram calls Diana 'a common gamester to the camp', a soldiers' whore. It is his lowest moment.","1039":"Bertram blames Diana's cunning for his own lust. He still lies about the ring.","1066":"Parolles, the great talker, is now a careful witness who knows 'more than I'll speak'.","1085":"'If ever I knew man, 'twas you': 'know' meant to sleep with. Diana's riddles are all literally true.","1092":"Diana's riddle: one that's dead is quick. 'Quick' means alive and also pregnant.","1097":"Helena quotes Bertram's letter back to him: both conditions are met.","1098":"Bertram's promise to love her is conditional: 'If she... can make me know this clearly'. Critics argue over whether he means it.","1101":"The King's last couplet says all 'seems' well, 'if' it ends so. The title is hedged even at the end.","1104":"The epilogue turns the King into an actor and asks the audience for applause."},"gloss":{"adoptious":"adopted, given","affect":"pretend, put on","amendment":"recovery","ames-ace":"two aces, lowest throw at dice","appeach'd":"informed against you","apprehensive":"quick to learn","araise":"raise from the dead","armipotent":"powerful in arms","Bajazet":"a Turkish sultan","bannerets":"small flags","barnes":"children","barricado":"barricade","bauble":"fool's stick","beguile":"cheat; pass (time)","botcher":"mender of clothes","braving":"defiant","bunting":"a dull bird","calumnious":"slanderous","canary":"a lively dance","caprichio":"whim","captious":"deceiving","carbonado'd":"slashed like grilled meat","carp":"a fish; one who talks","casement":"window","cassocks":"soldiers' cloaks","chape":"metal tip of a scabbard","Charlemain":"Charlemagne, emperor","choughs":"jackdaws","christendoms":"names given at baptism","cicatrice":"scar","clew":"ball of thread","clog":"weight tied to an animal, burden","close-stool":"chamber pot","coil":"fuss","collateral":"parallel, from a distance","commendations":"praise","congied":"took leave","conjectural":"suspected","convoy":"escort","coragio":"courage!","coranto":"a quick dance","counterfeit":"fake","coxcomb":"fool","cozen":"cheat","cozen'd":"cheated","Cressid":"Cressida, the faithless lover of Troilus","cuckold":"man whose wife is unfaithful","curious":"careful","curtal":"horse with a docked tail","curvet":"leap of a horse","customer":"prostitute","debile":"weak","dilated":"fuller","dilemmas":"problems","diurnal":"daily","dower":"money brought by a bride","egregious":"outrageous","embossed":"cornered (like a hunted animal)","embowell'd":"emptied out","empirics":"quacks","entail":"fixed line of inheritance","enwombed":"born from my womb","equivocal":"double-meaning","ergo":"therefore","exorcist":"conjuror of spirits","exploit":"military action","favour":"face, looks; token","fee-simple":"absolute ownership","fistula":"a deep ulcer","fond":"foolish","forehorse":"lead horse of a team","Galen":"ancient Greek physician","gamester":"prostitute","gossips":"acts as godparent to","haggish":"ugly, hag-like","handkercher":"handkerchief","Hesperus":"the evening star","hilding":"good-for-nothing","hind":"female deer","hoodwink":"blindfold","importing":"urgent","impudence":"shamelessness","inteemable":"unable to hold anything","Isbel":"a girl's name","jack-an-apes":"monkey; fool","jades":"worn-out horses","kicky-wicky":"wife, darling (mocking)","lattice":"criss-cross window","leaguer":"military camp","linsey-woolsey":"mixed cloth; gibberish","list":"limit, edge","marjoram":"a herb","Marseilles":"port in southern France","Mile-end":"London training ground for citizen soldiers","misprision":"contempt; mistake","module":"model, image","mortality":"death","musk-cat":"civet cat, used for perfume","muster-file":"roll of soldiers","naturalize":"make familiar, at home","Nebuchadnezzar":"king who ate grass","Nessus":"centaur who tried to rape Hercules' wife","notorious":"well known","nursery":"training ground","obloquy":"disgrace","overlooking":"care, supervision","palmers":"pilgrims","Paracelsus":"Renaissance physician","pasty":"pie","perfidious":"treacherous","persever":"persevere","phisnomy":"face","phoenix":"rare, unique creature","pile":"thickness of velvet","Pippen":"King Pepin, father of Charlemagne","pitchy":"pitch-black","plausive":"pleasing; plausible","Plutus":"god of wealth","poniards":"daggers","porridge":"thick soup","posting":"travelling fast","predominant":"in the ascendant (astrology)","prejudicates":"judges in advance","quart d'ecu":"quarter crown, small coin","quick":"alive; pregnant","recantation":"taking back what was said","receipts":"prescriptions","rector":"priest in charge","relics":"remains of a saint","retrograde":"moving backwards (astrology)","Rossillon":"Roussillon, in southern France","ruttish":"lustful","saffron":"yellow dye","Saint Jaques":"Santiago de Compostela in Spain","sanctimony":"holiness","saucy":"lustful; cheeky","scruple":"tiny amount","Senoys":"Sienese, people of Siena","shrieve":"sheriff","sinister":"left","sithence":"since","smock":"woman's undergarment","snuff":"burnt end of a candle wick","sovereignty":"power to cure","sprat":"tiny fish; worthless person","staggers":"giddiness; a horse disease","stratagem":"military plan","strumpet":"whore","subjection":"obedience","surplice":"white church robe","taffeta":"thin silk","Tartar":"from Central Asia, thought fierce","theoric":"theory","tinct":"alchemist's elixir","tithe":"a tenth part","traduc'd":"slandered","tragedians":"actors","Tuscan":"of Tuscany, around Florence","uncropped":"unpicked","undermine":"dig under (a wall)","unseason'd":"inexperienced","validity":"value","vassal":"servant, subject","vendible":"saleable","virginity":"being a virgin","ward":"a minor under a guardian's control","woodcock":"foolish bird, easily caught"}};
const PARTS={"23":[[0,3,"Bless you, Bertram, and may you be like your father in behaviour as you are in looks! May your noble blood and your goodness compete to rule you, and may your virtue match your birth! Love everyone, trust only a few,"],[4,7,"and do wrong to no one. Be strong enough to beat your enemy, but don't use that strength. Guard your friend as closely as your own life. Be told off for keeping quiet, but never blamed for what you say."],[8,11,"Whatever else heaven wants to give you, and my prayers can bring down, may it fall on your head! Goodbye. My lord, he's an inexperienced courtier. Please, my lord, advise him."]],"30":[[0,6,"If only that were all! I'm not thinking about my father, and these big tears honour his memory more than the ones I actually shed for him. What did he look like? I've forgotten him. My imagination holds no face but Bertram's. I'm ruined: there's no living, none at all, if Bertram is away."],[7,13,"I might as well love one bright star in the sky and think of marrying it, he is so far above me. I must take comfort in his light shining on me from a distance, not in sharing his sphere. My ambitious love torments itself: the deer that wants to mate with the lion must die of love."],[14,19,"It was sweet, though it was torture, to see him every hour, to sit and draw his arched eyebrows, his keen hawk-like eyes and his curls on the drawing board of my heart, a heart that took in every line and feature of his lovely face. But now he's gone, and my love, which worships him like an idol, must treat the things he left behind as holy relics. Who's this coming?"]],"45":[[0,4,"There's little to be said for it; it's against the law of nature. To speak up for virginity is to accuse your own mothers, which is outright disobedience. A virgin is like a man who hangs himself: virginity murders itself, and should be buried at a crossroads outside holy ground, as a desperate offender against nature."],[5,10,"Virginity breeds mites, just like a cheese; it eats itself away to the rind, and so it dies from feeding on itself. Besides, virginity is bad-tempered, proud, idle and made of self-love, which is the most forbidden sin in the Church's law. Don't keep it; you can only lose by it. Out with it! Within a year it will turn into two, which is a fine profit, and the original investment won't be much the worse. Get rid of it!"]],"47":[[0,5,"Let me see. Well, by doing wrong and liking a man who doesn't like virginity. It's a product that loses its shine the longer it sits on the shelf; the longer it's kept, the less it's worth. Sell it while it's still saleable; meet the demand while it lasts. Virginity is like an old courtier who wears an old-fashioned hat: richly dressed but out of style, like the brooch and the toothpick, which nobody wears any more."],[6,9,"A date is better in your pie and your porridge than in your cheek. And your virginity, your old virginity, is like one of our French withered pears: it looks bad and tastes dry. Yes, it's a withered pear. It used to be better, but it's still a withered pear. Do you want to do anything with it?"]],"48":[[0,6,"Not with my virginity yet. At court your master will find a thousand loves: a mother, a mistress and a friend, a phoenix, a captain and an enemy, a guide, a goddess and a ruler, an adviser, a traitor and a darling;"],[7,12,"his humble ambition and proud humility, his clashing harmony and sweet discord, his faith and his sweet disaster, and a whole world of pretty, foolish pet names that blind Cupid gives out. Now he will... I don't know what he will. God keep him well! The court is a place for learning, and he is one..."]],"70":[[0,5,"The cures we look to heaven for are often inside ourselves. The stars give us freedom to act; they only hold back our plans when we ourselves are slow. What power is it that lifts my love so high, that lets me see what I want but never have it?"],[6,13,"Nature can bring together things separated by the greatest distance of fortune, and make them join and kiss as if they were born equals. Strange attempts seem impossible only to those who measure the effort by common sense and assume that what has never happened can't happen. What woman who tried to show her worth ever failed to win her love? The King's disease... my plan may let me down, but my mind is made up, and it won't leave me."]],"85":[[0,4,"I wish I were as healthy in body now as I was when your father and I, as friends, first tried our hand at soldiering. He understood the warfare of the time deeply, and he learned from the bravest."],[5,11,"He lasted a long time, but ugly old age crept up on us both and wore us out of action. It does me a lot of good to talk about your fine father. In his youth he had the wit I can see today in our young lords, but they can go on joking until their mockery comes back on them unnoticed, before they can cover their lightness with honour."],[12,17,"He was such a true courtier that there was no contempt or bitterness in his pride or his sharpness. If there was, it was only because an equal had provoked it. His sense of honour, like a clock, knew the exact minute when an objection called on him to speak, and then his tongue obeyed his hand."],[18,24,"People below him he treated as if they came from a higher rank, and bowed his great head to their low position, so that they were proud of his humility while he humbled himself by accepting their poor praise. Such a man could be a model for these younger times. If they followed it well, it would show them that they are now only going backwards."]],"87":[[0,6,"I wish I were with him! He always used to say... I think I can hear him now. He didn't scatter his fine words into people's ears; he planted them there to grow and bear fruit. \"Let me not live,\" his gentle sadness often began, at the end of some entertainment, when it was over. \"Let me not live,\" he said,"],[7,11,"\"after my lamp runs out of oil, to be the snuff-end of younger spirits, whose quick minds scorn anything that isn't new, whose judgements give birth to nothing but new clothes, and whose loyalty dies before their fashions do.\" That's what he wished."],[12,15,"And I, after him, wish the same. Since I can bring home neither wax nor honey, I wish I were quickly removed from my hive to make room for some workers."]],"115":[[0,4,"You don't understand much about great friends, madam, because those rogues come to do for me the work I'm tired of. The man who ploughs my land saves my horses and lets me bring in the harvest. If he makes me a cuckold, he's my labourer. The man who comforts my wife looks after my flesh and blood;"],[5,9,"the man who looks after my flesh and blood loves my flesh and blood; the man who loves my flesh and blood is my friend; therefore the man who kisses my wife is my friend. If men could be happy to be what they are, there'd be nothing to fear in marriage. Young Charbon the Puritan and old Poysam the Catholic, however much their hearts are split by religion, have heads that are just the same: they can knock horns together like any deer in the herd."]],"121":[[0,10,"[Sings] \"Was this lovely face the reason,\" she said, \"why the Greeks destroyed Troy? Foolishly done, done foolishly; was this King Priam's joy?\" With that she sighed as she stood, and then gave this verdict: \"Among nine bad, if one is good, there's still one good in ten.\""]],"130":[[0,5,"Madam, I was much nearer her just now than I think she wanted. She was alone, and speaking her own words to her own ears. She thought, I'd swear, that no one else could hear. What she said was that she loved your son. Fortune, she said, was no goddess, to have put such a difference between their two ranks; Love was no god, if he would only use his power where people were equal;"],[6,11,"Diana was no queen of virgins, if she let her poor follower be captured in the first attack with no rescue, or ransom afterwards. She said this in the most bitter sorrow I ever heard a young woman speak in. I thought it my duty to tell you quickly, since, in case anything bad comes of it, it is your concern to know."]],"138":[[0,5,"No, a mother. Why not a mother? When I said \"mother\", I thought you looked as if you'd seen a snake. What is there in \"mother\" that makes you start? I say I am your mother, and I put you on the list of those born from my womb."],[6,9,"We often see adoption compete with nature, and choice grow a branch of our own from someone else's seed. You never made me groan in childbirth, yet I show you a mother's care."],[10,14,"God's mercy, girl! Does it curdle your blood to hear me say I'm your mother? What's the matter, that this upset messenger of rain, the many-coloured rainbow, rings your eye? Why, because you're my daughter?"]],"144":[[0,8,"Yes, Helen, you could be my daughter-in-law. God forbid that's what you mean! \"Daughter\" and \"mother\" make your pulse race so. What, pale again? My fear has caught your love. Now I see the secret of your loneliness, and find the source of your salty tears. Now it's plain to anyone that you love my son. Excuses are ashamed to deny it against what your passion proclaims."],[9,18,"So tell me the truth, and tell me it is so, because look, your cheeks confess it to each other, and your eyes see it so plainly in your behaviour that they speak it in their own way. Only sin and wicked stubbornness tie your tongue, so that the truth is doubted. Speak: is it so? If it is, you've wound yourself a fine tangle. If it isn't, swear it isn't. Either way, I order you, as heaven will help me to help you, to tell me truly."]],"151":[[0,9,"Then I confess, here on my knees, before high heaven and you, that more than you, and next only to high heaven, I love your son. My family were poor but honest, and so is my love. Don't be offended, because it does him no harm that I love him. I don't pursue him with any sign of presumptuous wooing, nor would I have him until I deserve him, and yet I don't know how I ever could."],[10,16,"I know I love in vain and struggle against hope. Yet into this deceiving sieve that holds nothing I keep pouring the waters of my love, and never run short of more to lose. So, like a sun-worshipper in India, devoted in my mistake, I adore the sun, which looks down on its worshipper but knows nothing more of him."],[17,26,"My dearest madam, don't let your hate meet my love because I love the one you love. If you yourself, whose honourable old age points to a virtuous youth, ever in so true a flame of love wished chastely and loved dearly, so that your Diana was both chaste and loving, then pity a woman whose situation means she can only lend and give where she's sure to lose; who doesn't try to find what she's searching for, but, like a riddle, lives sweetly where she dies!"]],"155":[[0,4,"I'll tell the truth; I swear by grace itself. You know my father left me some prescriptions with rare and proven effects, which his reading and plain experience had collected as cures for everything,"],[5,10,"and that he told me to keep them with the greatest care, as notes whose powers were greater than people knew. Among them is a remedy, tested and written down, to cure the hopeless wasting illness that the King is said to be lost to."]],"168":[[0,4,"No, no, that can't be. And yet my heart won't admit it suffers from the illness that is besieging my life. Goodbye, young lords. Whether I live or die, show yourselves the sons of worthy Frenchmen."],[5,9,"Let northern Italy (except those who have inherited only the fall of the last empire) see that you come not to court honour but to marry it. Where the bravest seeker shrinks back, find what you are looking for, so that fame shouts your name aloud. I say goodbye."]],"204":[[0,4,"Oh, won't you eat any grapes, my royal fox? Yes, you would eat my noble grapes, if my royal fox could reach them. I have seen a medicine that can breathe life into a stone,"],[5,9,"bring a rock to life, and make you dance a lively canary dance with fiery energy. Its mere touch has the power to raise King Pepin from the dead, or even to put a pen in great Charlemagne's hand and have him write her a love poem."]],"219":[[0,5,"Then I'll spare my praise of him; knowing him is enough. On his deathbed he gave me many prescriptions, and one above all, the dearest result of his practice and the only darling of his long experience. He told me to treasure it like a third eye, more safely than my own two."],[6,11,"I have done so. And hearing that your majesty is afflicted with the very illness in which my dear father's gift is most powerful, I've come to offer it, and my treatment, in all due humility."]],"220":[[0,4,"We thank you, young lady, but we can't be so ready to believe in a cure, when our most learned doctors have given up on us, and the whole college of physicians has concluded that their hard-working skill can never rescue nature from her hopeless state."],[5,10,"I say we must not so stain our judgement, or spoil our hope, as to hand over our incurable illness to quacks, or so split our royal self from our reputation as to value a senseless remedy when we think we're past all sensible help."]],"223":[[0,5,"There's no harm in trying what I can do, since you've staked everything against any remedy. God, who finishes the greatest works, often does them through the weakest servant. So the Bible shows wisdom in babies when judges have behaved like babies."],[6,10,"Great floods have flowed from small springs, and great seas have dried up when the greatest men refused to believe in miracles. Expectation often fails, and most often where it promises most; and it often succeeds where hope is coldest and despair seems most fitting."]],"225":[[0,4,"So inspired worth is shut out by a word. It isn't like this with God, who knows everything, as it is with us, who judge by appearances. But it is the greatest presumption in us when we count heaven's help as the work of human beings."],[5,10,"Dear sir, agree to my attempt. Make a trial of heaven, not of me. I'm not a fraud who claims more than I can hit. Know that I think, and think I know for certain, that my skill is not powerless and you are not past cure."]],"230":[[0,5,"I think some blessed spirit speaks its powerful voice through you, a weak instrument. What common sense would rule impossible, a higher sense saves another way. Your life is precious, because everything that makes life worth the name is valued in you:"],[6,11,"youth, beauty, wisdom, courage, everything that happiness and the prime of life can call happy. To risk all this, you must have either infinite skill or monstrous desperation. Sweet doctor, I will try your medicine, which will bring about your own death if I die."]],"236":[[0,4,"Here is my hand. If you keep to your terms, I'll keep my promise and do your will. So choose your own time, because I, your determined patient, rely on you completely. I ought to question you more, and I must, though knowing more could not make me trust you more:"],[5,9,"where you come from, who came with you. But stay unquestioned and welcome, and blessed beyond doubt. Help me here, someone! If you do as much as you've said, my actions will match yours."]],"334":[[0,7,"It's only her rank you despise in her, and that I can build up. It's strange that our bloods, which if you poured them all together would be impossible to tell apart in colour, weight and heat, should stand apart with such great differences. If she is everything virtuous, except for what you dislike (a poor doctor's daughter), you dislike virtue because of a name. But don't do that."],[8,14,"When virtuous deeds come from the lowest place, the place is honoured by the doer's deed. Where great titles puff someone up and there's no virtue, it is a swollen, diseased honour. Good is good on its own, without a name; so is wickedness. A thing should be judged by what it is, not by what it is called."],[15,24,"She is young, wise and beautiful; she inherits these directly from nature, and these produce honour. The honour that claims to be honour's child but is not like its father deserves scorn. Honours flourish when we earn them by our own deeds rather than from our ancestors. The mere word is a slave, abused on every tomb, a lying trophy on every grave, and is just as often silent where dust and cursed forgetting are the only tomb of truly honourable bones."],[25,27,"What more is there to say? If you can like this creature as a young woman, I can create the rest. Her virtue and herself are her own dowry; honour and wealth will come from me."]],"338":[[0,8,"My honour is at stake, and to defend it I must use my power. Here, take her hand, you proud, scornful boy, unworthy of this good gift, who with your vile contempt chain up my love and her worth; who can't imagine that I, adding my weight to her lighter side of the scales, can outweigh you completely; who won't recognise that I have the power to plant your honour wherever I please."],[9,12,"Hold back your contempt. Obey my will, which works for your good. Don't trust your disdain, but at once give your own fortunes the obedience that your duty owes and my power demands,"],[13,17,"or I'll throw you out of my care for ever into the staggering and reckless fall of youth and ignorance, letting loose both my revenge and my hatred on you in the name of justice, without any pity. Speak! Your answer!"]],"412":[[0,2,"A good rogue, honestly, and well fed. Madam, my lord is going away tonight. Very serious business calls him away."],[3,9,"He acknowledges the great privilege and right of love, which time now claims as your due, but he is forced to postpone it. Its absence and its delay are sweetened with pleasures, which are being distilled during this time of restraint, to make the hour to come overflow with joy and fill pleasure to the brim."]],"453":[[0,6,"I'll obey his wishes. You mustn't be surprised, Helen, at what I'm doing, which doesn't suit the occasion, nor do I carry out the duties expected of me. I wasn't prepared for such a business, and so I'm very unsettled."],[7,12,"This makes me ask you to set off for home at once, and to wonder rather than ask why I ask you, because my reasons are better than they look, and my business has a need in it greater than appears at first sight to you, who don't know about it. This is for my mother."]],"532":[[0,9,"\"Until I have no wife, I have nothing in France.\" Nothing in France until he has no wife! You'll have none, Rossillon, none in France; then you'll have everything again. Poor lord, is it me who is chasing you out of your country, and exposing your tender body to the chances of merciless war? And is it me who drives you from the playful court, where beautiful eyes shot at you, to be the target of smoking muskets?"],[10,17,"Oh, you lead bullets that ride on the violent speed of fire, fly wide of your aim! Pierce the air that whistles as you cut through it, and closes up behind you; don't touch my lord. Whoever shoots at him, I put him there; whoever charges at his brave chest, I'm the wretch who holds him to it. And though I don't kill him, I'm the cause of his death if it happens."],[18,24,"It would be better for me to meet the ravenous lion when he roars with the sharp pain of hunger; better that all the miseries nature has to give were mine at once. No: come home, Rossillon, from where honour wins only a scar from danger, and often loses everything. I'll go. It's my being here that keeps you away."],[25,30,"Shall I stay here and do that? No, no, even if the air of paradise cooled the house and angels did all the work. I'll go, so that rumour can pityingly report my running away and comfort your ears. Come, night; end, day; because with the dark I'll steal away like a poor thief."]],"544":[[0,7,"[Reads] \"I'm a pilgrim of Saint Jaques, and gone there. My ambitious love has sinned so badly that I'm trudging barefoot on the cold ground, with a holy vow to have my faults put right. Write, write, so that my dearest master, your dear son, may hurry home from the bloody course of war. Bless him at home in peace, while I, far away, honour his name with my devoted prayers."],[8,13,"Tell him to forgive me for the hardships he has taken on. I, his cruel Juno, sent him away from his friends at court to live among enemies in camp, where death and danger chase at the heels of the brave. He is too good and too handsome for death and me. I embrace death myself to set him free.\""]],"547":[[0,4,"What angel will bless this unworthy husband? He can't prosper unless her prayers, which heaven delights to hear and loves to grant, save him from the anger of the highest justice."],[5,9,"Write, write, Rinaldo, to this husband unworthy of his wife. Let every word weigh heavy with her worth, which he values too lightly. Write down sharply my great grief, though he feels it little. Send the quickest messenger."],[10,17,"Perhaps when he hears she has gone he will come back, and I can hope that she, hearing that, will hurry back here too, led by pure love. Which of them is dearer to me I can't tell. Get this messenger ready. My heart is heavy, and my age is weak. Grief wants tears, and sorrow makes me speak."]],"558":[[0,4,"I know that rogue, hang him! A man called Parolles: a filthy go-between for the young count in these proposals. Watch out for them, Diana. Their promises, temptations, oaths, love tokens and all these weapons of lust are not what they pretend to be. Many a girl has been seduced by them;"],[5,9,"and the misery of it is that the example, which looks so terrible in the wreck of a girl's virginity, still can't stop others following, but they're caught in the very traps that threaten them. I hope I don't need to advise you further, and I hope your own goodness will keep you as you are, even if the only danger were the loss of modesty."]],"615":[[0,4,"I'll ambush him suddenly with a troop of Florentines, men I'm sure he can't tell apart from the enemy. We'll tie him up and blindfold him, so that he'll think he's being carried into the enemy camp when we take him to our own tents."],[5,8,"Just be there, your lordship, when we question him. If he doesn't, for the promise of his life and driven by the lowest fear, offer to betray you and tell all he knows against you, and swear it on his immortal soul, never trust my judgement in anything again."]],"656":[[0,3,"Take this purse of gold, and let me buy your friendly help so far. I'll pay you more, and pay you again, once I have it."],[4,8,"The count is wooing your daughter, laying his lustful siege to her beauty, determined to conquer her. Let her finally agree, and we'll tell her the best way to handle it. His urgent desire won't refuse anything she asks for."],[9,14,"The count wears a ring that has been handed down in his family from son to son for four or five generations since the first ancestor wore it. He values this ring very highly. Yet in his foolish passion, to get what he wants, it won't seem too high a price, however much he regrets it afterwards."]],"724":[[0,5,"It isn't a lot of oaths that makes something true, but one simple vow sincerely made. We don't swear by what is not holy, but call on the highest to witness. Then tell me, please: if I swore by all Jove's great powers that I loved you dearly, would you believe my oaths when I was loving you badly?"],[6,10,"It makes no sense to swear by God, whom I claim to love, that I'll act against him. So your oaths are just words and empty conditions, unsealed, at least in my opinion."]],"732":[[0,5,"When midnight comes, knock at my bedroom window. I'll make sure my mother doesn't hear. Now I'll bind you by your promise of truth: when you have conquered my still virgin bed, stay only an hour and don't speak to me."],[6,11,"My reasons are very strong, and you'll learn them when this ring is given back to you. And in the night I'll put another ring on your finger, so that whatever happens later may be a sign to the future of what we did. Goodbye until then. Don't fail. You've won a wife in me, though my hope ends there."]],"735":[[0,5,"May you live long to thank both heaven and me for it! You may do, in the end. My mother told me exactly how he would woo me, as if she were inside his heart. She says all men swear the same oaths. He has sworn to marry me when his wife's dead, so I'll sleep with him when I'm buried."],[6,9,"Since Frenchmen are so deceitful, let those who want to marry; I'll live and die a virgin. Only, in this disguise, I don't think it's a sin to cheat a man who would win unfairly."]],"806":[[0,4,"Honestly, sir, if I had only an hour to live, I'll tell the truth. Let me see: Spurio, a hundred and fifty; Sebastian, the same; Corambus, the same; Jaques, the same; Guiltian, Cosmo, Lodowick and Gratii, two hundred and fifty each; my own company, Chitopher, Vaumond and Bentii, two hundred and fifty each."],[5,7,"So the whole muster, rotten and sound, on my life, doesn't come to fifteen thousand heads, and half of them don't dare shake the snow off their cloaks in case they shake themselves to pieces."]],"831":[[0,4,"[Reads] \"When he swears oaths, tell him to drop gold, and take it. Once he has run up a bill, he never pays it. A bargain half won is a bargain well made; make the bargain, and make it well. He never pays debts afterwards; get paid first."],[5,10,"And tell yourself, Diana, a soldier told you this: men are for sleeping with, boys are not to be kissed. Because take note of this: the count's a fool, I know it, who pays in advance but not when he owes. Yours, as he swore to you in your ear, Parolles.\""]],"838":[[0,4,"He would steal an egg out of a monastery, sir. For rape and abduction he's the equal of Nessus. He makes a point of not keeping oaths; in breaking them he's stronger than Hercules. He'll lie, sir, so fluently that you'd think truth was a fool."],[5,8,"Drunkenness is his best virtue, because he'll get pig-drunk, and in his sleep he does little harm, except to his bedclothes; but they know his habits and lay him on straw. I have little more to say about his honesty, sir: he has everything an honest man shouldn't have, and nothing an honest man should."]],"870":[[0,4,"Still, I'm thankful. If my heart were proud, this would make it burst. I'll be a captain no more, but I'll eat and drink and sleep as comfortably as any captain. Simply being what I am will keep me alive."],[5,10,"Anyone who knows he's a braggart, let him fear this, because it will come to pass that every braggart is found out to be an ass. Rust, sword; cool down, blushes; and, Parolles, live safest in shame. Having been made a fool of, prosper by foolery. There's a place and a living for every man alive. I'll go after them."]],"874":[[0,6,"So that you can see clearly I haven't wronged you, one of the greatest men in the Christian world will guarantee my word. Before I can complete my plans, I must kneel before his throne. Once I did him a service he longed for, almost as dear to him as his life."],[7,13,"Gratitude for that would show itself even through the stony heart of a Tartar and answer with thanks. I'm reliably told his majesty is at Marseilles, and we have a convenient escort there. You must know that I'm thought to be dead. Now the army is breaking up, my husband is hurrying home, where, with heaven's help and the permission of my good lord the King, we'll arrive before we're expected."]],"876":[[0,5,"Nor, mistress, did you ever have a friend whose thoughts worked harder to repay your love. Don't doubt that heaven has raised me up to provide your daughter's dowry, just as it has destined her to be my means and my helper to a husband."],[6,9,"But oh, strange men, who can make such sweet use of what they hate, when the lustful trust of their deceived thoughts stains the pitch-black night! So lust plays with what it loathes, in place of what is absent."],[10,12,"But more of this later. You, Diana, must still put up with something more for my sake, under my poor instructions."]],"986":[[0,5,"With admiration, my lord. I fixed my choice on her at first, before my heart dared send my tongue out as too bold a messenger. When her image was fixed in my eye, Contempt lent me his scornful lens,"],[6,11,"which twisted the shape of every other face, scorned a fair complexion or claimed it was painted, and stretched or shrank all proportions into a hideous object. That's how it came about that she whom all men praised, and whom I myself have loved since I lost her, was to my eye the speck of dust that hurt it."]],"987":[[0,4,"Well excused. That you did love her cancels some of the great debt. But love that comes too late, like a pardon brought slowly and with regret, turns into a bitter offence against the great one who sent it, crying, \"What's gone was good.\""],[5,11,"Our rash faults put a low price on the serious things we have, not knowing their worth until we know their grave. Often our anger, unjust to ourselves, destroys our friends, and afterwards we weep over their dust. Our own love wakes and weeps to see what's been done, while shameful hate sleeps through the afternoon."],[12,15,"Let this be sweet Helen's funeral bell, and now forget her. Send your love token to fair Maudlin. The main agreements have been made, and we'll stay here to see our widower's second wedding day."]],"997":[[0,4,"You're mistaken, my lord: she never saw it. In Florence it was thrown to me from a window, wrapped in a paper with the name of the woman who threw it. She was noble, and thought I was free to marry."],[5,9,"But when I had explained my situation and told her fully that I couldn't respond honourably to the offer she had made, she gave up, sadly accepting it, and would never take the ring back."]],"998":[[0,4,"Plutus himself, who knows the secret of turning metal to gold, doesn't understand nature's mysteries better than I know this ring. It was mine, it was Helen's, whoever gave it to you."],[5,11,"So if you know yourself at all, confess that it was hers, and by what brutal force you got it from her. She called on the saints to witness that she would never take it off her finger unless she gave it to you yourself in bed (where you have never been), or sent it to us in some great disaster."]],"1007":[[0,4,"Gracious sovereign, I don't know whether I've done wrong or not. Here's a petition from a Florentine woman who has missed you at four or five stops on the way and so couldn't hand it to you herself."],[5,10,"I took it on, won over by the poor petitioner's grace and speech. I know she is here by now, waiting. Her business shows in her face as something urgent, and she told me in a sweet short speech that it concerned your highness and herself."]],"1039":[[0,5,"I think she has. It's true that I liked her and made advances to her in the reckless way of youth. She knew how to keep her distance, and fished for me, maddening my eagerness with her restraint, since every obstacle in the course of desire makes desire stronger."],[6,9,"In the end her endless cunning, with her ordinary charms, brought me down to her price. She got the ring, and I got what any lower man could have bought at the market price."]],"1101":[[0,5,"Let us hear this story point by point, so that the plain truth can flow with pleasure. [To Diana] If you are still a fresh, unpicked flower, choose your husband, and I'll pay your dowry, because I can guess that by your honest help you kept a wife a wife and yourself a virgin."],[6,9,"Of that, and of everything that happened, great and small, we'll hear in full when there's more time. All still seems well, and if it ends so fittingly, the bitter past makes the sweet more welcome."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"love":["Love and desire","Helena's hopeless love, Bertram's lust for Diana and whether desire can become love."],"worth":["Birth and worth","A poor doctor's daughter and a count: is honour inherited or earned?"],"honour":["Honour and war","The Florentine war, Bertram's soldiering and the lost drum: what honour really is."],"words":["Words and deception","Parolles the man of words, lying letters, oaths, riddles and the bed-trick."],"women":["Women who act","Helena, the Countess and Diana take charge where men fail or run away."],"age":["Age and youth","A dying King, a widowed Countess and old Lafew judge a careless younger generation."],"cure":["Sickness and cure","The King's fistula, Helena's medicine, and the moral sickness that also needs healing."],"ending":["Endings and forgiveness","'All's well that ends well': does the ending really make everything well?"]},"quotes":[{"id":"7","who":"COUNTESS","text":"This young gentlewoman had a father","themes":["cure"],"note":"Helena is introduced through her dead father, the great doctor."},{"id":"15","who":"COUNTESS","text":"she derives her honesty, and achieves her goodness.","themes":["worth"],"note":"The Countess's first praise of Helena: virtue earned, not just inherited."},{"id":"19","who":"LAFEW","text":"Moderate lamentation is the right of the dead; excessive grief the","themes":["age"],"note":"Lafew's wisdom about grief, from the older generation."},{"id":"23","who":"COUNTESS","text":"Love all, trust a few,","themes":["age"],"note":"The Countess's advice to Bertram, which he will ignore."},{"id":"23","who":"COUNTESS","text":"Thy blood and virtue","themes":["worth","honour"],"note":"The Countess hopes Bertram's goodness will match his noble birth."},{"id":"30","who":"HELENA","text":"That I should love a bright particular star,","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena sees Bertram as a star, far above her in rank."},{"id":"30","who":"HELENA","text":"The hind that would be mated by the lion","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena's image of hopeless love across the gap in rank."},{"id":"30","who":"HELENA","text":"my idolatrous fancy","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena admits her love is a kind of worship."},{"id":"32","who":"HELENA","text":"I know him a notorious liar,","themes":["words"],"note":"Helena sees through Parolles at once."},{"id":"32","who":"HELENA","text":"Cold wisdom waiting on superfluous folly.","themes":["words"],"note":"How the world rewards show over substance."},{"id":"43","who":"PAROLLES","text":"It is not politic in the commonwealth of nature to preserve","themes":["words"],"note":"Parolles's mock-argument against virginity."},{"id":"70","who":"HELENA","text":"Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie,","themes":["women","cure"],"note":"Helena's motto: act, don't wait for the stars."},{"id":"70","who":"HELENA","text":"The mightiest space in fortune nature brings","themes":["worth"],"note":"Nature can join people separated by rank."},{"id":"85","who":"KING","text":"Making them proud of his humility,","themes":["worth"],"note":"The King's ideal noble: courteous to those below him."},{"id":"87","who":"KING","text":"Let me not live,","themes":["age","honour"],"note":"The old King quotes Bertram's father on not outliving his usefulness."},{"id":"87","who":"KING","text":"Since I nor wax nor honey can bring home,","themes":["age"],"note":"The sick King feels like a useless drone in the hive."},{"id":"130","who":"STEWARD","text":"Fortune, she said, was no goddess,","themes":["worth"],"note":"Helena blames Fortune for the gap between her and Bertram."},{"id":"134","who":"COUNTESS","text":"this thorn","themes":["love"],"note":"The Countess remembers young love as a thorn on the rose."},{"id":"141","who":"HELENA","text":"I am from humble, he from honoured name;","themes":["worth"],"note":"Helena states the class gap plainly."},{"id":"143","who":"HELENA","text":"So I were not his sister.","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena's dread of being Bertram's sister reveals her love."},{"id":"151","who":"HELENA","text":"I love your son.","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena's confession to the Countess."},{"id":"151","who":"HELENA","text":"Thus, Indian-like,","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena compares herself to a sun-worshipper who adores what cannot know her."},{"id":"158","who":"COUNTESS","text":"How shall they credit","themes":["cure"],"note":"The Countess doubts the doctors will trust a young woman."},{"id":"168","who":"KING","text":"Not to woo honour, but to wed it,","themes":["honour"],"note":"The King urges the young lords to win real honour in war."},{"id":"180","who":"BERTRAM","text":"By heaven, I'll steal away.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Bertram longs to escape the court for war."},{"id":"204","who":"LAFEW","text":"I have seen a medicine","themes":["cure"],"note":"Lafew introduces Helena's cure as almost magical."},{"id":"223","who":"HELENA","text":"He that of greatest works is finisher","themes":["cure","women"],"note":"Helena argues God works through the weakest."},{"id":"223","who":"HELENA","text":"Oft expectation fails, and most oft there","themes":["ending"],"note":"Helena on how hope fails where it promises most."},{"id":"225","who":"HELENA","text":"Of heaven, not me, make an experiment.","themes":["cure"],"note":"Helena presents her cure as heaven's work."},{"id":"229","who":"HELENA","text":"Tax of impudence,","themes":["women"],"note":"Helena risks her good name as well as her life."},{"id":"235","who":"HELENA","text":"What husband in thy power I will command:","themes":["women","cure"],"note":"Helena's daring reward: she will choose a husband."},{"id":"272","who":"LAFEW","text":"They say miracles are past;","themes":["cure"],"note":"Lafew presents the cure as a modern miracle."},{"id":"327","who":"HELENA","text":"I dare not say I take you, but I give","themes":["love","women"],"note":"Helena chooses Bertram by giving herself."},{"id":"333","who":"BERTRAM","text":"A poor physician's daughter my wife!","themes":["worth","love"],"note":"Bertram's snobbery in one line."},{"id":"334","who":"KING","text":"'Tis only title thou disdain'st in her,","themes":["worth"],"note":"The King insists rank is all Bertram objects to."},{"id":"334","who":"KING","text":"From lowest place when virtuous things proceed,","themes":["worth","honour"],"note":"The heart of the King's argument: deeds honour the place."},{"id":"334","who":"KING","text":"Honours thrive","themes":["worth","honour"],"note":"Honour should come from our own acts, not our ancestors'."},{"id":"335","who":"BERTRAM","text":"I cannot love her, nor will strive to do 't.","themes":["love"],"note":"Bertram's flat refusal."},{"id":"338","who":"KING","text":"Proud scornful boy,","themes":["age","worth"],"note":"The King turns his power on Bertram."},{"id":"356","who":"LAFEW","text":"the scarfs","themes":["words"],"note":"Lafew sees through Parolles's showy clothes."},{"id":"387","who":"PAROLLES","text":"France is a stable; we that dwell in't, jades,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Parolles urges Bertram to run off to war."},{"id":"388","who":"BERTRAM","text":"War is no strife","themes":["honour"],"note":"Bertram prefers war to marriage."},{"id":"391","who":"PAROLLES","text":"A young man married is a man that's marr'd.","themes":["love"],"note":"Parolles's cynical jingle on marriage."},{"id":"445","who":"LAFEW","text":"the soul of this man is his clothes;","themes":["words"],"note":"Lafew's verdict on Parolles."},{"id":"450","who":"BERTRAM","text":"Here comes my clog.","themes":["love"],"note":"Bertram calls his wife a weight tied to his leg."},{"id":"462","who":"HELENA","text":"like a timorous thief, most fain would steal","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena asks for what is already hers by law."},{"id":"464","who":"HELENA","text":"Strangers and foes do sunder and not kiss.","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena's broken request for a farewell kiss."},{"id":"493","who":"COUNTESS","text":"I have wedded her, not bedded her,","themes":["words"],"note":"Bertram's cruel letter to his mother."},{"id":"509","who":"HELENA","text":"When thou canst get the ring upon my finger,","themes":["words","love"],"note":"Bertram's impossible conditions."},{"id":"512","who":"COUNTESS","text":"And thou art all my child.","themes":["women"],"note":"The Countess chooses Helena over her own son."},{"id":"522","who":"COUNTESS","text":"she deserves a lord","themes":["worth"],"note":"The Countess says Helena deserves better than Bertram."},{"id":"532","who":"HELENA","text":"O you leaden messengers,","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena pictures the bullets flying at Bertram."},{"id":"532","who":"HELENA","text":"For with the dark, poor thief, I'll steal away.","themes":["women","love"],"note":"Helena decides to leave Rossillon."},{"id":"539","who":"BERTRAM","text":"A lover of thy drum, hater of love.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Bertram dedicates himself to war."},{"id":"544","who":"STEWARD","text":"I, his despiteful Juno, sent him forth","themes":["women"],"note":"Helena's letter blames herself for Bertram's danger."},{"id":"547","who":"COUNTESS","text":"What angel shall","themes":["ending"],"note":"The Countess fears for her unworthy son."},{"id":"556","who":"MARIANA","text":"the honour of a maid is her","themes":["women"],"note":"Mariana's warning to Diana."},{"id":"592","who":"DIANA","text":"'Tis pity he is not honest.","themes":["love"],"note":"Diana judges Bertram's character."},{"id":"611","who":"FIRST LORD","text":"he's a most notable coward,","themes":["words"],"note":"The lords' verdict on Parolles."},{"id":"660","who":"HELENA","text":"Is wicked meaning in a lawful deed,","themes":["words","love"],"note":"Helena's defence of the bed-trick."},{"id":"672","who":"PAROLLES","text":"I find my tongue is too foolhardy,","themes":["words"],"note":"Parolles admits his tongue outruns his courage."},{"id":"724","who":"DIANA","text":"'Tis not the many oaths that makes the truth,","themes":["words"],"note":"Diana exposes Bertram's empty oaths."},{"id":"730","who":"DIANA","text":"Mine honour's such a ring;","themes":["women","words"],"note":"Diana turns Bertram's argument back on him."},{"id":"735","who":"DIANA","text":"Since Frenchmen are so braid,","themes":["women"],"note":"Diana's verdict on men's vows."},{"id":"746","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"Merely our own traitors.","themes":["honour","words"],"note":"The lords reflect on how men betray themselves."},{"id":"765","who":"FIRST LORD","text":"The web of our life is of a mingled yarn,","themes":["ending","honour"],"note":"Good and ill are woven together in every life."},{"id":"772","who":"BERTRAM","text":"buried a wife, mourn'd for her,","themes":["love"],"note":"Bertram lists his wife's death as one errand among many."},{"id":"839","who":"FIRST LORD","text":"I begin to love him for this.","themes":["words"],"note":"Parolles's shamelessness becomes almost admirable."},{"id":"870","who":"PAROLLES","text":"Simply the thing I am","themes":["words","honour"],"note":"Parolles accepts what he is and decides to survive."},{"id":"870","who":"PAROLLES","text":"There's place and means for every man alive.","themes":["ending"],"note":"Parolles's hopeful last word."},{"id":"876","who":"HELENA","text":"That can such sweet use make of what they hate,","themes":["love"],"note":"Helena on lust in the dark."},{"id":"878","who":"HELENA","text":"All's well that ends well; still the fine's the crown.","themes":["ending","women"],"note":"Helena speaks the title phrase."},{"id":"883","who":"COUNTESS","text":"it was the death of the most virtuous","themes":["women"],"note":"The Countess mourns Helena."},{"id":"938","who":"HELENA","text":"All's well that ends well yet,","themes":["ending"],"note":"Helena repeats the proverb, with a doubtful 'yet'."},{"id":"974","who":"LAFEW","text":"He lost a wife","themes":["ending"],"note":"Lafew tells the King Bertram wronged himself most."},{"id":"987","who":"KING","text":"love that comes too late,","themes":["ending","love"],"note":"The King on regret that comes too late."},{"id":"986","who":"BERTRAM","text":"Since I have lost, have lov'd,","themes":["love"],"note":"Bertram claims he loved Helena once she was gone."},{"id":"1029","who":"BERTRAM","text":"And was a common gamester to the camp.","themes":["worth"],"note":"Bertram slanders Diana to save himself."},{"id":"1031","who":"COUNTESS","text":"That ring's a thousand proofs.","themes":["words"],"note":"The Countess recognises the family ring."},{"id":"1092","who":"DIANA","text":"one that's dead is quick,","themes":["words"],"note":"Diana's riddle: dead yet alive, and pregnant."},{"id":"1095","who":"HELENA","text":"The name, and not the thing.","themes":["ending"],"note":"Helena calls herself only the shadow of a wife."},{"id":"1098","who":"BERTRAM","text":"I'll love her dearly, ever, ever dearly.","themes":["ending","love"],"note":"Bertram's promise, with a condition before it."},{"id":"1101","who":"KING","text":"All yet seems well, and if it end so meet,","themes":["ending"],"note":"The King's last words are full of 'seems' and 'if'."},{"id":"1104","who":"KING","text":"The king's a beggar, now the play is done;","themes":["ending","words"],"note":"The epilogue asks the audience to make all well."}]};
const EX={"intro":["France and Italy. Helena, the orphaned daughter of a famous doctor, lives in the household of the Countess of Rossillon and secretly loves the Countess's son Bertram, a young count far above her in rank.","The King of France is dying. Helena cures him with her father's medicine and, as her reward, chooses Bertram as her husband. He obeys the King, then runs away to the war in Florence, setting her two 'impossible' conditions."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Bertram leaves for court; Helena reveals her love","id":30},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Helena resolves to act: 'Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie'","id":70},{"s":"a1s2","t":"The King lets French lords fight in the Florentine war","id":78},{"s":"a1s3","t":"The Steward reports Helena's love to the Countess","id":130},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Helena confesses her love and her plan to cure the King","id":151},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Bertram is kept at court as 'too young'","id":178},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Helena stakes her life on curing the King","id":229},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The King promises her a husband of her choice","id":236},{"s":"a2s3","t":"The King is cured; Helena chooses Bertram","id":327},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Bertram refuses; the King lectures and threatens him","id":334},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Lafew sees through Parolles","id":356},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Bertram vows never to sleep with Helena","id":384},{"s":"a2s5","t":"Bertram refuses Helena a kiss and sends her home","id":465},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Bertram's letter: the ring and the child","id":509},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Helena decides to leave Rossillon","id":532},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Bertram is made general of the Florentine cavalry","id":536},{"s":"a3s4","t":"Helena's letter: she has become a pilgrim","id":544},{"s":"a3s5","t":"Helena arrives in Florence and learns Bertram is chasing Diana","id":584},{"s":"a3s6","t":"The lords plan to expose Parolles over the drum","id":615},{"s":"a3s7","t":"Helena and the Widow plan the bed-trick","id":656},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Parolles is ambushed and blindfolded","id":695},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Diana gets Bertram's ring and sets the meeting","id":731},{"s":"a4s3","t":"News of Helena's 'death'; peace is made","id":757},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Parolles betrays everyone and is unmasked","id":870},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Helena: 'All's well that ends well'","id":878},{"s":"a4s5","t":"Lafew proposes Bertram marry his daughter","id":909},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Helena misses the King at Marseilles","id":938},{"s":"a5s3","t":"The King recognises Helena's ring on Bertram's finger","id":993},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Diana accuses Bertram","id":1023},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Helena appears, pregnant, with the ring","id":1097},{"s":"a5s4","t":"The King asks the audience for applause","id":1104}],"irony":{"23":"The Countess tells Bertram to 'trust a few'. He will trust exactly the wrong one.","187":"Parolles boasts of his sword's scars. He will later beg for his life without drawing it.","321":"Lafew thinks the lords are refusing Helena; really she is passing them over for Bertram, who is the one who will refuse.","339":"Bertram says Helena is now 'as 'twere born' noble. He does not mean a word of it.","455":"Bertram tells Helena he will see her in two days. He has already decided never to go home.","539":"Bertram swears to be a 'hater of love'. In the next act he will be chasing Diana.","575":"Helena says she knows Bertram only 'by the ear'. She is his wife.","581":"Helena praises 'his lady' modestly, while hiding that she is that lady.","626":"Parolles swears he will get the drum 'or hic jacet' (here lies). He has no intention of trying.","673":"Parolles thinks he is alone. Every word of his plan to fake wounds is overheard.","733":"Bertram thinks he has won 'a heaven on earth'. He will spend the night with his own wife.","772":"Bertram counts the seduction as his greatest business of the night; the audience knows he slept with Helena.","812":"Parolles slanders Captain Dumaine to his face without knowing it.","834":"Bertram now calls Parolles a cat. His own behaviour has been worse.","986":"Bertram says he loved Helena once he had lost her. He had slept with her without knowing it.","997":"Bertram invents a story about the ring. The audience knows how he really got it.","1092":"Diana's riddles are all true: she is a maid, and the 'dead' wife is alive and pregnant."},"devices":{"war":"Love as war","stars":"Stars and Fortune","cure":"Sickness and cure","ring":"Rings and tokens","clothes":"Clothes and show","trap":"Hunting and trapping","riddle":"Riddles and wordplay"},"dev":{"30":[["stars","Bertram is a star in a higher sphere; Helena can only see its light from below.","bright particular star"]],"40":[["war","Virginity is a city under siege; men attack and undermine it.","some warlike resistance"]],"41":[["war","Parolles turns the siege into a sexual joke about blowing up.","undermine you and blow"]],"61":[["stars","Helena teases Parolles about being born under Mars, the planet of war.","born under"]],"70":[["stars","Helena denies that the stars decide our fate.","the fated sky"]],"87":[["cure","The old King pictures himself as a burnt-out lamp.","my flame lacks oil"]],"151":[["stars","Helena worships Bertram like the sun that does not know her.","Indian-like"]],"168":[["war","Honour is a bride to be married, not merely courted.","Not to woo honour, but to wed it"]],"204":[["cure","Lafew describes the cure as a medicine that can raise the dead.","breathe life into a stone"]],"223":[["cure","Helena argues that heaven heals through weak instruments.","weakest minister"]],"227":[["stars","Helena measures time by the sun's horses and the evening star.","horses of the sun"]],"338":[["cure","The King's 'staggers' is a horse disease: Bertram will fall into giddy ruin.","the staggers"]],"356":[["clothes","Parolles's scarves and bannerets are like flags on a ship too small for them.","the scarfs"]],"358":[["clothes","Lafew sees through Parolles like a lattice window.","window of"]],"387":[["war","Marriage drains the 'manly marrow' that should ride Mars's horse.","Mars's fiery steed"]],"445":[["clothes","Parolles is a light nut with no kernel; his soul is his clothes.","the soul of this man is his clothes"]],"509":[["ring","The ring is Bertram's family honour, which he thinks can never come off.","the ring upon my finger"]],"532":[["war","Bullets are 'leaden messengers' riding on fire.","leaden messengers"]],"558":[["trap","Girls are caught with birdlime on the twigs that threaten them.","limed with the twigs"]],"640":[["trap","The lords will smoke out the fox and skin him.","the fox ere we case him"]],"641":[["trap","Setting twigs with birdlime to catch Parolles like a bird.","look my twigs"]],"707":[["trap","Parolles is a woodcock, a bird thought foolish and easy to catch.","caught the woodcock"]],"730":[["ring","Diana makes her chastity a ring, an heirloom to keep.","Mine honour's such a ring"]],"765":[["clothes","Life is a cloth woven of good and bad threads.","mingled yarn"]],"796":[["clothes","Parolles kept all his military theory in the knot of his scarf.","the knot of his scarf"]],"993":[["ring","The King's ring was a token of protection; now it seems to accuse.","This ring was mine"]],"1031":[["ring","Bertram's family ring, worn by six generations, proves Diana's story.","That ring's a thousand proofs"]],"1077":[["clothes","Diana is an 'easy glove' who slips on and off.","an easy glove"]],"1092":[["riddle","'Quick' means both alive and pregnant.","one that's dead is quick"]],"1095":[["riddle","Helena is the shadow of a wife: the name, not the thing.","The name, and not the thing"]]},"stage":{"rossillon":"The Countess's palace at Rossillon","palace":"A royal palace","house":"The Widow's house","camp":"The army camp","street":"Out of doors","epilogue":"The stage"},"stagerules":[["epilogue","epilogue"],["rossillon|countess","rossillon"],["widow","house"],["camp","camp"],["walls|street","street"],["palace|same","palace"]],"place":{"a1s1":"Rossillon","a1s2":"Paris","a1s3":"Rossillon","a2s1":"Paris","a2s2":"Rossillon","a2s3":"Paris","a2s4":"Paris","a2s5":"Paris","a3s1":"Florence","a3s2":"Rossillon","a3s3":"Florence","a3s4":"Rossillon","a3s5":"Florence","a3s6":"Florence","a3s7":"Florence","a4s1":"Florence","a4s2":"Florence","a4s3":"Florence","a4s4":"Florence","a4s5":"Rossillon","a5s1":"Marseilles","a5s2":"Rossillon","a5s3":"Rossillon"},"arc":{"Helena's hopes":[2,2,5,7,7,9,7,3,3,1,1,1,3,4,6,6,7,7,8,7,6,7,9,10],"Parolles's standing":[6,6,6,7,7,4,6,5,5,5,5,6,6,5,5,4,3,1,1,1,0,1,2,3]},"arcnote":"Helena's hopes rise with the cure and peak at the wedding, then crash when Bertram leaves and writes his letter. They climb again in Florence as the bed-trick works. Parolles stands high with Bertram until Lafew starts to expose him, and falls to nothing when he is unmasked in 4.3, before Lafew gives him a meal.","context":[["Date and printing","Probably written about 1604–1605, around the time of Measure for Measure, though a few scholars place it as early as 1598 and link it to 'Love's Labour's Won', a lost play named in a list of that year. It was first printed in the First Folio of 1623; there is no earlier quarto."],["Source","The story comes from Boccaccio's Decameron (Day 3, story 9), known in English through William Painter's Palace of Pleasure (1566–7). There Giletta cures the King, marries Beltramo and fulfils his conditions. Shakespeare added the Countess, Lafew, Parolles and the Clown."],["Problem play","Critics since F. S. Boas (1896) have grouped All's Well with Measure for Measure and Troilus and Cressida as 'problem plays': comedies with dark themes, morally troubling characters and endings that do not fully satisfy."],["Wardship","An under-age heir of a noble whose father died became the King's ward. The guardian controlled his upbringing and could arrange his marriage; refusing a match could be costly. Bertram is 'now in ward' to the King."],["Rank and marriage","Marrying beneath one's rank was seen as a disgrace to a noble family. The King's speech that honour comes from deeds, not birth, challenges this, but he still offers to give Helena a title and wealth."],["Women and medicine","Women did practise healing in Shakespeare's time, but learned physicians were men trained at universities, and unlicensed healers were called 'empirics'. Helena's cure succeeds where the college of physicians failed."],["The bed-trick","Substituting one woman for another in a dark bedroom is an old folk-tale device. Shakespeare uses it again in Measure for Measure. It depends on darkness and silence, and many modern readers find it troubling."],["Pilgrimage","Saint Jaques le Grand is the shrine of St James at Santiago de Compostela in Spain, a major pilgrimage site. Pilgrims wore a cloak and carried a staff, and stayed at inns for pilgrims, as Helena does in Florence."],["The Florentine war","Italian city-states such as Florence and Siena often fought each other and hired foreign soldiers. French nobles could gain honour fighting abroad while their King stayed neutral."],["Braggart soldiers","Parolles belongs to a long line of boastful cowardly soldiers going back to the Roman comedies of Plautus (the miles gloriosus). His exposure follows the pattern of the trickster tricked."],["Fools","Lavatch is an 'allowed fool', a household jester licensed to speak freely. His bitter, bawdy humour is darker than Feste's or Touchstone's."],["Honour and the drum","A regiment's drum carried its signals and its pride. Losing one in battle was a disgrace, which is why Parolles's boast to recover it matters."],["The title","'All's well that ends well' was already a proverb. The play keeps testing it: the King's last line says only that all 'seems' well."]],"critics":{"ending":[["Samuel Johnson (edition, 1765)","Paraphrased: he could not reconcile his heart to Bertram, a noble without generosity who marries as a coward, leaves as a libertine, lies when accused and is still rewarded with happiness."],["Harold Bloom, Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human (1998)","Paraphrased: he doubts Bertram's sincerity in the last scene and finds the happy ending hard to believe."]],"words":[["William Hazlitt, Characters of Shakespear's Plays (1817)","Paraphrased: he thought Parolles a very amusing creation, a coward and braggart whose exposure is one of the play's best comic scenes."]],"women":[["Samuel Taylor Coleridge (lectures, early 1800s)","Paraphrased: he called Helena one of Shakespeare's loveliest characters, though he found the plot around her unpleasant."],["George Bernard Shaw (1890s)","Paraphrased: he admired the Countess as the most beautiful old woman's part ever written and saw Helena as a forerunner of the independent modern woman."]],"worth":[["G. K. Hunter, Arden edition (1959)","Paraphrased: the play sets inherited honour against personal virtue, and the King's speech gives the play's idea of true nobility."]],"love":[["Susan Snyder (Oxford edition, 1993)","Paraphrased: Helena must hide or disguise her desire to make it acceptable, and the play shows how hard it is for a woman to pursue a man openly."]]},"insults":[{"id":32,"f":"a notorious liar","who":"Helena","n":"About Parolles, to herself, before he even speaks."},{"id":98,"f":"What does this knave here?","who":"Countess","n":"To the Clown: 'knave' is half affectionate, half scolding."},{"id":114,"f":"Such friends are thine enemies, knave.","who":"Countess","n":"Answering the Clown's joke about friends who sleep with his wife."},{"id":323,"f":"These boys are boys of ice","who":"Lafew","n":"Misreading the scene, he mocks the young lords for refusing Helena."},{"id":326,"f":"if thou","who":"Lafew","n":"Lafew calls Bertram an ass, using 'thou' as an old man to a boy."},{"id":333,"f":"A poor physician's daughter my wife!","who":"Bertram","n":"Not an insult to her face, but contempt for her birth."},{"id":338,"f":"Proud scornful boy,","who":"King","n":"The King's anger at Bertram's refusal."},{"id":358,"f":"Lord have mercy on thee for a hen!","who":"Lafew","n":"To Parolles: a hen is a coward."},{"id":368,"f":"scurvy, old,","who":"Parolles","n":"About Lafew, once he has safely left."},{"id":374,"f":"The devil it is that's thy master.","who":"Lafew","n":"Lafew returns to insult Parolles again."},{"id":376,"f":"you are a vagabond, and no true traveller.","who":"Lafew","n":"Lafew calls Parolles a tramp, not a gentleman traveller."},{"id":450,"f":"Here comes my clog.","who":"Bertram","n":"Bertram's word for his new wife."},{"id":526,"f":"A very tainted fellow, and full of wickedness.","who":"Countess","n":"The Countess blames Parolles for corrupting her son."},{"id":594,"f":"That jack-an-apes with scarfs.","who":"Diana","n":"Diana's view of Parolles as a dressed-up monkey."},{"id":611,"f":"an hourly promise-breaker","who":"First Lord","n":"The lords' list of Parolles's faults."},{"id":830,"f":"Damnable both sides rogue!","who":"Bertram","n":"Bertram, hearing Parolles's letter about him."},{"id":827,"f":"a foolish idle boy","who":"Parolles","n":"Parolles about Bertram, not knowing he is listening."},{"id":1029,"f":"a common gamester to the camp.","who":"Bertram","n":"Bertram's worst insult, to Diana, to save himself."}],"pronouns":[{"id":23,"f":"Be thou blest, Bertram","n":"The Countess uses loving 'thou' to her son."},{"id":56,"f":"If I can remember thee","n":"Parolles uses 'thee' to Helena: a man talking down to a poor gentlewoman."},{"id":138,"f":"I say I am your mother","n":"The Countess uses 'you' while she tests Helena, then 'thy' when she presses her ('does it curd thy blood')."},{"id":338,"f":"Check thy contempt","n":"The King uses 'thou' as a ruler rebuking a disobedient subject."},{"id":356,"f":"I did think thee","n":"Lafew calls Parolles 'thou', as a lord to an inferior; Parolles replies with 'you' until he loses his temper."},{"id":512,"f":"I pr'ythee, lady","n":"The Countess moves to tender 'thou' with Helena once Bertram has deserted her."},{"id":717,"f":"But, fair soul","n":"Bertram woos Diana with 'you', then slips into 'thee' ('I love thee') as he presses harder."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["The King refuses Helena's help","Helena goes to Paris","Bertram returns home"],"a1s2":["The King dies","A cure arrives from Rossillon","Bertram goes to war"],"a1s3":["The Countess forbids Helena to go","Helena reaches the King","Helena stays home"],"a2s1":["The cure fails","The King is cured","Helena runs away"],"a2s2":["Helena chooses Bertram","Helena chooses a different lord","Helena asks for gold instead"],"a2s3":["Bertram learns to love Helena","Bertram runs away","Helena refuses Bertram"],"a2s4":["Bertram says a loving goodbye","Bertram refuses her even a kiss","Bertram takes Helena with him"],"a2s5":["The Duke refuses French help","The Duke welcomes French volunteers","Florence makes peace"],"a3s1":["Helena gives up and grieves","Helena follows Bertram","Helena returns to Paris"],"a3s2":["Bertram wins honour in the war","Bertram is killed","Bertram returns at once"],"a3s3":["Helena reaches Saint Jaques","Helena has left a letter","Helena comes back"],"a3s4":["Helena turns up where Bertram is","Helena is lost at sea","Helena enters a convent"],"a3s5":["Parolles is exposed at once","A plot against Parolles is laid","Diana gives in to Bertram"],"a3s6":["Helena tells Bertram who she is","Helena plans to trick Bertram","Helena goes home"],"a3s7":["Parolles gets the drum back","Parolles is caught and betrays everyone","Parolles runs away"],"a4s1":["Diana refuses Bertram","Diana gets his ring","Bertram discovers the plot"],"a4s2":["Bertram learns the truth","Bertram leaves Florence thinking Helena dead","Bertram marries Diana"],"a4s3":["Helena goes to find the King","Helena gives up","Helena writes to Bertram"],"a4s4":["The Countess hears Helena is alive","Bertram is engaged to someone else","Bertram goes to Spain"],"a4s5":["Helena reaches the King quickly","Helena misses the King","The King comes to Florence"],"a5s1":["Parolles is executed","Parolles finds a patron","Parolles becomes a soldier again"],"a5s2":["Bertram marries Maudlin","The ring gives Bertram away","Helena arrives first"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Helena and Bertram","pts":["Helena is low-born but noble in mind; Bertram is high-born but mean in his behaviour.","Helena acts boldly to win love; Bertram runs away from it.","Helena sees through Parolles at once; Bertram trusts him until he hears his own name slandered."],"ids":[30,333]},{"t":"Helena and Diana","pts":["Both are virtuous young women of modest means pursued by men of rank.","Helena pursues the man she loves; Diana refuses the man who pursues her.","Diana's riddles in the last scene match Helena's cleverness, and both win husbands through the King."],"ids":[151,724]},{"t":"Lafew and Parolles","pts":["Lafew is old, plain-speaking and honourable; Parolles is young, showy and a liar.","Lafew sees through Parolles at once and keeps saying so.","In the end Lafew, his first enemy, feeds and keeps him."],"ids":[356,445]},{"t":"The Countess and the King","pts":["Both are wise elders who love Helena and are let down by Bertram.","The Countess backs Helena's love; the King makes the marriage happen.","The Countess forgives quickly; the King's anger nearly sends Bertram to prison."],"ids":[512,334]},{"t":"Parolles and Bertram","pts":["Both brag and both lie when cornered.","Parolles is exposed in 4.3; Bertram is exposed in 5.3, in a scene that mirrors it.","Parolles accepts what he is; Bertram promises to change."],"ids":[870,1039]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","All's Well That Ends Well"],["Written","Probably about 1604–1605; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Comedy, often called a 'problem play' or 'problem comedy'"],["Setting","Rossillon (south-west France), Paris, Florence and Marseilles"],["Language","Verse for most serious scenes, rhyme for Helena's moments of decision and the cure, prose for Parolles, the Clown and Lafew"],["Main plot","Helena cures the King, wins Bertram as her husband, is abandoned, and fulfils his conditions through the bed-trick"],["Second plot","Parolles is exposed as a coward by the French lords' fake ambush"],["Source","Boccaccio's Decameron, via William Painter's Palace of Pleasure"],["Protagonist","Helena"],["Turning point","Bertram's letter (3.2) sets the conditions; the bed-trick and Parolles's unmasking (4.2–4.3) turn the plot"],["Resolution","Helena appears in 5.3 with the ring and pregnant; Bertram promises to love her; Diana may choose a husband"],["Key themes","Love and desire, birth and worth, honour and war, words and deception, women who act, age and youth, endings"],["Famous lines","'Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie'; 'The web of our life is of a mingled yarn'; 'All's well that ends well'"]],"analysis":{"HELENA":["Helena is a heroine without rank or money. Her first soliloquy shows her loving Bertram 'as a bright particular star', but her second ('Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie') shows her refusing to wait on Fortune. She turns her father's knowledge into power, stakes her life and name on the cure, and claims a husband as her reward.","She is both humble and determined. She asks the Countess's pardon on her knees, offers herself to Bertram with 'I dare not say I take you', and leaves Rossillon so that he can come home. Yet she follows him to Florence, buys the Widow's help and arranges the bed-trick. Whether her pilgrimage was a plan from the start is left open.","Her love is clear-eyed: she knows Parolles is a liar and that Bertram can 'make sweet use' of a woman he hates. In the last scene she wins on her own terms, holding his ring and carrying his child. Her words 'the name, and not the thing' admit that the marriage is still not complete."],"BERTRAM":["Bertram is young, handsome and brave, but proud of his rank. His only reason for rejecting Helena is that she is 'a poor physician's daughter'. He obeys the King only under threat, then runs away, calling his wife his 'clog'.","He trusts Parolles against the warnings of Lafew, his mother and the lords, and is shocked only when Parolles insults him. In Florence he wins real honour as a soldier but uses his oaths to try to seduce Diana, and gives away his ancestral ring for one night.","In the last scene he lies about the ring, slanders Diana as a camp whore, and changes his story when cornered. His final promise to love Helena 'dearly, ever, ever dearly' comes after an 'if'. Some productions show real change; others leave him unconvinced."],"PAROLLES":["Parolles (his name means 'words') is a braggart soldier who dresses in scarves and talks of wars he has seen. Helena, Lafew and the lords see through him at once; only Bertram is fooled.","He encourages Bertram's worst instincts: running away to war and pursuing Diana. His boast that he will recover the lost drum leads to the ambush, where, blindfolded, he betrays the army's numbers and slanders his friends to save his life.","His exposure is the comic centre of the play, but his response is surprising: 'Simply the thing I am shall make me live.' He accepts his shame and survives, and in the end Lafew feeds him. He mirrors Bertram, whose lies are exposed in the next act."],"COUNTESS":["The Countess is a wise and generous widow. She guesses Helena's secret and draws it out gently, playing with the word 'mother', and then supports her plan to go to Paris.","When Bertram deserts his wife she sides with Helena completely: 'He was my son, but I do wash his name out of my blood, and thou art all my child.' She blames Parolles for corrupting him.","She grieves for Helena as for a daughter, yet still hopes for her son. In the last scene she identifies the family ring that proves Diana's story. She represents the older generation's good judgement."],"KING":["The King begins the play dying and longing for the past, praising Bertram's father as an ideal noble. He has lost faith in his doctors and in hope itself.","Helena's cure restores his strength and his authority. He keeps his word, lectures Bertram that honour comes from deeds, not names, and forces the marriage with threats. His rule is generous but absolute.","In the last scene his anger over the ring drives the plot: he suspects murder, arrests Bertram, and threatens Diana with prison. His final lines, 'All yet seems well', and his epilogue as a beggar for applause, leave the ending uncertain."],"LAFEW":["Lafew is an old lord with a sharp tongue and a kind heart. He brings Helena to the King with jokes about grapes and Cressida's uncle, and mocks the young lords he thinks are refusing her.","He is the first to see through Parolles, insulting him as a hen, a lattice window and a nut with no kernel. He warns Bertram, who ignores him.","He mourns Helena, proposes his own daughter for Bertram, then withdraws when Bertram is accused. At the end he weeps at the reunion and takes the ruined Parolles home, showing mercy."],"DIANA":["Diana is the Widow's daughter in Florence, chaste and quick-witted. She refuses Bertram's gifts and letters and, with her mother, agrees to help Helena.","In 4.2 she out-argues Bertram: oaths sworn to do wrong are worthless, and her chastity is as precious as his ring. She gets the ring and sets the rules for the night.","In the last scene she accuses Bertram before the King, then speaks in riddles that are all literally true until Helena appears. The King offers her a husband of her choice, echoing Helena's reward."],"CLOWN":["Lavatch, the Countess's fool, is bawdy and cynical. He wants to marry Isbel for the flesh, then loses interest after visiting the court.","His jokes about cuckolds and 'one good woman in ten' bring a bitter note to the comedy. His 'O Lord, sir!' routine mocks empty court manners.","He mocks the fallen Parolles as a smelly fish from Fortune's pond, and talks of the narrow and broad gates to heaven and hell. He is the darkest of Shakespeare's fools."]},"motifs":[["Sickness and cure","The King's fistula is cured, but the play is full of other sickness: Bertram's 'sick desires', the Countess's grief and the 'disease' of pride. Helena is a healer of more than the body.",[0,26,31,28]],["Rings and tokens","Two rings drive the plot: Bertram's family ring, which he swears will never leave his finger, and the King's ring, a token of protection. Swapping them in the dark proves the truth in the light.",[48,62,78]],["Stars and Fortune","Helena sees Bertram as a star far above her and blames Fortune for the gap, but then insists 'our remedies oft in ourselves do lie'.",[5,11,16]],["Love as war","From the virginity debate to Bertram's siege of Diana, love is pictured as an attack on a city. Bertram himself prefers real war to marriage.",[10,41,53]],["Words and deeds","Parolles is all words. Oaths, letters and boasts are tested against actions: Bertram's vows to Diana, his letter's 'never', Parolles's drum.",[60,61,68]],["Endings","The title proverb is spoken twice by Helena and hedged by the King. The play asks whether a good ending can make up for what came before.",[71,73,82]]],"symbols2":[["Bertram's ring","An heirloom passed down through six generations, it stands for his family honour. He gives it away to seduce Diana, and it ends up proving his marriage."],["The King's ring","Given to Helena as a promise of help, it passes to Bertram in the dark and makes the King fear she has been murdered."],["The drum","A regiment's drum is its honour. Parolles's boast to recover it exposes him; Lafew later calls him 'Tom Drum'."],["Parolles's scarves","His showy clothes stand for empty show. Lafew says the soul of this man is his clothes."],["The pilgrim's dress","Helena's disguise as a pilgrim to Saint Jaques suggests penance and devotion, while hiding her journey to Florence."],["The velvet patch","Bertram comes home with velvet on his face. It might cover a war scar or something less honourable; the Clown leaves it open."]],"bigq":[["Is Helena admirable or manipulative?",["She cures the King and risks her life","She is loved by the Countess, the King and Lafew","She chooses a man who does not want her","She uses money and a bed-trick to trap him"],[11,29,59,71]],["Does Bertram deserve Helena?",["He is brave and wins honour in war","He rejects her only for her birth","He lies and slanders Diana","He promises love only when cornered"],[33,37,77,81]],["Is the ending happy?",["Helena wins her husband and is pregnant","Bertram promises to love her","The King's last words are 'seems' and 'if'","Bertram's change may not be real"],[82,81,80,65]],["Is honour inherited or earned?",["The King says deeds make honour","Bertram has rank but behaves badly","Helena has no rank but great virtue","The King still has to give Helena a title"],[35,36,1,34]],["Is Parolles a villain or a fool?",["He leads Bertram astray","He betrays the army to save his life","Everyone wise sees through him","He ends forgiven and fed"],[8,68,67,69]],["Is this a comedy?",["It ends in marriage and forgiveness","The old characters are close to death","Love is pursued through tricks and lust","Much of the humour is bitter"],[2,14,65,83]]],"symbols":{"Rings":"\\brings?\\b","Honour":"\\bhonou?rs?\\b|\\bhonourable","Virginity and maids":"\\bvirgin|\\bmaids?\\b|maidenhead","The drum":"\\bdrums?\\b|drumming","Death and dying":"\\bdeath\\b|\\bdead\\b|\\bdie\\b|\\bdies\\b","Physic and cure":"\\bcure|\\bphysic|\\bphysician|\\bmedicine|\\bdisease|\\bmalady"},"solo":{"30":1,"32":1,"70":1,"368":1,"532":1,"672":1,"674":1,"735":1,"870":1},"iago":[["a1s1","Resolves to act: 'Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie'."],["a1s3","Confesses her love and wins the Countess's support for going to Paris."],["a2s1","Persuades the King to try the cure and bargains for a husband."],["a2s3","Cures the King and chooses Bertram."],["a3s2","Reads Bertram's conditions and leaves Rossillon as a pilgrim."],["a3s5","Arrives in Florence and learns that Bertram is pursuing Diana."],["a3s7","Reveals herself to the Widow and pays for help with the bed-trick."],["a4s2","Diana wins Bertram's ring and arranges the night meeting."],["a4s3","Bertram hears she is dead (the report she has spread) and leaves for France."],["a4s4","Plans to reach the King and present the proof."],["a5s1","Sends her petition to the King after missing him at Marseilles."],["a5s3","Appears with the ring and his child: both conditions met."]],"rel":{"1":[["HELENA","BERTRAM","love"],["COUNTESS","HELENA","trust"],["COUNTESS","BERTRAM","love"],["HELENA","PAROLLES","hate"],["KING","BERTRAM","trust"],["LAFEW","COUNTESS","trust"]],"2":[["KING","HELENA","trust"],["HELENA","BERTRAM","love"],["BERTRAM","HELENA","hate"],["BERTRAM","PAROLLES","trust"],["LAFEW","PAROLLES","hate"],["PAROLLES","HELENA","deceive"],["KING","BERTRAM","hate"]],"3":[["COUNTESS","HELENA","love"],["COUNTESS","BERTRAM","hate"],["BERTRAM","DIANA","love"],["DIANA","BERTRAM","hate"],["WIDOW","HELENA","trust"],["FIRST LORD","PAROLLES","deceive"],["BERTRAM","PAROLLES","trust"]],"4":[["DIANA","BERTRAM","deceive"],["HELENA","BERTRAM","deceive"],["BERTRAM","DIANA","love"],["FIRST LORD","PAROLLES","deceive"],["PAROLLES","BERTRAM","deceive"],["BERTRAM","PAROLLES","hate"],["DIANA","HELENA","trust"]],"5":[["KING","BERTRAM","hate"],["BERTRAM","DIANA","hate"],["DIANA","BERTRAM","deceive"],["LAFEW","PAROLLES","trust"],["HELENA","BERTRAM","love"],["BERTRAM","HELENA","love"],["COUNTESS","HELENA","love"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Helena?",[11,20,29,59,70,71]],["How does Shakespeare present Bertram?",[33,37,44,53,77,81]],["How does Shakespeare explore the idea of honour?",[35,36,23,62,4,64]],["How is Parolles presented, and why is he important?",[8,43,60,67,68,69]],["How does Shakespeare present women in the play?",[11,49,56,61,62,63]],["Does the play's ending make 'all well'?",[71,75,80,81,82,83]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short, embedded quotations","Analysed key words and images, not just the meaning","Linked to context (wardship, rank and marriage, women healers, the bed-trick, problem plays)","Tracked change across the whole play: Rossillon, Paris, Florence, the return","Considered the audience and dramatic effect (irony, the bed-trick, the unmasking)","Mentioned an alternative reading or critic","Wrote a conclusion that answers the question","Checked spelling of names: Helena, Bertram, Parolles, Lafew, Rossillon, Diana"],"models":[[11,"Helena is presented as a heroine who refuses to accept the limits Fortune has set her.","In 'Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie' the rhyming couplets give her decision the force of a proverb, and 'ourselves' moves power from the heavens to the individual.","The audience, who have just seen her weep over a 'bright particular star', watch her turn grief into action.","A woman of no rank could not normally choose a noble husband, so her plan challenges the social order.","Some critics admire her determination; others find her pursuit of an unwilling man troubling."],[33,"Bertram is presented as a young nobleman whose pride in rank blinds him to worth.","The exclamation 'A poor physician's daughter my wife!' puts the stress on 'poor' and 'physician', reducing Helena to her father's trade.","The audience, who know Helena has just saved the King, see Bertram as ungrateful and snobbish.","Noble families feared marrying beneath them, and as the King's ward Bertram had little right to refuse.","Some productions make him sympathetic as a boy forced into marriage; Johnson found him impossible to like."],[35,"Shakespeare presents true honour as earned through deeds rather than inherited with a title.","The King's antithesis between 'lowest place' and 'virtuous things' argues that the doer's deed 'dignifies' the place, reversing the usual order.","The audience may agree with the King, but notice that he still has to promise Helena wealth and a title.","Elizabethan society linked honour to birth; humanist writers argued for virtue as true nobility.","Critics such as Hunter see this speech as the play's moral centre; others note how Bertram's 'honour' in war contrasts with his dishonour in love."],[68,"Parolles is presented as a braggart whose exposure leaves him strangely honest.","In 'Simply the thing I am shall make me live' the plain monosyllables contrast with his earlier fancy language: the man of words is stripped back to being.","The audience laugh at his unmasking, but his refusal to despair can win some sympathy.","He belongs to the tradition of the boastful soldier from Roman comedy, who is always exposed.","Some critics see him as Bertram's scapegoat, punished so that Bertram can be forgiven."],[61,"Women in the play are presented as morally clearer and cleverer than the men.","Diana's contrast between 'many oaths' and 'the plain single vow' exposes Bertram's empty promises with simple logic.","The audience enjoy seeing a young woman out-argue a count and turn his own words about his ring against him.","Women were expected to be silent and obedient; Diana and Helena use wit and planning instead.","Feminist critics praise their agency, but note that both women still depend on the King for their happy endings."],[82,"The ending is presented as a hope rather than a certainty.","The King's 'seems' and 'if' qualify the title phrase, and the rhyme 'meet/sweet' sounds neat but tentative.","The audience, who have seen Bertram lie throughout the scene, may doubt his sudden promise to love Helena.","The phrase was a proverb, and comedies were expected to end in marriage.","Bloom and Johnson doubt the ending; others argue the play asks us to accept forgiveness as Helena does."]],"lang":{"un":[[70,"The mightiest space in fortune nature brings To join like likes","Nature brings together people separated by the widest gap of fortune, so that they join as equals."],[151,"Then I confess, Here on my knee, before high heaven and you,","Then here on my knee, before high heaven and you, I confess."],[334,"'Tis only title thou disdain'st in her,","It's only her rank that you despise in her."],[532,"Whoever shoots at him, I set him there;","I put him in front of whoever shoots at him."]],"pun":[[61,"The wars hath so kept you under, that you must needs be born under","under","pun","'Born under' a planet means ruled by it; 'kept under' means held down, beaten."],[493,"I have wedded her, not bedded her,","bedded","pun","The rhyme makes the difference between wedding and sleeping together sharp and cruel."],[391,"A young man married is a man that's marr'd.","marr'd","pun","'Married' and 'marred' (ruined) sounded alike."],[1092,"one that's dead is quick,","quick","pun","'Quick' means alive, and also pregnant: the dead wife is both."]],"st":[[70,"Our remedies oft in ourselves do lie,",[["Our","The answer belongs to us."],["ourselves","Not the stars or Fortune."],["lie","Hidden, waiting to be used."]]],[333,"A poor physician's daughter my wife!",[["poor","Her lack of money."],["physician's","Her father's trade."],["wife","The word Bertram cannot accept."]]],[870,"Simply the thing I am",[["Simply","No more pretence."],["thing","Not a captain, not a gentleman."],["am","Being, not boasting."]]]]},"dir":[[327,"I dare not say I take you","How should Helena approach Bertram?",[["Shyly, eyes down","She knows she is reaching above her station."],["Confidently, holding his gaze","She has earned this and claims it."],["Quickly, before she loses courage","Her nerve is barely holding."]]],[333,"A poor physician's daughter my wife!","How should Bertram say this line?",[["Loudly, to the whole court","His contempt is public and humiliating."],["Quietly, to the King alone","He is embarrassed and pleading."],["With a disbelieving laugh","He cannot take the idea seriously."]]],[464,"Strangers and foes do sunder and not kiss.","How should Helena ask for the kiss?",[["Almost whispering","She can barely bring herself to ask."],["With a sad smile","She already knows he will refuse."],["Stepping close to him","She makes it hard for him to turn away."]]],[870,"Yet am I thankful.","How should Parolles speak his last soliloquy?",[["Shaken, close to tears","The humiliation has hurt him deeply."],["Shrugging, almost cheerful","He bounces back at once."],["Slowly, thinking it through","He is genuinely learning about himself."]]],[1095,"'Tis but the shadow of a wife you see,","How should Helena enter in 5.3?",[["Quietly, from the back","She lets the truth speak for itself."],["Centre stage, visibly pregnant","Her proof is unmistakable."],["Straight to Bertram","The reunion is personal before it is public."]]],[1098,"I'll love her dearly, ever, ever dearly.","How should Bertram make his promise?",[["Kneeling, with tears","He is truly changed."],["Stiffly, looking at the King","He says what he must."],["Hesitating on the second 'ever'","He is not sure he means it."]]]],"why":{"0":"Helena's only inheritance is her father's skill, which will cure the King.","1":"Sets up the play's debate: virtue that is earned, not just inherited.","2":"The older generation's wisdom about grief frames a play full of death.","3":"Bertram will ignore this advice and trust Parolles.","4":"Raises the question of whether Bertram's character will match his birth.","5":"Shows the huge gap in rank that Helena must cross.","6":"Hints that her love could destroy her, but also that she aims high.","7":"Helena's love is close to worship, which some critics find blind.","8":"Helena's judgement is sound from the start, unlike Bertram's.","9":"Sums up a world that rewards show, which the play will expose.","10":"Parolles's cynicism about sex shapes Bertram's attitude to women.","11":"Helena's turning point from passive grief to action.","12":"Helena believes nature can overcome differences of rank.","13":"The ideal of nobility that Bertram will fail to follow.","14":"The old fear being replaced by a shallow younger generation.","15":"Shows how close to death the King is before Helena arrives.","16":"Helena's secret complaint against Fortune and rank.","17":"The Countess remembers being young and in love, so she sympathises.","18":"States the obstacle the whole plot must overcome.","19":"Her wordplay with the Countess reveals her love.","20":"The confession that wins the Countess's support.","21":"Her love is devoted but one-sided, like worshipping the sun.","22":"Shows the prejudice Helena faces as a young woman healer.","23":"Links war and marriage: Bertram will wed honour and flee his wife.","24":"Bertram's first act of disobedience, prompted by Parolles.","25":"Prepares the King, and the audience, for something miraculous.","26":"Helena gives her bold act a religious justification.","27":"Her argument against despair also fits the play's uncertain ending.","28":"She presents herself as heaven's instrument, not a proud woman.","29":"Shows how much a woman risked by acting publicly.","30":"A woman choosing a husband reverses the usual roles.","31":"The cure is treated as a wonder, raising Helena's status.","32":"Helena's humility at the moment of her greatest power.","33":"Bertram's snobbery is the cause of everything that follows.","34":"The King names the real obstacle: rank, which he can supply.","35":"The play's clearest statement that deeds make honour.","36":"Honour must be earned by each person, not inherited.","37":"Love cannot be commanded, which the King's power cannot fix.","38":"The forced marriage begins in anger and threat.","39":"Lafew sees that Parolles's clothes hide nothing.","40":"Parolles feeds Bertram's wish to escape.","41":"Bertram prefers danger in war to life with Helena.","42":"A cynical view of marriage that the play tests.","43":"The clearest judgement of Parolles, ignored by Bertram.","44":"Shows Bertram's contempt for Helena on their wedding day.","45":"Helena must beg for what is hers, foreshadowing the bed-trick.","46":"The refused kiss is the emotional low point of their marriage.","47":"Bertram's cruelty in a neat jingle.","48":"Sets the conditions that the rest of the plot fulfils.","49":"The Countess chooses virtue over blood.","50":"Even Bertram's mother says Helena is too good for him.","51":"Helena's love is selfless here: she fears for his life.","52":"Her departure sets up the journey to Florence.","53":"Bertram's choice of war over love, soon contradicted.","54":"Helena takes all the blame, which wins sympathy for her.","55":"The Countess hopes Helena's prayers can save Bertram.","56":"Introduces Diana's chastity, which will protect her.","57":"Diana sees Bertram's charm and his faults together.","58":"The lords begin to free Bertram from Parolles.","59":"Helena defends the morality of the bed-trick.","60":"Parolles's first honest words, overheard.","61":"Diana exposes the emptiness of Bertram's vows.","62":"Diana turns Bertram's argument against him, winning the ring.","63":"Diana's cynicism about men, learned from her mother.","64":"The lords see Bertram's lust as self-betrayal.","65":"Sums up the play's mixed tone of good and bad.","66":"Shows how little Helena's 'death' means to Bertram.","67":"Parolles's exposure becomes comedy rather than tragedy.","68":"Parolles's surprising self-acceptance after his fall.","69":"Even the disgraced can survive, a note of mercy.","70":"Helena's bitterness about male lust.","71":"The title phrase, used to justify the means by the end.","72":"Helena is mourned as the best of women.","73":"The 'yet' shows Helena's hope wavering.","74":"Lafew frames Bertram's loss as his own fault.","75":"The King's warning about regret fits Bertram exactly.","76":"Bertram's late love may be real or a convenient excuse.","77":"Bertram at his worst, slandering an innocent woman.","78":"The ring finally exposes Bertram's lies.","79":"The riddle prepares Helena's return from 'death'.","80":"Helena admits the marriage is not yet real.","81":"Bertram's promise, open to belief or doubt.","82":"The ending is hopeful but hedged.","83":"Hands the verdict on the ending to the audience."},"ev":{"ctx":[["878","938"],["15","70"],["878","938","1104"],["23","333","335"],["141","334","335"],["70","223","235"],["660","730","1095"],["544","562","601"],["74","388"],["870","445","686"],["901","130"],["623","686","539"],["878","938","1104"]],"cr":{"ending":[["335","32","1098"],["1098","1101","1104"]],"words":[["870","611","445"]],"women":[["70","141","327"],["134","512","522"]],"worth":[["334","23","15"]],"love":[["141","143","462"]]}}};
